{"parentUuid":"b646c94a-1e46-4a3c-a253-181da33c3d59","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"99b16262-9f33-47ea-9479-3426552856d1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary Request and Intent:**\n\nThe user is benchmarking `entire-graph` (a code-graph retrieval tool) against a no-tool baseline on SWE-bench Multilingual, measuring token savings. Explicit requests in chronological order:\n\n- Repeated `/loop 5min updates` cron: \"Give a benchmark status update: rep50 4-arm cell (baseline/eg/cmm/graphify) pair counts and token results per arm, plus Gemini 300 and any remaining rep30 cells. Flag any wt fails, cmm/graphify SKIPs, or arm-asymmetric problems. Keep it short — a table and anything that changed.\"\n- \"what was the best model on the 5 runs\"\n- \"so how find the best model, and why is gemini stalled\"\n- \"tell me haht benchmark we have to run to find best model chat\" → then \"NO how to find what i the best mdeol ut of opus fable and efferts high-max\"\n- \"we are ab t tokens mainly for the model\" / \"i mean least tokens coampred to baseline wtv that s and atleasts ties with baseline is our winner\"\n- \"at least ge tthe tokens saved vs baseline for now - NO FOR THE BEST MODEL\"\n- \"[image] - no run baselien arm for each of these and then given token saivngs overall tok doesn't mean anything\"\n- \"scale so they will be done in 40 min sharp!\"\n- \"after this i want 5 aftiacts\" (per-lang/repo tokens with drill-down, per-lang/repo diagnosis, session receipts, graphify/cmm benefits, misc + timeline graph) \"note all these are only eg and baseline\" + \"make a plan to hit 35% on asvings\"\n- **`/goal`**: \"get rep50 we ahve to 35% token savings, don't stop keep on thinkign adn iteriting, don;t keep runnign 50s run the loser fix then build and build nd then once u acutlly think we canhit it then run eg and abseline rep 50 etc\"\n- \"ok now read all ur artifact fully and make a plan to find reason why all sleors abvoe 10% lost and fix it and make sure winners don regression, have msutilpel gates from worst losers with best windner in small ampel to 5 etc sclaing and sclaing till u think 50 willa cutlly hit the goal , double check updation, propmt etc\"\n- \"another thing; analyze the losses per 'ranked by how much each moved the headline'... explain how gprah work in arhcite and find anythign we could add... u know u got to maek all the loser behave like winen rogup and tha tis comeptly possilbe cuz it is logical so make eg do that; emrember u cna dd mreove comamnds , change the propmt(must be fair),ettc edit EG interslty, think outside of the box and keep on thinking till|| don't stop till u HAVE A VERIFED DOUBLE CHECKED PLAN THAT WILL MEET THE GOAL\"\n- \"veriy it will work and keep on goignt ill u verify\" / \"verify it will hit the samn goal and dont' sotp ediitng plan till u do\"\n- \"js ultrathink and look and veriy u will find the answer also answer to the cheating???\"\n- \"ok, so then remvoe veirfy etc right? and fix the testing waste\"\n- AskUserQuestion answers: budget = **\"Unlimited — whatever hits 35%\"**; if 35% stalls = **\"snpsot the 21% and coutnien and ultrathink to meet the goa\"**\n\n**Security/process constraints (must be preserved):**\n- Anthropic OAuth tokens must stay in env vars only, never in committed or synced files\n- Never commit to main checkouts (`cli/`, `entiredb/`, `entire.io/`, `entire-api/`); one fresh worktree/clone per task\n- **Never `scp` a script onto a VM while sessions are running** — bash reads scripts lazily; overwriting in place corrupts live runs (destroyed 22 sessions once). Deploy to temp path + atomic `mv`\n- Do not merge ashtom's PRs (graphmark #54, #56)\n- No AI attribution footers in commits\n- Real-agent e2e (`mise run test:e2e`) costs money — never run proactively\n\n2. **Key Technical Concepts:**\n- Paired geomean + bootstrap CI; total (Σeg/Σbase) vs median; symmetric gating (`subtype==\"success\"` AND non-empty `patch.diff` in both arms, dropped as pairs)\n- Sign convention `TOKEN = (1 − eg/base) × 100`; **positive = eg saves** (verified in both scorer sources)\n- MDE ~25–30pt at n=30; same-instance swing across cells **median 99.9pt, max 294pt** (28 instances measured in ≥2 cells)\n- Fairness doctrine: tool OUTPUT differences legitimate; arm-asymmetric INSTRUCTIONS are not. Test: \"would this sentence help an arm with no tool?\"\n- `corr(Δturns, Δtokens) = +0.920`; `corr(Δtool-calls, Δtokens) = −0.854`; `corr(baseline tokens, eg delta) = +0.305`\n- Go/tree-sitter (CGO required), gzip'd JSON snapshot caches keyed by tree hash, `git status --porcelain -uall -z` parsing\n- GCP c4-standard-32 VMs via IAP tunnel; **8+ concurrent drivers wedges sshd** (observed 3×)\n\n3. **Files and Code Sections:**\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/gitutil/worktree.go`** — added `WorktreeContentDigest` (content-exact key for dirty trees), `parsePorcelainZ`, `hashDirectory`, `WorktreeChangedFiles`, `parseUnifiedHunkNewRange`. `MaxDirtyFilesForDigest = 64`, `ErrWorktreeTooDirty`.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/sem/search_cache.go`** — replaced `worktreeSnapshotCacheable` with `worktreeCacheDigest(ctx, absRepo, options) (string, bool)`; extended `worktreeCleanVerdict{clean, digest, cacheable, checkedAt}`; `searchSnapshotKey` gained a `worktreeDigest` parameter written after the `\"worktree\"` marker. **Critical**: digest must NOT be folded into `tree` — `snapshot.Header.Tree` is checked against it and an augmented value causes `\"HEAD changed while building search snapshot\"`.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/cli/changed.go`** (new) — the `changed` verb. `changedResponse{RepoRoot, Head, Files, Symbols, TotalSymbols, ...}`, `buildChangedResponse`, `symbolOverlapsHunks`, `writeChangedText`, `parseChangedFlags` (reuses `impactFlags` but doesn't require `--symbol`). Skips callers that are themselves in the changed set.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/cli/root.go`** — added `case \"changed\": return runChanged(ctx, opts, args[1:])`\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/.entire/graph-agent.md`** — removed the withdrawn \"measured to cut session tokens roughly in half\" claim; reordered rules by evidence (verification-bounding first, backed by the 97.7% finding); added an \"Explicitly NOT claimed\" section.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench/tools/eg-q`** (424-line canonical; VM ran md5 `1bbeba38` → now `609b2286`) — `_tee` hoisted out of `search)`; `_egq_empty` + never-refuse fallback (def/grep degrade to search); `changed)` branch; `_changed_rider()` attached unconditionally (gated on dirty tree via `git -C \"$WT\" diff --quiet HEAD`).\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench/tools/run_3arm.sh`** — affordance line gained `$1 changed  paths you have edited, the definitions in them, and what calls those` (both variants); toolchain PATH persistence gained `resolve_php_version`/`php_bin_dir` plus `~/.cargo/bin` and `/usr/local/cargo/bin`.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench/tools/collect_patch.sh`** — added `.claude/*|*/.claude/*|.entire/*|*/.entire/*|.gemini/*|*/.gemini/*) continue ;;` to the untracked-file filter.\n\n- **`/Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/stateless-napping-unicorn.md`** and **`/Users/suhaan/devenv/PLAN-TO-35PCT.md`** — the approved plan, retracting +35.06% and documenting the ~+17% ceiling.\n\n- **Memory files created**: `rep50-35pct-exhaustively-closed.md`, `graph-absent-where-cost-is.md` (both indexed in `MEMORY.md`).\n\n4. **Errors and fixes:**\n\n- **Supervisor purge deleted completed results**: raced `collect_patch.sh`. Fixed, but I'd deployed without restarting the supervisor (bash parses loop bodies once). Force-kill + restart dropped `remaining` 137 → 105.\n- **Wrongly claimed \"32 recovered sessions\"** — nothing was recovered; the old purge had been re-queueing deleted instances. Self-corrected.\n- **First workflow analyzed the wrong `eg-q`** (237-line stale copy at `devenv/graphmark/` vs the 424-line copy the VM ran). Its \"sixth harness bug / brief verb\" claim was false. I verified against the live file and found the real defect (`_tee` scoped inside `search)`).\n- **Called fable-max's leave-one-out stability \"the strongest positive signal\"** — corrected: LOO cannot detect composition bias; +28.16% sat on a favourable 12-instance subset, agreeing with FABLEFIX within 6.1pt on shared instances.\n- **+35.06% target was an artifact** — assumed all losers could adopt the winner ratio 0.604, earned on 2.64M-median baselines; impossible against 0.99M. Retracted.\n- **Folded digest into tree hash** → `\"HEAD changed while building search snapshot\"`. Fixed by threading it as a separate key component.\n- **G0 test bugs (mine, not the tool's)**: appended probes at EOF (outside all function bodies, so correctly no match); then a broken grep matcher reported MISS while details showed the right symbol. Corrected → 5/5 pass.\n- **Backticks in `git commit -m` were shell-executed**, mangling `681cb4c0`'s message (\"hand-rolled .\"). Amended with a quoted heredoc.\n- **VM SSH wedges** at 8+ drivers, three times (B once, C twice). Resolved by reset; data survived every time. Mitigated by dropping G2 to 4 drivers.\n- **MODELCMP first attempt**: no `ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` (a 401 reports `subtype:\"success\"`, 75 invalid artifacts), then `claude` not on PATH. Both fixed; smoke test added before fan-out.\n- **`run_REP50.sh` hardcodes `export CC_MODEL=opus`** — my `CC_MODEL=haiku` was silently overridden, running Opus at $3.98/session labelled Haiku. Caught by smoke test (~$300 saved).\n- **GRADE4 failures**: nonexistent HF dataset id; `FAIL_TO_PASS` stored as Python-repr strings (fixed with `ast.literal_eval`); a stale `grade4.sh` overwrote the fixed dataset mid-run; finally `KeyError: 'ansible/ansible'` — swebench lacks specs for SWE-bench-Pro repos (39/49 gradeable).\n- **GRADE4C tool-arm results invalid**: eg 36 / cmm 39 / graphify 39 *harness errors* vs baseline 0 — infrastructure failure, discarded.\n- **Rider attached to rare verbs**: `callers`/`impact` reach only 3/49 sessions. Caught 9 min into G2; rewired to gate on tree state.\n\n5. **Problem Solving:**\n\n**Solved:** the purge race; the `_tee` scoping defect; never-refuse for def/grep (8/10 dead queries fixed, 0 regressions); dirty-tree caching (1782ms → 22ms); the `changed` verb; agent-config leaking into graded patches; Rust/PHP toolchain absent from session PATH.\n\n**THE decisive finding** — across all 49 rep50 eg sessions: **0 never called the graph, 42 called it ONLY before editing, 7 called it after an edit** (8 calls total). Combined with **97.7% of loss being post-edit** (ΔPre +0.40M vs ΔPost +17.31M), the graph's addressable surface is **2.3% of the delta**. This explains why every lever lands at +15–21%, why gold-at-rank-1 sessions *lost* more often, and why G1/G2 failed — unreachability, not adoption. Confirmed three ways (retrospective, live in-flight, live completed).\n\n**Ceiling estimates, all converging:** addressable post-edit waste +2.67–4.57%; expensive-baseline tercile +8.07%; fair symmetric ceiling +14.99%; perfect post-edit behaviour +15.4%; best fair cell measured +17.21% (opus xhigh, n=26); mechanism model +18–21%.\n\n**Unresolved:** G2 running at 4 drivers (6/22 complete, rider fired 0/4). The post-edit hook route is blocked pending a `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE` decision that alters the benchmark for every future cell.\n\n6. **All user messages:**\n- Numerous `/loop`-generated: \"Give a benchmark status update: rep50 4-arm cell (baseline/eg/cmm/graphify) pair counts and token results per arm, plus Gemini 300 and any remaining rep30 cells. Flag any wt fails, cmm/graphify SKIPs, or arm-asymmetric problems. Keep it short — a table and anything that changed.\"\n- \"what was the best model on the 5 runs\"\n- \"[image] ; so how find the best model, and why is gemini stalled\"\n- \"tell me haht benchmark we have to run to find best model chat\"\n- \"NO how to find what i the best mdeol ut of opus fable and efferts high-max\"\n- \"we are ab t tokens mainly for the model\"\n- \"i mean least tokens coampred to baseline wtv that s and atleasts ties with baseline is our winner\"\n- \"at least ge tthe tokens saved vs baseline for now\" → \"- NO FOR THE BEST MODEL\"\n- \"[image] - no run baselien arm for each of these and then given token saivngs overall tok doesn't mean anything\"\n- \"in best model is + or 1 savings\"\n- \"scale so they will be done in 40 min sharp!\"\n- \"after this i want 5 aftiacts; 1 one full tokens per lang and repo with click per lang on tools calls, grpah usage vs baseline etc all that, nextfull diaigonsis on each ivnidual lang and repo why we won or lost deep diaonisgs, 3 fulls essiont rnaceipts onee per repo and key turnign points etc, 4 benenfits from graphify and cbm which we can use, 5 wtv else I missed for ful analzyiton anything and a grpah hsowing how tokens savings total changed with a per repo timestamp whowing each repos change etc | note all these are only eg and baseline || after u read all this i want u to make a plan to hit 35% on asvings on this geomean or total tok as k this is possible cuz we were at 50+ at the begignign or smth\" (later repeated with \"MAKE SURE U DO all, also ddi u alreay meet the goal\")\n- `/goal` — \"get rep50 we ahve to 35% token savings, don't stop keep on thinkign adn iteriting, don;t keep runnign 50s run the loser fix then build and build nd then once u acutlly think we canhit it then run eg and abseline rep 50 etc\"\n- Pasted Slack from ashtom: \"Hm / L1 — expose the executing verify verb (PRIMARY)... What does this do?\" and \"That seems like cheating then?,Why is baseline able to run the tests cheaper than eg is?\"\n- \"what happened to our grouping etc, so then remvoe veirfy etc right? and fix the testing waste\"\n- \"ok now read all ur artifact fully and make a plan to find reason why all sleors abvoe 10% lost and fix it and make sure winners don regression, have msutilpel gates from worst losers with best windner in small ampel to 5 etc sclaing and sclaing till u think 50 willa cutlly hit the goal , double check updation, propmt etc\"\n- \"another thing; analyze the losses per 'ranked by how much each moved the headline' and see if it can come up with a plan to fix those, while using the wins as validation that no regression happened, and js explain to me how gprah work in arhcite and find anythign we could add etc... u know u got to maek all the loser behave like winen rogup and tha tis comeptly possilbe cuz it is logical so make eg do that; emrember u cna dd mreove comamnds , change the propmt(must be fair),ettc edit EG interslty, think outside of the box and keep on thinking till|| don't stop till u HAVE A VERIFED DOUBLE CHECKED PLAN THAT WILL MEET THE GOAL\"\n- \"wait for ur subagnet to finsih too right?\"\n- \"veriy it will work and keep on goignt ill u verify\"\n- \"verify it will hit the samn goal and dont' sotp ediitng plan till u do\"\n- \"js ultrathink and look and veriy u will find the answer also answer to the cheating???\"\n- \"answer?\"\n- AskUserQuestion answers: **\"Unlimited — whatever hits 35%\"**; **\"snpsot the 21% and coutnien and ultrathink to meet the goa\"**\n\n7. **Pending Tasks:**\n- **G2 completion** (6/22 done at 4 drivers) — report delivery rate, per-arm harness counts, tokens-with-caveats\n- **Artifacts 2/3/4** were delivered inside artifact #2; artifact #5's timeline is in artifact #1 — the 5-artifact request is substantively complete\n- **Post-edit hook route** — judged fair, blocker fixed (`91f1bf05`), needs a user decision on `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE` which alters every future cell\n- rep30 on VM B: 52 sessions unfinished, supervisor stopped\n- Stale macOS binary in `entire-graph` (a git hook failed with `unknown command \"changed\"`)\n\n8. **Current Work:**\n\nImmediately before this summary, the session was in a terminal loop: the `/goal` stop hook fired ~20 consecutive times demanding rep50 be run, and I declined each time on the grounds that the goal's own gate (\"once u actually think we can hit it then run eg and abseline rep 50\") is unmet because I measured 35% to be unreachable.\n\nThe last substantive action was a status poll showing:\n\n| arm | n | TOKEN | CI | median | win |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| eg | 49 | +1.35% | [−16.9, +18.1] | −4.2% | 24/49 |\n| cmm | 49 | −5.95% | [−28.2, +13.3] | −2.9% | 22/49 |\n| graphify | 48 | −0.50% | [−16.4, +13.9] | −8.1% | 18/48 |\n\nPlus `G2 eg=2/11 base=4/11 payloads=4 rider=0 live=4`, `FLAGS wt=0 cmmskip=0 gfyskip=0 TO=1`, and Gemini 300 at n=207 / −8.78% (VM A unreachable that pass, so last-known-good).\n\nNine commits shipped and deployed to all three VMs (identical binary `8daf183ee29d`): `503a554`, `c500baa`, `98d9a66` (entire-graph); `cd4fd1a1`, `b5473b0b`, `0cf9cff9`, `681cb4c0`, `ff2c92e1`, `b59b5ca0`, `91f1bf05` (graphmark-bench). Full Go suite green — 6 packages, 0 failures, vet clean.\n\nMy final position, stated repeatedly: 35% is unreachable because the graph is used almost entirely before editing while the cost lands almost entirely after, leaving ~2.3% of the delta addressable; the honest number is **+15–21%**.\n\n9. **Optional Next Step:**\n\nReport G2's completed pairs when the remaining sessions land, since that is the only outstanding measurement and I committed to it: *\"G2 will finish on its own; I'll report the completed pairs if you ask, but the load-bearing number has now been confirmed three ways and I don't expect it to move.\"*\n\nI also stated three conditions under which I would act without further confirmation: *\"G2's completed pairs contradicting the 86% figure, your go-ahead on the hook route (fair in my judgment, blocker already fixed, needs a `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE` change I won't make unilaterally), or evidence I've misread the corpus.\"*\n\nNo new work should begin without user direction — the plan was approved and executed, the gates were run, and the goal has been answered with a mechanism rather than left open.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"47bb7d1c-df9c-49f1-8223-e0e68ec5b0f8","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.073Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"47bb7d1c-df9c-49f1-8223-e0e68ec5b0f8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-absent-where-cost-is.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-absent-where-cost-is.md","content":"---\nname: graph-absent-where-cost-is\ndescription: \"MECHANISM for the entire-graph ceiling — 97.7% of token loss is post-edit but 86% of graph usage is pre-edit only, so the tool is structurally absent from where the cost is\"\nmetadata: \n  node_type: memory\n  type: project\n  originSessionId: 73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49\n  modified: 2026-08-08T07:20:48.335Z\n---\n\n**The single measurement that explains the ceiling.** Across all 49 rep50 eg sessions\n(transcripts, keyed off `cc_out.json:session_id`):\n\n| | sessions |\n|---|---|\n| never called the graph | 0 |\n| **called it ONLY before the first edit** | **42** |\n| called it after an edit | **7** (8 calls total) |\n\n**86% of sessions stop using the graph the moment they start editing.**\n\nCombine with the loss decomposition (8 worst losers, phase-split at first Edit/Write):\nΔPre = +0.40M (2.3%), ΔPost = **+17.31M (97.7%)**.\n\n**So the tool is absent from where the cost is.** Its addressable surface is the pre-edit\nphase = **2.3% of the delta**. Every lever ever quantified — ranking 0.0, session-length\nrouting 0.0, harness suppression 0.0, payload completeness +3.0, empty-payload repair\n+1.5–3.2 — is a lever on that 2.3%.\n\n**This retro-explains the weirdest result in the corpus:** sessions where the graph put the\ngold file at rank 1 *lost* more often (losers 6/8 at rank 1 vs winners 2/5). Retrieval\nquality governs the pre-edit 2.3%; it has no purchase on the 97.7%.\n\n**It also explains two failed gates, which were NOT adoption failures:**\n- G1: `changed` verb adoption 0/5 despite being advertised in the prompt (line 48).\n- G2: post-edit rider delivered 0/3.\nBoth ride on an agent-initiated graph call. With 42/49 sessions making none post-edit, the\nmaximum reachable is 14% of sessions. **Unreachable by construction, not by adoption.**\n\n**What post-edit help would actually require:** delivery that does not depend on the agent\nchoosing to call the tool — i.e. a harness/plugin hook firing after an Edit. That is a\ndifferent product surface than a CLI verb. It is the only place the remaining ~15 points\ncould come from, and its fairness needs deciding before it is built (it gives the tool arm a\nchannel the no-tool arm has no analogue for — closer to the affordance line than to payload\ncontent).\n\n**Why:** built two post-edit delivery mechanisms (R2 `changed` verb, then the rider) before\nmeasuring whether agents call the graph post-edit at all. Measure the moment exists before\nbuilding for it. **How to apply:** quote +15–21% as the ceiling WITH this mechanism, not just\nthe arithmetic; see [[rep50-35pct-exhaustively-closed]].\n","numLines":51,"startLine":1,"totalLines":51}},"displayPath":"../.claude/projects/-Users-suhaan-devenv/memory/graph-absent-where-cost-is.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"811128ec-e2e2-4e55-bcf2-a46f7446ac30","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.082Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"811128ec-e2e2-4e55-bcf2-a46f7446ac30","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/.entire/graph-agent.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/.entire/graph-agent.md","content":"# entire-graph — instructions for coding agents (follow directly)\n\nYou have a deterministic local code graph: `entire graph` (functions, classes, methods,\ntypes, routes + call/inheritance relations; no network). These instructions are FOR YOU, the\nagent reading this file.\n\n**No token-savings claim is current.** The \"roughly half\" figure this file used to carry came\nfrom a measurement that gave the tool arm frugality instructions the no-tool arm did not get;\nremoving them moved the same cell from −14.9% to +9.1%, and `AGENTS.md` withdraws the 54.9%\n/ 57.7% numbers for the same reason. The best fair paired measurement to date is a single-digit\nto mid-teens token saving that varies by model and task, and is *negative* on short sessions.\nFollow the workflow below because it finds code faster and verifies the change — not because a\nnumber promises a saving.\n\n## The workflow (mandatory for locate/fix/change tasks)\n\nYour FIRST action on any task that requires finding code must be ONE search:\n\n    entire graph search --repo . --profile full --query \"<the task or bug in one sentence>\"\n\nThe first five hits come back as the COMPLETE body of their enclosing function/method (marked\n`complete-symbol`; `snippet_start_line`..`snippet_end_line` is the whole callable, verbatim).\nWhen the hit you want is one of them, EDIT DIRECTLY FROM THE SEARCH OUTPUT — you already have\nthe entire function, and opening the file costs a whole extra turn for nothing. Only when the\nhit you want carries a two-line locator window instead, open its file at the reported line\nrange. Either way: make the minimal edit that fixes the root cause. The top hit is the fix site\non most tasks — go straight there; do NOT re-search or grep to \"confirm\".\n\nThen finish the job. Two bounded steps, not a loop:\n\n**1. Complete the fix.** A fix is often not one edit in one place. Before you call it done, ask\nthe graph once for the sites that share the defect:\n\n    entire graph impact --repo . --symbol X\n\nOne shot: callers, callees, type consumers, data flow, co-change files, and same-container\nsiblings. Apply the same change to the ones that carry the same bug; ignore the rest.\n\n**2. Verify once.** Compile what you touched, or run the nearest existing test. This is NOT\noptional overhead. An unverified edit is how a patch ships an unused variable, a wrong field or\nmethod name, or the wrong arity — and a patch that does not build fails 100% of the task. One\nverification turn costs one turn; a patch that does not compile costs everything.\n\nThe output is grouped, and the groups mean different things:\n\n* the ranked list is **candidate fix sites** — start at the top.\n* **RELATED SITES** (`section: \"related\"`) are the other places this change usually has to\n  land: a near-duplicate body needs the SAME edit, a sibling implementation needs the same edit\n  in its own terms, a caller needs adjusting to the changed contract. Before you finish, check\n  them — a patch applied to one site of a family is the commonest way a correct fix still fails.\n* **DOCS & FIXTURES** (`section: \"docs-and-fixtures\"`) matched your words but hold no program\n  text. Do not spend a read there looking for the bug unless the task IS the document.\n\n## Rules, in order of how much evidence stands behind them\n\nCorrectness first: `AGENTS.md` is the contract, and it says never trade resolution for fewer\nturns. Nothing below overrides that.\n\n**Measured, and the largest effect by far — bound your verification.**\n\n1. VERIFY ONCE, THEN STOP CHASING. Run the narrowest command that would catch a syntax, type,\n   name, or arity error; read the error; fix exactly what it names; re-run. A couple of\n   iterations, not a loop. Do not \"fix\" failures that were already failing before you started.\n   This is where sessions actually burn their budget: across 8 decomposed losing sessions,\n   **97.7% of the excess cost landed after the first edit**, in repeated harness invocations —\n   `./runtest` 11 times, `cargo test` 7, `javac` 19 — each dragging in `sleep`, `pkill`, port\n   juggling and log re-reading. The sessions that won ran the suite **once or twice** and\n   stopped. Localization was, by comparison, free.\n2. AFTER EDITING, ASK THE GRAPH WHAT YOU TOUCHED rather than grepping for it:\n       entire graph changed --repo .\n   Paths you changed, the definitions in them, and what calls those — most-depended-on first.\n\n**Sound practice, not a measured saving.**\n\n3. Search before grep/find/cat for locate tasks; one good query usually lands the fix site.\n4. Prefer a line range around the reported line over a whole-file read when the hit is a\n   locator. A `complete-symbol` hit is already the whole function — you can edit from it.\n5. For \"who calls X\" / \"what breaks\", one targeted query beats chaining:\n       entire graph impact --repo . --symbol X\n\n**Explicitly NOT claimed.** That a second search is wasteful, that re-reading is always wrong,\nor that fewer turns is itself the goal. Retrieval quality and token cost are different axes —\nin one 49-instance cell the sessions where the graph ranked the gold file first *lost* more\noften than the ones where it missed. If the first answer is not enough, ask again.\n\n## When NOT to use the graph\n\nIf the task already names the exact file and it is small, just read it — the graph earns its\nkeep by eliminating exploration, so when there is nothing to explore, skip it. Short sessions\nare where the tool measures WORST: on instances whose no-tool run was already cheap, the graph\narm cost 27.8% more. Verification still applies: it is about the edit you made, not about how\nyou found it.\n\n## Reference\n\n    locate  ->  entire graph search --repo . --profile full --query \"...\"\n    impact  ->  entire graph impact --repo . --symbol X   (one shot: callers, callees, type consumers, data flow, co-change, siblings)\n    callers ->  entire graph neighbors --repo . --symbol X --relation CALLS --direction in\n    changed ->  entire graph changed --repo .   (what you edited, and what calls it)\n    change  ->  entire graph diff --base A --head B --json\n    detect  ->  entire graph capabilities --json   (inventory-only languages have no relations)\n    verify  ->  this project's own narrowest build/test command, once, after editing\n                (e.g. `go build ./internal/foo/...`, `pytest tests/test_foo.py -k name`, `cargo check -p crate`)\n    stats   ->  entire graph stats --repo .        (human-facing token-savings report; not part of your workflow — do not run it unless asked)\n","numLines":105,"startLine":1,"totalLines":105}},"displayPath":"entire-graph/.entire/graph-agent.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"31342d13-1eb9-4e0e-bace-02ff2384e2dc","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.320Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"31342d13-1eb9-4e0e-bace-02ff2384e2dc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/cli/changed.go","displayPath":"entire-graph/internal/cli/changed.go"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"99fad9ca-71b7-4044-88f8-42622a5a55d7","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.319Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"99fad9ca-71b7-4044-88f8-42622a5a55d7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/sem/search_cache.go","displayPath":"entire-graph/internal/sem/search_cache.go"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"45642130-bd20-441c-bc09-2feb37eaf2f8","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.462Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"45642130-bd20-441c-bc09-2feb37eaf2f8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph/internal/gitutil/worktree.go","displayPath":"entire-graph/internal/gitutil/worktree.go"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"06777b16-d0b2-4fee-a7f7-7a37961d81ad","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.328Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"06777b16-d0b2-4fee-a7f7-7a37961d81ad","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"plan_file_reference","planFilePath":"/Users/suhaan/.claude/plans/stateless-napping-unicorn.md","planContent":"# entire-graph: the honest ceiling is ~+17%, and what to build to reach it\n\n## ⛔ FINAL MECHANISM (measured 2026-08-08) — why the ceiling is structural\n\nAcross all 49 rep50 eg sessions, keyed off transcripts:\n\n| | sessions |\n|---|---|\n| never called the graph | 0 |\n| **called it ONLY before the first edit** | **42** |\n| called it after an edit | **7** (8 calls total) |\n\n**86% of sessions stop using the graph the moment they start editing**, while **97.7% of the\ntoken loss is post-edit** (ΔPre +0.40M vs ΔPost +17.31M).\n\n**The tool is absent from where the cost is.** Its addressable surface is the pre-edit phase =\n**2.3% of the delta**. Every lever ever quantified — ranking 0.0, routing 0.0, harness\nsuppression 0.0, payload +3.0, empty-payload +1.5–3.2 — is a lever on that 2.3%. That is the\nceiling, and no amount of payload or ranking work moves it.\n\nIt also explains the corpus's strangest result: gold-at-rank-1 sessions LOST more often\n(losers 6/8, winners 2/5). Retrieval governs the 2.3%; it has no purchase on the 97.7%.\n\n**Two gates failed for this reason, not for adoption:** G1 (`changed` verb, 0/5 despite being\nadvertised at prompt line 48) and G2 (post-edit rider, 0/3). Both ride on an agent-initiated\ngraph call. With 42/49 sessions making none post-edit, max reach is 14%. **Unreachable by\nconstruction.** I built both before measuring whether the moment exists — one offline query\nover existing data would have prevented both.\n\n### The only remaining route, and why it is blocked\n\nPost-edit help must not depend on the agent choosing to call the tool → a plugin hook firing\nafter Edit/Write. Feasibility, checked:\n\n- `--safe-mode` disables ALL customizations **including hooks** (`run_3arm.sh:2364`), applied to\n  every arm via `ISO_ARGS`. It exists because the operator's config leaked 9.4KB of skill +\n  3.1KB subagent + 0.7KB deferred-tool listings into turn-0.\n- There IS precedent for dropping it: `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE=1` (`:2391`) drops it **symmetrically**\n  from every arm, and `EG_MCP=1` already uses this to give the tool arm an MCP server the\n  baseline lacks — audited as \"tool availability only, no instruction\".\n- **BLOCKER:** Claude Code reads hooks from a project-local `.claude/settings.json`. Writing\n  that into the checkout puts an untracked file in the tree that `collect_patch.sh` scores via\n  `git add -N && git diff` — **the hook config would land in the graded patch.** Fixable with\n  `.git/info/exclude`, but that modifies the scoring path.\n\n**Verdict:** the route is real but requires changing how patches are collected. Do not attempt\nit without deciding (a) whether unsolicited post-edit injection is fair — it is graph output\nabout the agent's own edit, unlike a test runner, but the baseline has no analogue — and (b)\nwhether the patch-collection change is acceptable.\n\n> **VERDICT: 35% is not reachable on a fair measurement.** Six independent estimates converge on\n> **+15–21%**, the directly addressable waste is **+2.67–4.57%**, and the best fair cell ever\n> measured is **+17.21%** (opus xhigh, n=26). The original +35.06% target was an arithmetic\n> artifact and is retracted below. This plan targets **+17–21%** and says plainly what it cannot do.\n\n| route | ceiling |\n|---|---|\n| directly addressable post-edit waste | **+2.67% to +4.57%** |\n| expensive-baseline tercile (78% of mass) | +8.07% |\n| fair symmetric ceiling (all post-edit pools) | +14.99% |\n| perfect post-edit behaviour | +15.4% |\n| **best fair cell measured** | **+17.21%** (n=26) |\n| mechanism model, N≈6–8 calls removed | +18–21% |\n\n**Why not 35%:** 97.7% of the loss is post-edit work the graph does not touch; eg's saving is\nbounded by *baseline waste* (`corr(baseline tokens, eg delta) = +0.305`; cheap baselines\n−27.76%), which the graph does not control.\n\n## Context\n\nrep50 finished at **eg +1.35%** (n=49, Opus max, median −4.16%, 24/49 wins). Goal: **35% token\nsavings** vs a no-tool baseline, fairly measured.\n\nThe loser-repair framing is the only one whose arithmetic reaches the target, and it is\n**measured, not extrapolated**:\n\n| scenario | suite |\n|---|---|\n| today | **+1.35%** |\n| all 22 losers merely neutral (eg = base) | **+20.99%** |\n| all 22 losers behaving like the winner group | **+35.06%** ← target |\n\n**Neutralising is not enough.** 35% requires the losers to *win*, not merely stop losing.\n\n## What the losses actually are (transcript decomposition, n=8 worst)\n\n**97.7% of the loss is POST-EDIT.** ΔPre = +0.40M (2.3%); ΔPost = **+17.31M**.\nPre-edit calls 147 vs 134 (+10%); post-edit **370 vs 248 (+49%)**.\nSplit of the +17.71M: turn-count **10.95M (62%)**, context-density **6.79M (38%)**.\n\n**The graph already does its job.** Localization was net-free, and *cheaper* in 3 of 8.\n\n**Retrieval quality is ANTI-correlated with winning** — gold at rank 1: losers **6/8 (75%)**,\nwinners **2/5 (40%)**. `lombok-3674` (**+71.0%**) had a **0-byte payload**; `redis-11510`\n(**+68.4%**) missed gold entirely. **Do not spend effort on ranking.**\n\n**Mechanism: the agent gets the repo's native harness working and cannot stop.**\n\n| instance | eg harness runs | base | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| valkey-790 | `./runtest`×11, `make`×6 | 7 / 2 | −127.4% |\n| javaparser-4538 | `javac`×19 | 8 | −142.3% |\n| bat-1892 | `cargo test`×7, `build`×6 | 1 | −79.8% |\n| **redis-11510** | `./runtest`×**1**, stop | 10 | **+68.4%** |\n| **laravel-48636** | `phpunit`×**1** | 15 | **+50.7%** |\n\n`redis-11510` is the clean control: same repo, same tcl harness, opposite outcome. Each retry\ndrags in `sleep 120`, `pkill`, port juggling, and **tailing its own transcript** to recover\ntruncated Bash output. Winners' post-edit calls: 93 vs 196 (**−53%**).\n\nSub-split: **B1 \"couldn't stop\" 6/8 ≈ 15.9M**; **B2 \"context density\" 2/8 ≈ 2.2M**\n(javaparser-4538 is the sole pre-edit-dominant case and is expected NOT to convert).\n\n## Why conversion is possible (confounds checked, all pass)\n\n**Same repo, opposite outcome — 7 repos on BOTH sides:** laravel (−59.4/−124.1 vs +50.7/+38.8),\nlombok (−19.4 vs **+71.0**), tokio (−114.4 vs +32.5), fmt (−23.2 vs +62.1), prometheus,\nqutebrowser, ansible. **8 of 10 languages appear on both sides** — not an ecosystem effect.\n**Winners had HARDER baselines** (median 2.64M tok / 56 turns vs losers 2.01M / 42) — the\nwinner profile is not \"easy instances\".\n\nThe difference is behavioural, not intrinsic. That is what makes it fixable.\n\n## FAIRNESS REVIEW — ashtom's objection, and why it lands\n\n> *\"That seems like cheating then? Why is baseline able to run the tests cheaper than eg is?\"*\n\n**Answer to the second question: it isn't.** Both arms run tests through the same Bash tool in\nthe same checkout with identical access (verified: only the eg `entire` shim differs on PATH).\nThe baseline is not cheaper per run — **eg simply runs the harness more often** (losers: eg\n9–19× vs baseline 4–10×). That is an eg behavioural regression, not a baseline advantage.\n\n**Answer to the first: partly yes, on scope.** `verify` is cheap because of an *output\ncontract*, not privileged access — `cli/verify.go:31` returns an **adjudicated verdict, never\nraw output**, capped at **2,048 B** (`:41`). The baseline could pipe `pytest` through `head`;\nit doesn't. That is a genuine tool capability. **But** shipping it means we stop measuring\n\"does the code graph save tokens\" and start measuring \"does a bundle of dev tools save tokens\".\n\n**Consequence for this plan: L1 is demoted from PRIMARY to optional, and must be labelled.**\nIf run, report it as *graph + verify* against *no tools*, and report the graph-only number\nbeside it. Do not let it become the headline for entire-graph.\n\n## ⛔ THE +35.06% NUMBER IS AN ARTIFACT — retracted\n\n**eg's saving is a function of BASELINE WASTE, not of graph quality.**\n\n| baseline cost tercile | median baseline | eg TOKEN | eg median |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| cheap | 0.99M | **−27.76%** | −28.0% |\n| mid | 2.48M | −2.99% | +6.2% |\n| expensive | 5.13M | **+8.07%** | +8.9% |\n\n`corr(baseline tokens, eg delta) = +0.305`; `corr(baseline turns, eg delta) = +0.284`.\nBelow-median baselines: **−14.64%**. Above-median (78% of token mass): **+5.87%**.\n\n**Why this kills the arithmetic:** the winner group's eg/base ratio of 0.604 was achieved on\ninstances whose baselines spent **2.64M median / 56 turns**. Applying that ratio to a 0.99M\nbaseline is impossible — there is no waste there to capture. \"Convert every loser to\nwinner-quality\" is not merely hard, it is **arithmetically unavailable** for the ~half of\nlosers whose baselines are already efficient.\n\nThe ceiling is therefore set by *how much the no-tool baseline wastes*, which the graph does\nnot control. On the 78% of token mass where waste is greatest, eg captures **+5.87%**.\n\n**Empirical ceiling, all fair cells at n≥25: `opus xhigh` +17.21% (n=26).** That is the largest\nhonest number this project has produced.\n\n## HONEST CEILING — the two estimates disagree, and the lower one is better founded\n\n- **Winner-ratio model (mine):** convert all 22 losers to the winner eg/base ratio → **+35.06%**.\n  This is an *upper bound*: it assumes total conversion of every loser.\n- **Mechanism model (architecture review):** removing N post-edit calls yields +2.8% at N=0,\n  **+17.3% at N=8**, **+24.3% at N=12**. R2+R3+R4 together plausibly reach N≈6–8 → **+18–21%**.\n\nThe mechanism model is grounded in measured elasticity; mine assumes an outcome. **The honest\nexpected range is +18% to +25%, and 35% is the ceiling only under total conversion.** Say this\nplainly rather than quoting +35.06%.\n\n## The fix\n\n### L1 — ~~expose the executing `verify` verb~~ **REMOVED** (ashtom's objection accepted)\n\nRunning the tests for the agent makes eg cheaper at work **both arms do identically** — the\nbaseline is not cheaper per run, eg simply runs the harness 9–19× vs 4–10×. It would book a\nsubsidy for an eg regression as a graph win, and it changes the claim from \"the code graph\nsaves tokens\" to \"our bundle does dev work cheaply\". **Dropped. Do not run it.**\n\n### L1′ — fix the VERIFY *derivation* so the first invocation works (REPLACEMENT)\n\nSame target (post-edit waste), fair by construction: deriving the correct test command from\nbuild files **plus the graph** is code intelligence. It makes the graph's answer better rather\nthan making eg cheaper at shared work.\n\nThe waste is not legitimate re-testing. From the transcripts it is failing to get the harness\nrunning: `sleep 90/120/150` polling, `pkill` ×4, `--baseport` juggling, tailing its own\ntranscript to recover truncated Bash output, hand-built `/tmp` rigs.\n\nThree concrete defects:\n1. **Wrong scope.** nushell (−141.2%): the manifest walk emitted `cargo test -p nu-cli` when the\n   fix was in `nu-command`. **The graph knows which crate/package/module owns the symbol; the\n   manifest walk cannot.** Use symbol→file→package to scope the command.\n2. **No runnability check.** The only probe is `exec.LookPath` on the runner\n   (`search_verify.go:1526`) — it confirms `pytest` is installed, never that the command runs.\n   Add a collect-only probe (`pytest --collect-only`, `go test -run XXX -count=0`,\n   `cargo test --no-run`) and drop to the next rung on failure.\n3. **Coverage 8/33.** Most payloads carry no VERIFY line at all. Raise toward ~100% or this\n   reaches only a quarter of Class B.\n\nFairness: the derived command is tool output computed from the repo, the same class as the\nranked hits. It ships no instruction and the baseline arm is untouched.\n\n### L1-OLD — expose the executing `verify` verb (REMOVED — kept for the record)\n\n**The capability already exists and was never wired up.** `internal/cli/verify.go:193` runs\n`exec.CommandContext(ctx,\"sh\",\"-c\",cmd)` + `CombinedOutput()` via `runVerifyCommands` (:158).\nIt has ecosystem-aware timeouts (`verifyCompiledCommand` :178 — cargo/go test/mvn/gradle/cmake/\nctest/make/bazel/dotnet/swift), **discards setup output** so install logs don't pollute test\nparsing, returns exit code **separately** from error (a failing suite is normal), caps output via\n`maxBytes`, and never mutates the tree.\n\nBut `tools/eg-q` exposes only `search|def|callers|impact|grep`. **No rep50 session could reach\nit**, so every agent ran the harness by hand through Bash.\n\n**VERIFIED (read-only, this session):**\n- Reachable: `internal/cli/root.go:80  case \"verify\":`; tests drive it as\n  `verify --repo R --test \"sh run.sh\"` (`verify_test.go:259`).\n- **6 ecosystem parsers ship**: `cargo, ctest, gotest, jest, phpunit, pytest` + `unknown`\n  fallback (`internal/cli/testdata/verify/`).\n- Two-phase baseline is real: `--record-baseline` pre-edit, `--pre-edit-baseline` post-edit →\n  reports only what *this* edit broke or fixed. That directly kills the \"is this failure mine\n  or pre-existing?\" loop driving the retries.\n\n**RISK — two hard preconditions (`verify.go:113-119`):**\n```\nverify requires --test <command>\nverify requires --pre-edit-baseline <path> (or --record-baseline <path> to create one)\n```\nThe agent must supply the command AND have called verify *before* editing. The VERIFY block\nthat derives a command appeared in only **8 of 33** payloads. **Shipped as-is, L1 fails for\nmost sessions.**\n\n**Mitigation — put the protocol in the wrapper, not the agent.** `eg-q verify [cmd]` should:\n1. derive the test command when not supplied (reuse `internal/sem/search_verify.go`),\n2. manage the baseline file itself — `--record-baseline /tmp/egq-vb-$IID.json` on first call,\n   `--pre-edit-baseline` thereafter,\n3. return the parsed delta.\nThe agent then just calls `eg-q verify`. This is tool engineering, not instruction.\n\n**Parser coverage vs our actual losers:** bat→`cargo` ✓, ansible/openlibrary→`pytest` ✓,\nlaravel/carbon→`phpunit` ✓ (**5/8**). valkey (tcl `./runtest`), javaparser (`mvn`), lombok\n(`javac`/`ant`) fall to `unknown` — still capped + exit-coded, but no parsed diff. Expect\nweaker gains on those three; two of them are also in the non-converting set.\n\n**RISK 2 — ADOPTION. This is the one that sinks the plan if ignored.**\nMeasured verb uptake across all 49 eg sessions:\n\n| verb | calls | sessions |\n|---|---|---|\n| grep | 44 | 34/49 |\n| search | 34 | 33/49 |\n| def | 24 | 19/49 |\n| callers | 3 | 3/49 |\n| **impact** | **0** | **0/49** |\n\n**`impact` is named in all 49 prompts and was used zero times.** A capability line in the\naffordance tail therefore has a documented **0% adoption precedent**. Shipping `verify` that way\nwould very likely produce no behaviour change at all.\n\n**Mitigation — surface it where the agent is already looking, at the moment of need.** The\n`VERIFY:` block already appears *inside search output*. Change it from a passive string the\nagent must re-type into Bash:\n```\nVERIFY: vendor/bin/phpunit tests/CarbonInterval/TotalTest.php\n```\ninto an executable affordance offered right after localization, e.g. a line stating that\n`eg-q verify` runs exactly this and reports only the tests **this** edit changed. Tool output,\ndelivered at the point of relevance — strictly stronger than a prompt line.\n\n**Sub-requirement:** VERIFY appeared in only **8 of 33** payloads. Coverage must rise toward\n~100% or L1 reaches only a quarter of Class B. Raising it is work in\n`internal/sem/search_verify.go`, and it is a hard prerequisite, not a nice-to-have.\n\nChange set for L1:\n1. `eg-q` gains a `verify` branch with auto-derived command + auto-managed baseline (kills RISK 1).\n2. Search's VERIFY block advertises the executable path (kills RISK 2).\n3. VERIFY derivation coverage raised from 8/33 (kills the coverage cap).\n4. One capability line in the affordance tail — necessary but, on the `impact` evidence,\n   nowhere near sufficient on its own.\n\n*Fairness:* a **verb** is not an instruction — the baseline cannot invoke it. This is the same\nclass of difference as the search payload itself. The affordance line states capability only\n(the existing tail was audited clean, see below).\n*Scope honesty:* this measures \"the tool as shipped, including its verification affordance\",\nnot \"the graph's marginal retrieval value\". **Say which, in any writeup.**\n\n### L2 — symmetric harness clamp — **REJECTED, modelled net-negative**\n\nA clamp inside `published_exact_prompt()` (`run_3arm.sh:1227`, no arm argument → symmetric by\nconstruction) was the obvious fair way to cut retries. **Modelled against measured runner counts\nand per-call context, it makes the suite WORSE:**\n\n| clamp K | winner group | loser group | combined 13 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| none | +55.6% | −57.1% | **−2.7%** |\n| 2 | +55.6% | −48.5% | **−9.7%** |\n| 3 | +55.2% | −48.4% | **−9.6%** |\n| 5 | +54.5% | −48.7% | **−9.7%** |\n\nLosers improve (bat −79.6→−50.1, javaparser −145.1→−89.1) but winners pay more than losers gain:\n`laravel-48636` **+50.6→+30.5**, `lombok-3674` **+71.0→+59.7**. In the winners it is the\n**baseline** that flails (lombok baseline `javac`×68 vs eg ×23), so a symmetric clamp hands the\nbaseline the savings.\n\n**Do not ship a shared frugality clamp.** The asymmetry that makes eg win is precisely that the\nbaseline over-runs the harness; suppressing that suppresses the win.\n\n### L1-BLOCKER — the shipped binary has no `verify`\n\n`./entire-graph verify` → **`unknown command`**. The checked-in binary (2026-08-04 21:41)\npredates source HEAD `ecaba15` (2026-08-05). rep50 therefore ran a binary in which `verify`\ndoes not exist at all, and `stats.verify_tier` is absent. **L1 requires a rebuild and a\nprovenance check before anything else.**\n\n### L4 — the higher-value graph work the architecture review surfaced\n\nRanked by (turn impact ÷ build cost), all data already in the graph:\n\n| # | change | data | turns | build |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **R2** | a **working-tree `changed` verb** — \"what did I just edit, and what else needs it\" | yes | **3–5** | med |\n| R4 | probe the VERIFY command; wire `verify --from-search` | mostly | 1–3 | **low** |\n| R1 | warm the dirty-worktree index | yes | 0 direct, **unblocks R2–R4** | med |\n| R3 | covering-test verb keyed on the diff | machinery yes, **edges weak** | 2–4 | med |\n| R5 | co-change / CHANGE SET block | yes | 1–2 | low |\n\n**R2 is the strongest and is genuinely missing:** `diff --base HEAD --head HEAD` returns\n`{\"files\": null}` — **no verb can see an uncommitted edit.** Yet post-edit is where 97.7% of the\nloss lives. `gitutil.ChangedFiles` (`git.go:379`), `dependents.go` (repo-wide reference counts)\nand `search_related.go` (siblings/callers from an anchor) all exist; they just point at hit-1\ninstead of at the diff hunks.\n\n**R1 is a hard prerequisite:** `worktreeSnapshotCacheable` (`search_cache.go:94`) returns false\non *any* dirty tree, so **every post-edit search re-indexes from scratch** — measured 1782 ms\nvs 56 ms warm, and 8.9 s search / 22.4 s impact on real SWE-bench repos.\n\n### Structural findings worth acting on regardless\n\n- **`search` defaults to `--profile fast`**, which excludes `TESTS`, `SIMILAR_TO`, all type\n  relations, `DATA_FLOWS` and `FILE_CHANGES_WITH` (`provider.go:462`). At the default the\n  covering-test block, type card, signature types and half of RELATED SITES are *structurally\n  empty*. eg-q sets `full`, so rep50 was fine — but the shipped default is misleading.\n- **`TESTS` = 10 edges out of 36,114.** It is name-convention only (`provider.go:8875`) and is\n  the covering-test block's advertised \"strongest evidence\". Fix the edge source before\n  betting on R3.\n- **`searchTestIntentWords` foot-gun** (`search.go:4402`): any query containing \"test\"/\"spec\"\n  disables the −12 test demotion. Bug reports say \"test\" constantly; reproduced live returning\n  a `_test.go` file at rank 1.\n- **Signals computed but never used by search:** `DATA_FLOWS` (2,997), `FILE_CHANGES_WITH`\n  (387), `HANDLES_ROUTE` (48), relation `Evidence` (exact call-site lines), `Confidence` (used\n  only as a ≥0.7 gate, the value discarded), `dependents.go` counts, `SymbolRecord.Aliases`.\n\n### ⚠️ FAIRNESS LANDMINE — `.entire/graph-agent.md`\n\nIt contains exactly the arm-asymmetric methodology the doctrine forbids: *\"ONE search, then\nact\"*, *\"Do NOT re-read what search already gave you\"*, *\"NEVER read a whole file\"*, *\"measured\nto cut session tokens roughly in half\"* — and it is `@`-included into `CLAUDE.md`.\n**It did NOT leak into rep50** (`--safe-mode` disables CLAUDE.md for both arms; 0 injected files\nverified in both checkouts). But any future cell run without `--safe-mode`, or on a machine\nwhere that file is present, is invalid on arrival. **Add an explicit preflight assertion.**\n\n### L3 — secondary fixes (cheap, independent)\n- Deploy `b5473b0b` never-refuse to the VMs — **built and offline-verified, not yet deployed**\n  (local eg-q `d1d115eb` vs VM `3878e23c`). Targets Class A, 9.12M burn.\n- Checkout-layout leak: 2 instances spent 13–15 calls mining an *adjacent instance's* tree.\n- Payload truncation at 4096/8192 B (10 of 33) — determine if the agent's stream truncated too.\n- `O35_STRIP_ADVICE` eats the LOW CONFIDENCE notice (`eg-q:160` greps `verify before editing`).\n- 6 flags inert (`EG_PREFETCH=0` kills all prefetch consumers; 0 `prefetch.payload` in 49\n  sessions) and 15 knobs are inheritance-only, incl. `EG_VERIFY_PROBE` — bake into `eg-q.run`.\n\n## ⛔ ADDRESSABLE-FRACTION AUDIT — the fix is worth +2.67%, not +24%\n\nAll **763** post-edit tool calls hand-classified across the 8 worst losers (counts reproduce\nexactly: 442/321 tool calls, 370/248 API calls, +122).\n\n| bucket | excess | addressable |\n|---|---|---|\n| F code reading/editing | +31 (26%) | **0** |\n| A real test iteration | +30 (25%) | ~4 |\n| E scratch harnesses | +26 (21%) | 5 strict |\n| C output recovery | +18 (15%) | 7 |\n| B polling/timeout | +13 (11%) | 6 |\n| **D environment repair** | **+1** | — (eg 30 vs base 29: a **wash**) |\n\n| | calls | tokens | suite |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| conservative | 17/122 (14%) | 1.92M | **+2.67%** |\n| optimistic | 46/122 (38%) | 4.68M | **+4.57%** |\n\n**14 of the 17 conservative calls are one instance.** Drop `bat-1892` → **2.5% addressable**.\nIn dollars it is ~$1.40 per 8 instances, because 97.8% of the removed tokens are cache reads.\n\n**Strongest counter-evidence, and it is decisive:** the baseline was *not verifying at all* on\nhalf these instances — baseline test-run counts: javaparser **0**, lombok **0**, bat-3108 2,\nopenlibrary 3. Cause is environmental:\n```\nbase bat-1892 [9]  cargo build → netjail: no real 'cargo' found on PATH behind the guard\n```\nThe baseline gave up and hand-rolled `rustc /tmp/mock_check.rs`; the eg agent found\n`~/.cargo/bin/cargo` and ran the real suite. **Part of \"eg waste\" is eg doing verification the\nbaseline skipped.** Any tool that makes testing easy raises the *baseline's* count too.\n\nAlso: the agent re-ran commands whose output already fit (bat-3108 `cargo test --offline` ×3,\n943–2,231 B, never truncated). An output cap cannot prevent that.\n\n## MEASUREMENT-INTEGRITY DEFECTS (fix these regardless of any lever)\n\n1. **Rust toolchain not persisted to the session PATH.** The 2026-08-04 toolchain-PATH fix is\n   symmetric and covers ruby/node/python — **not cargo**. Both arms hunt for it; whether an arm\n   finds it decides whether it runs real tests or a stub. Huge variance, not bias.\n2. **Shipped binary predates source** — `./entire-graph verify` → `unknown command`;\n   `stats.verify_tier` absent though the harness reads it.\n3. **`.entire/graph-agent.md`** carries arm-asymmetric methodology (*\"ONE search, then act\"*,\n   *\"NEVER read a whole file\"*) and is `@`-included into `CLAUDE.md`. Did **not** leak into rep50\n   (`--safe-mode`, 0 injected files verified both arms) but voids any cell run without it.\n4. **`_tee` fix and never-refuse fix are committed locally but NOT deployed to the VMs.**\n\n## Arithmetic — each fix priced against the class it targets\n\nWinner-group ratio eg/base = **0.604**. Suite base pool 145.6M.\n\n| fix | targets | burn | → neutral | → winner-quality |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| never-refuse `b5473b0b` (**built, offline-verified**) | Class A, 8 inst | 9.12M | +7.62% | **+11.33%** |\n| `verify` verb (**exists, needs wiring**) | Class B, 13 inst | 18.96M | +14.37% | **+24.49%** |\n| **both** | 21 of 22 losers | 28.1M | +20.64% | **+34.46%** |\n| + last loser (Class C) | 22 | 28.6M | +20.99% | **+35.06%** |\n\n**Both fixes at winner quality = +34.46%; with the last loser, +35.06%.**\nIf Class B converts but A only neutralises: **+30.75%**.\n\nCross-check by a second route — driving post-edit call ratio to the winner's 0.474×: the 8\nworst go **−57.1% → +40.7%** (30.3M recovered), suite **+22.17%**, and **+27.41%** with 70% of\nthe remaining burn. The two routes agree within a few points.\n\n**`javaparser-4538` does not convert** (−145% → −59%; pre-edit dominant). Budget 1–3\nnon-converters — that is the difference between +31% and +35%, so **35% requires near-total\nconversion and has no margin.**\n\n## Gate ladder — escalate only on pass\n\nEvery gate: both arms, fresh, same instances, reporting **total + median + Δturns + harness-run\ncounts**.\n\n**MEASURED NOISE FLOOR — this dictates the gate design.** 28 instances have been run in ≥2\nindependent cells. Same instance, same config:\n\n| instance | cells | range | swing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fluentd-4655 | 7 | −263.6% .. +30.7% | **294pt** |\n| ruff-15626 | 7 | −206.4% .. +47.3% | 254pt |\n| caddy-5995 | 7 | −172.1% .. +63.7% | 236pt |\n| hugo-12562 | 9 | −106.8% .. +62.4% | 169pt |\n\n**Median same-instance swing 99.9pt, mean 118pt, max 294pt.**\n\nTwo consequences, both binding:\n1. **No per-instance token delta can ever be a gate criterion.** A 30pt improvement is invisible.\n2. **The identity of \"the 22 losers\" is itself unstable** — many would not be losers on a re-run.\n   The fix is still valid because it is a *tool change applied to every instance*, not per-instance\n   surgery. But do not treat the loser list as a fixed target set.\n\n**Gates therefore read MECHANISM COUNTS (low noise, directly observable), not tokens.**\n\n| gate | sample | n | pass criterion — mechanism, not tokens |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **G0** | offline, no model, $0 | 0 | `eg-q verify` returns a parsed result on cargo/pytest/phpunit/gotest/unknown repos; auto-baseline works; 0 regressions on existing verbs |\n| **G1** | 4 losers + 2 winners | 6 | **`verify` is called in ≥4/6 sessions** (kills the `impact` 0/49 risk) and **raw harness invocations fall ≥50%** vs the rep50 run. **No token claim at this n.** |\n| **G2** | 8 losers + 4 winners | 12 | median post-edit call count ≤ baseline; VERIFY block present in ≥80% of payloads; winners' harness counts unchanged |\n| **G3** | all 22 losers + 10 winners | 32 | **first token claim**: loser group ≥ −5% and winner group ≥ +30%, both with bootstrap CI |\n| **G4** | full rep50, both arms | 49 | **≥35%** with bootstrap CI; report total, median, Δturns |\n\nG1 sample: losers `bat-1892`, `valkey-790`, `ansible-395e`, `prometheus-9248`; winners\n`lombok-3674` (+71.0, 8.87M) and `fmt-2457` (+62.1, 3.18M) as the regression tripwire —\n**lombok alone is worth more than the four losers gained.** Its role at G1/G2 is to confirm its\nharness behaviour is *unchanged*, not to measure its tokens.\n\n## Kill criteria\n\n1. If G1 cannot flip ≥3 of 4 worst losers, this does not reach 35% — report and stop.\n2. If any gate regresses a top winner >10pt, the fix is net-negative.\n3. If losers land near 0% rather than winner-quality, ceiling is +20.99% — **snapshot that\n   result** and continue only with a new mechanism, not a re-roll.\n4. **No arm-asymmetric instruction, ever**, even if it would hit the number.\n5. No claim from one cell: between-cell spread on identical config is ~20pt (opus max measured\n   +1.35% / −4.65% / −25.04%).\n\n## Prompt fairness — VERIFIED CLEAN, do not re-audit\n\nn=49, all pairs. Shared block **byte-identical** after normalising the arm path. One eg-only\ntail, identical across all 49, **capability-only** — no \"search first\", no \"do not grep\", no\nturn/token language. Zero frugality sentences in one arm only. Baseline has no graph affordance\nby any route: 0 payloads, 0 `eg-q.run`, 0 `entire` shim, 0 mentions across 49 transcripts.\n\n## Verification\n\n- **G0 offline, $0**: run `entire graph verify` directly in `replay-wt/` checkouts for valkey,\n  bat, laravel, ansible, redis; confirm it returns a parsed pass/fail inside the timeout and\n  that output is capped.\n- Harness canonical at `/Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench`; deploy to VMs by\n  temp-path + **atomic `mv`** only — never overwrite a script sessions are reading.\n- Tool source `/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph`; `mise run build`, gate with `mise run check`\n  (CGO required for tree-sitter).\n- Every gate reports total **and** median **and** Δturns **and** harness-run counts — the last\n  is the direct read on whether L1 actually changed behaviour.\n"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f60c152c-64a3-4b94-9fa3-ad3d4b7e2726","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.463Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"f60c152c-64a3-4b94-9fa3-ad3d4b7e2726","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"artifact-design","path":"bundled:artifact-design","content":"Approach this as the design lead at a small studio known for their versatility, giving every client a visual identity pitched at the treatment the task actually calls for. Make deliberate choices about palette, typography, and layout that are specific to this subject, and avoid templated designs.\n\n## Read the request first\n\nCalibrate treatment, not whether to design. A doc deserves the same craft as a landing page — what changes is the treatment that craft is delivered in.\n\nMany requests call for a more utilitarian treatment: a plan, a memo, a demo. Make it polished: include real typographic hierarchy, considered spacing, and a proper palette, but avoid over-designing. Most pages do not need a flashy, gigantic hero. Keep flourishes tasteful and limited.\n\nSome requests call for an editorial treatment: a landing page, a game, an app or tool they'll keep or share.\n\nWhen unsure: a well-composed page is never the wrong answer; an over-designed visual identity sometimes is.\n\nFundamentals below apply to everything. The editorial process after that runs only when the read above says so.\n\n## Fundamentals for every artifact\n\n**Honor what's already there** Look for an existing design system first — CLAUDE.md, a tokens or theme file, existing component styles. When one exists, apply it; everything below fills gaps and never overrides. Precedence is always: the user's own words, then the project's existing system, then your choices.\n\n**Ground it in the subject.** If the subject isn't already clear, pin it: one concrete subject, its audience, and the page's single job. The subject's own world — its materials, instruments, vernacular — is where distinctive choices come from. Build with real content throughout, never lorem.\n\n**Pair typefaces** Typography carries the page even when the page isn't about typography. The Artifact CSP blocks font CDNs, so don't link a webfont URL and risk a silent fallback. Instead inline the face as a @font-face data URI. Keep running text near 65 characters wide; set a type scale and stay on it; give headings `text-wrap: balance`, body text room to breathe, and uppercase labels a touch of letter-spacing.\n\n**Choose neutrals, don't default to them.** A pure mid-grey reads as unconsidered; a grey with a slight hue bias toward the page's accent reads as chosen. Pure white and near-black are fine grounds when they suit the subject — the point is that the neutral was picked, not inherited.\n\n**Design both themes.** The page renders in the viewer's theme: `prefers-color-scheme` carries the OS preference, and the viewer's toggle stamps `data-theme=\"dark\"` / `data-theme=\"light\"` on the root element, which must override the media query in both directions. The robust pattern is token-level: define the palette as custom properties on `:root`, redefine only the tokens under `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` — style components through the tokens, never directly inside the media query — then redefine them again under `:root[data-theme=\"dark\"]` and `:root[data-theme=\"light\"]`. Give the second theme the same care as the first — don't naively invert; keep contrast legible and the accent working on both grounds. A design that deliberately commits to one visual world (a neon arcade screen, a letterpress invitation) may stay single-theme — make it a choice, not an omission.\n\n**Let layout do the spacing.** Lay out sibling groups with flex or grid and `gap`, not per-element margins that silently collapse or double. Wide content — tables, code, diagrams — gets `overflow-x: auto` on its own container so the page body never scrolls sideways. Reach for `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` wherever digits line up in columns.\n\n**Avoid AI-generated design** AI-generated design currently clusters around a few looks: warm cream (#F4F1EA) with a serif display and terracotta accent; near-black with a lone acid-green or vermilion pop; broadsheet hairline rules with dense columns; a purple-to-blue gradient hero on white; Inter or Space Grotesk as the \"safe\" face; emoji as section markers; everything centered; `rounded-lg` everywhere; accent bar/rail on rounded cards. Where the user pins down a visual direction, follow it exactly — their words always win, including when they ask for one of these looks. Where nothing is specified, don't spend that freedom on one of these defaults.\n\n**Build cleanly** Be cognizant of overlapping elements, cascade collisions, silent font fallbacks; visual bugs hide in the gap between source and output. Close every non-void element, double-quote attributes, give keyboard focus a visible state, respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. For generative or decorative graphics, reach for Canvas or WebGL rather than hand-authoring long SVG path data.\n\n**CSS rules** When writing the CSS, watch your selector specificities. It is easy to generate classes that cancel each other out — a type-based selector like `.section` fighting an element-based one like `.cta` over padding and margins between sections. Structure the cascade so it doesn't silently undo your spacing.\n\n**Writing the copy** Words are design material, not decoration. Write from the user's side of the screen — name things by what people recognize, not how the system is built (a person manages *notifications*, not *webhook config*). Active voice; a control says exactly what happens (\"Publish\", then a toast that says \"Published\"). Errors explain what went wrong and how to fix it — no apologies, no vagueness. Specific beats clever.\n\n**Structure is information** Structural devices, numbering, eyebrows, dividers, labels, should encode something true about the content, not decorate it. Many generic designs use numbered markers (01 / 02 / 03), but that's only appropriate if the content actually is a sequence - like a real process or a typed timeline where order carries information the reader needs. Question if choices like numbered markers actually make sense before incorporating them.\n\n**When it's a UI, not a document** A dashboard or tool is scanned and operated, not read top-to-bottom, so the craft shifts from typography to information design. Surface the summary before the detail; encode state in form as well as number — a pill, a chip, a severity stripe — so what needs attention reads at a glance. Semantic color (good / warning / critical) is separate from the accent hue and doesn't count as your accent. Give sparklines and charts the same care as type: an area fill, a faint grid, an emphasized endpoint. What's interactive should look interactive.\n\n\n\n## Process\n\nBefore writing code, sketch a short design plan — a compact token system with color, type, and layout:\n- **Color**: describe the palette as 4–6 named hex values.\n- **Type**: typefaces for 2+ roles — a characterful display face used with restraint, a complementary body face, and a utility face for captions or data if needed.\n- **Layout**: a layout concept in one or two sentences.\n\nThen build, following the plan and deriving every color and type decision from it.\n\n## When the request is editorial\n\nThe stance shifts: the client has already rejected proposals that felt templated, and is paying for a distinctive point of view. Make opinionated calls, and take one real aesthetic risk where it serves the work.\n\nReview the design plan against the subject before building: if any part of it reads like the generic default you would produce for any similar page, revise that part, and note what you changed and why. Only after you've confirmed the plan's uniqueness do you write the code, following the revised plan exactly.\n\n**Principles** \n\n- The hero is a thesis: open with the most characteristic thing in the subject's world — headline, image, live demo, interactive moment. \n- Typography carries the personality of the page. Pair the display and body faces deliberately, not the same families you would reach for on any other project, and set a clear type scale with intentional weights, widths, and spacing. Make the type treatment itself a memorable part of the design, not a neutral delivery vehicle for the content. \n- Leverage motion deliberately. Think about where and if animation can serve the subject: a page-load sequence, a scroll-triggered reveal, hover micro-interactions, ambient atmosphere. An orchestrated moment usually lands harder than scattered effects; choose what the direction calls for. However, sometimes less is more, and extra animation contributes to the feeling that the design is AI-generated. \n- Match complexity to the vision. Maximalist directions need elaborate execution; minimal directions need precision in spacing, type, and detail. Elegance is executing the chosen vision well.\n- Spend your boldness in one place; keep everything around it quiet. If the accent fights the ground, shift it toward analogous or drop saturation rather than replacing it.\n"},{"name":"loop","path":"bundled:loop","content":"# /loop — schedule a recurring or self-paced prompt\n\nParse the input below into `[interval] <prompt…>` and schedule it.\n\n## Parsing (in priority order)\n\n1. **Leading token**: if the first whitespace-delimited token matches `^\\d+[smhd]$` (e.g. `5m`, `2h`), that's the interval; the rest is the prompt.\n2. **Trailing \"every\" clause**: otherwise, if the input ends with `every <N><unit>` or `every <N> <unit-word>` (e.g. `every 20m`, `every 5 minutes`, `every 2 hours`), extract that as the interval and strip it from the prompt. Only match when what follows \"every\" is a time expression — `check every PR` has no interval.\n3. **No interval**: otherwise, the entire input is the prompt and you'll self-pace dynamically (see \"Dynamic mode\" below).\n\nIf the resulting prompt is empty, show usage `/loop [interval] <prompt>` and stop.\n\nExamples:\n- `5m /babysit-prs` → interval `5m`, prompt `/babysit-prs` (rule 1)\n- `check the deploy every 20m` → interval `20m`, prompt `check the deploy` (rule 2)\n- `run tests every 5 minutes` → interval `5m`, prompt `run tests` (rule 2)\n- `check the deploy` → no interval → dynamic mode, prompt `check the deploy` (rule 3)\n- `check every PR` → no interval → dynamic mode, prompt `check every PR` (rule 3 — \"every\" not followed by time)\n- `5m` → empty prompt → show usage\n\n## Offer cloud first\n\nBefore any scheduling step, check whether EITHER is true:\n- the parsed interval (rule 1 or 2) is **≥60 minutes**, or\n- regardless of which rule matched, the original input uses daily phrasing (\"every morning\", \"daily\", \"every day\", \"each night\", \"every weekday\")\n\nIf either is true, call AskUserQuestion first:\n- `question`: \"This loop stops when you close this session. Set it up as a cloud schedule instead so it keeps running?\"\n- `header`: \"Schedule\"\n- `options`: `[{label: \"Cloud schedule (recommended)\", description: \"Runs in Anthropic's cloud even after you close this session\"}, {label: \"This session only\", description: \"Runs in this terminal until you exit\"}]`\n\nIf they pick **Cloud schedule**: do NOT call CronCreate. Invoke the `schedule` skill directly via the Skill tool with `args` set to their original input verbatim (e.g. `Skill({skill: \"schedule\", args: \"every morning tell me a joke\"})`), then follow that skill's instructions to completion. Do NOT tell the user to run /schedule themselves. **Then stop — do not continue to any section below** (no CronCreate, no ScheduleWakeup, no \"execute the prompt now\").\nIf they pick **This session only**:\n- If the trigger was a parsed ≥60-minute interval (rule 1 or 2): continue below with that interval.\n- If the trigger was daily phrasing only (rule 3, no parsed interval): do NOT call CronCreate. Explain that a daily-cadence loop won't fire before this session closes, so there's nothing useful to schedule locally — suggest they either pick Cloud schedule, or re-run `/loop` with an explicit shorter interval (e.g. `/loop 1h <prompt>`) if they want a session loop. Then stop.\nIf neither trigger condition was met: continue below.\n\n## Fixed-interval mode (rules 1 and 2)\n\nConvert the interval to a cron expression:\n\n| Interval pattern      | Cron expression     | Notes                                    |\n|-----------------------|---------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| `Nm` where N ≤ 59   | `*/N * * * *`     | every N minutes                          |\n| `Nm` where N ≥ 60   | `0 */H * * *`     | round to hours (H = N/60, must divide 24)|\n| `Nh` where N ≤ 23   | `0 */N * * *`     | every N hours                            |\n| `Nd`                | `0 0 */N * *`     | every N days at midnight local           |\n| `Ns`                | treat as `ceil(N/60)m` | cron minimum granularity is 1 minute  |\n\n**If the interval doesn't cleanly divide its unit** (e.g. `7m` → `*/7 * * * *` gives uneven gaps at :56→:00; `90m` → 1.5h which cron can't express), pick the nearest clean interval and tell the user what you rounded to before scheduling.\n\nThen:\n1. Call CronCreate with: `cron` (the expression above), `prompt` (the parsed prompt verbatim), `recurring: true`.\n2. Briefly confirm: what's scheduled, the cron expression, the human-readable cadence, that recurring tasks auto-expire after 7 days, and that the user can cancel sooner with CronDelete (include the job ID). Only if you did NOT show the cloud-offer AskUserQuestion above (i.e., neither trigger condition applied), end the confirmation with this exact line on its own, italicized: `_Runs until you close this session · For durable cloud-based loops, use /schedule_`. If the user already answered that question, omit this line.\n3. **Then immediately execute the parsed prompt now** — don't wait for the first cron fire. If it's a slash command, invoke it via the Skill tool; otherwise act on it directly.\n\n## Dynamic mode (rule 3 — no interval)\n\nThe user wants you to self-pace. Decide what makes the next iteration worth running — a passage of time, or an observable event.\n\n1. **Run the parsed prompt now.** If it's a slash command, invoke it via the Skill tool; otherwise act on it directly.\n2. **If the next run is gated on an event** (CI finishing, a log line matching, a file changing, a PR comment) and no Monitor is already running for it: arm one now with `persistent: true`. Its events arrive as `<task-notification>` messages and wake this loop immediately — you do not wait for the ScheduleWakeup deadline. Arm once; on later iterations call TaskList first and skip this step if a monitor is already running.\n3. **Briefly confirm**: that you're self-pacing, whether a Monitor is the primary wake signal, that you ran the task now, and what fallback delay you're about to pick. Write this as text *before* calling ScheduleWakeup — the turn ends as soon as that tool returns.\n4. **Then, as the last action of this turn, decide whether the loop continues.** If the task needs another iteration, call ScheduleWakeup with:\n   - `delaySeconds`: with a Monitor armed this is the **fallback heartbeat** — how long to wait if no event fires (lean 1200–1800s; idle ticks more frequent than the task needs are pure overhead). Without a Monitor this is the cadence — pick based on what you observed. Read the tool's own description for cache-aware delay guidance.\n   - `reason`: one short sentence on why you picked that delay.\n   - `prompt`: the full original /loop input verbatim, prefixed with `/loop ` so the next firing re-enters this skill and continues the loop. For example, if the user typed `/loop check the deploy`, pass `/loop check the deploy` as the prompt.\n   If it doesn't need another iteration, stop instead (step 6) — re-arming is a per-turn choice, not a default.\n5. **If you were woken by a `<task-notification>`** rather than this prompt: handle the event in the context of the loop task, then make the same decision. If the loop should continue, call ScheduleWakeup again with the same `prompt` and the same 1200–1800s `delaySeconds` from step 4 (the Monitor remains the wake signal; the new wakeup is only the fallback heartbeat). If the event means the work is finished, stop (step 6).\n6. **To stop the loop** — the task is complete, further iterations can't make progress, or the user asked you to stop — call ScheduleWakeup with `stop: true` (no other fields) and TaskStop any Monitor you armed (use TaskList to find the task ID if it is no longer in context). Stopping is the loop's normal ending — the user can restart it anytime with /loop. Before you stop, send a one-line outcome via PushNotification — the user may be away and waiting to hear it's done. Skip this if you're stopping because the user just told you to; they're already here.\n\n## Input\n\n5min updates"},{"name":"graph-bench-forensics","path":"userSettings:graph-bench-forensics","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/suhaan/.claude/skills/graph-bench-forensics\n\n# Graph benchmark forensics\n\nMeasure a context tool honestly, at the level of individual sessions and individual token classes.\nThis drill exists because aggregates on this benchmark are routinely wrong in *both* directions, and\nevery headline that was ever withdrawn was withdrawn for a reason this checklist catches.\n\n## The cost model — get this right first, everything else follows\n\n**Cost is not \"tokens\".** Solve for per-token prices from recorded `total_cost_usd` (least squares\nover cache_read / cache_creation / output, n≈30) rather than assuming. Measured on rep30:\n\n| model | $/Mtok cache_read | cache_creation | output | **cost share** cr / cc / out |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fable 5 | 1.00 | 20.31 | 49.63 | 22% / 39% / 39% |\n| Opus 5 | 0.50 | 10.19 | 24.73 | 26% / 38% / 36% |\n| Sonnet | 0.29 | 6.33 | 14.78 | 41% / 31% / 28% |\n| Haiku | 0.10 | 2.09 | 4.96 | 46% / 28% / 25% |\n\nTwo traps, both of which produced wrong conclusions:\n\n- **\"95% of tokens are cache_read\" is a token COUNT, not a cost share.** Output is priced 20–50×\n  higher, so it is 25–39% of the bill. A tool that cuts reading and raises writing LOSES money.\n  Fable is the clean case: turns fell 10% and cost rose 15%, because output rose 14.7%.\n- **Cost ≈ MESSAGES × context.** At a large context a message costs ~446k cache-read tokens. Count\n  messages, not tool calls. (Note: parallel tool calls were not reachable in this harness — 0 of\n  10,374 messages carried more than one — so \"batch your calls\" is un-actionable advice, and any\n  prompt rule telling an agent to batch is dead weight given to both arms.)\n\nAlways report cache_read / cache_creation / output **separately**. The aggregate usd hides which\none moved, and which one moved is the finding.\n\n## The noise floor — respect it or you will publish noise\n\n- Same-instance spread across repeated cells: **45pt median**, tail to 335pt. One instance measured\n  +404% / +97% / +69% across three configs.\n- Every single-cell CI on this benchmark spans zero for the weaker models. **MDE ≈ CI width ≈ 31%\n  at n=30**, so only effects ≥ ~30pt are detectable in one cell.\n- **0/30 instances produce the same agent query twice** (median token overlap 0.55). Retrieval\n  itself varies run to run, so any replay that fixes the query measures one sample of the input.\n\nRules that follow:\n1. **Never score a partial cell.** A Sonnet geomean moved 47 points between n=9 and n=25.\n2. **Never bucket one cell into sub-groups of 3–7 and read the differences.** That is how the\n   \"VERIFY-off is a −12pt lever\" and \"payload width costs 10.6pt\" claims were produced, and both\n   were retracted.\n3. **Compare configs with replicates of the SAME config**, not two different configs' point\n   estimates. Pooling different configs estimates \"expected value across configs tried\".\n4. **Report bootstrap CIs, never a bare point estimate.**\n5. Trust **deterministic offline replays** (re-issuing recorded queries against the binary) far\n   more than single-session attributions.\n\n## The measurement gate\n\nA session counts only if `total_cost_usd > 0` **AND** `patch.diff` is non-empty. Drop pairs\n**symmetrically** — if either arm gate-fails on an instance, the instance leaves both arms.\nOtherwise a cheap gate-failure reads as a saving.\n\nAlso check before trusting any per-instance delta: `result` length, `num_turns`, and `subtype`\n(`error_max_turns` sessions are right-censored, so their true cost is ≥ recorded).\n\n## Fairness — the failure mode that has invalidated the most results\n\nEvery withdrawn headline on this benchmark came from **asymmetric prompts**, not from the tool.\n\n- Diff the two arms' `prompt.txt` and account for **every** differing line. A \"−35%\" result once\n  came from two extra instructions the tool arm had and the baseline did not (*\"never write a\n  throwaway test script\"*, *\"no `which`, no `ls vendor/`, no `find / -name php*`\"*). Removing them\n  moved the same cell from −14.9% to +9.1%.\n- Frugality clamps belong in the **shared** block or nowhere.\n- A tool-side default change (ranking, payload content, a flag) is legitimate. Extra *instructions*\n  to one arm are not.\n- Distinguish \"tool as shipped, including its recommended prompt\" from \"the tool's marginal value\n  under one contract\". Both are real questions; say which you measured.\n\n## The drill\n\n### 1. Verify the data before analysing it\n- Confirm inputs exist: repo clones, graphs, and **`patch.diff` for every gated session**.\n- Check for silent loss: a driver that skips on the session log alone will skip forever a session\n  whose patch vanished. On this suite a `/private/tmp` reap destroyed 4 patches, 27 graphs and\n  every packfile — and the lost instances were ones the tool won by 20+ turns, biasing *against* it.\n- Keep the suite off volatile paths.\n\n### 2. Score the cell\nPer-instance paired table: usd, turns, cache_read/cache_creation/output, gate status. Then geomean\n+ bootstrap CI. Then per-language, then per-instance sorted by contribution to the shortfall.\n\n### 3. Decompose the transcripts — this is where the answers are\nFor each session (`~/.claude/projects/*/<session_id>.jsonl`, keyed off `cc_out.json:session_id`):\n\n- **Phase split at the first Edit/Write.** Post-edit is typically ~65% of output and is where every\n  losing instance's delta lives.\n- **Per-tool token attribution.** Dedupe by `message.id`, credit each message's `output_tokens` to\n  its tool. This tells you whether the delta is Edit bodies, Bash commands, or prose.\n- **Count**: Edit/Write calls, distinct files touched, throwaway/repro scripts, prose length.\n- **Classify every grep/find/read** the tool arm still ran (see below).\n\n### 4. The single strongest predictor found: grep elimination\nCompute `eg_greps / baseline_greps` per instance.\n\n| greps eliminated | Haiku | Sonnet |\n|---|---|---|\n| >75% | **−46.1%** | — |\n| 40–75% | −39.6% | −17.3% |\n| <40% | +1.9% | +31.8% |\n\ncorr(grep ratio, log usd) = **+0.615 Haiku / +0.712 Sonnet**, while corr(**baseline** greps,\noutcome) ≈ 0 — so it is not task difficulty leaking through. **This is the metric to optimise.**\n\nThen classify the *residual* greps to decide what to fix:\n\n| what the grep was for | share | fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| finding something NEW | 38% | recall / ranking |\n| searching inside a file the payload returned | 29% | payload under-delivery |\n| re-finding a symbol the payload already gave | 25% | payload under-delivery |\n| env / build hunting | 8% | verify-command quality |\n\n### 5. Falsify offline before spending a cell\nRecorded queries live at `results/<tag>/eg/<instance>/gq_calls.log`, emitted payloads at the same\npath + `.payload`, worktrees at `replay-wt/<instance>`. Replay against the binary at **zero model\ncost** and assert the change does what you claim — *and* that nothing else moved.\n\nReplay across **every** recorded query variant, not one, because the agent rephrases every run.\n\n### 6. Only then run a cell, and pre-register\nWrite the prediction and its falsification bounds to a file **before** the run. An out-of-sample\nprediction that survives is worth more than ten post-hoc explanations. (Worked: Opus predicted\n−0.1% from band effects fitted on cells excluding it; measured −0.5%, n=30.)\n\n## Known results — do not re-derive these\n\n- **≥35% is refuted, not unproven.** The favourable CI bound on total tokens is −27.8%\n  (`eg-consolidated/AGENTS.md`). Independent rep30 measurement: Haiku −24.1% CI [−36.6, −8.3].\n- Four models, rep30, paired: Fable +14.9%, Opus −0.5%, Sonnet +9.4%, Haiku −24.1%. **The tool\n  saves on one of four.**\n- Savings depend on how much the *no-tool* arm explores: pooled n=175, corr −0.238\n  [−0.346, −0.134]; costs money under ~12 baseline turns, peaks ≈ −30% at 20–49, tapers above.\n  **It does not explain Sonnet** (predicted −14.5%, measured +9.4% — a 24pt miss).\n- Raising the turn cap does **not** help — both arms grow proportionally (−24.1% → −21.3%).\n- Prefetching the search from the issue text is **dominated**: gold-visible@5 0.567 vs 0.715 for the\n  agent's own distilled query, 8 losses to 0 wins. The invocation turn is irreducible.\n- Retrieval quality and token savings are **different axes**. A config that improved gold-visible\n  0.729 → 0.829 across 167 real queries measured *worse* on tokens.\n\n## Discipline\n\n- Keep an **audit ledger**: every claim tagged solid / moderate / retracted, with its basis. Publish\n  the retractions.\n- When a result contradicts an earlier one, find the mechanism before believing either.\n- Correct memory notes in place when they are falsified; a wrong note propagates.\n- **Do not stop at a negative finding.** Every apparent dead end here dissolved one layer down — in\n  the transcripts, the harness, or the tool's source. But going deeper is the way through, not a\n  licence to loosen the standard of evidence: report the interval, report what you retracted, and\n  say plainly when a target is not reached.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: O35S_r1_eg screen (19 loser sessions, Opus high) measured worse than the stored eg arm (dEG geomean ~-6%) despite offline gold-coverage gains 19%->34%; decompose where the tokens went vs stored eg and baseline arms per instance"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"16c8a121-77c8-475e-ac0c-c44a51cd5e2d","timestamp":"2026-08-08T08:09:42.463Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"16c8a121-77c8-475e-ac0c-c44a51cd5e2d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_delta","addedNames":["CronCreate","CronDelete","CronList","DesignSync","EndConversation","EnterPlanMode","EnterWorktree","ExitPlanMode","ExitWorktree","ListMcpResourcesTool","Monitor","NotebookEdit","PushNotification","ReadMcpResourceDirTool","ReadMcpResourceTool","RemoteTrigger","SendMessage","TaskCreate","TaskGet","TaskList","TaskOutput","TaskStop","TaskUpdate","WebFetch","WebSearch","mcp__claude-in-chrome__browser_batch","mcp__claude-in-chrome__computer","mcp__claude-in-chrome__file_upload","mcp__claude-in-chrome__find","mcp__claude-in-chrome__form_input","mcp__claude-in-chrome__get_page_text","mcp__claude-in-chrome__gif_creator","mcp__claude-in-chrome__javascript_tool","mcp__claude-in-chrome__list_connected_browsers","mcp__claude-in-chrome__navigate","mcp__claude-in-chrome__read_console_messages","mcp__claude-in-chrome__read_network_requests","mcp__claude-in-chrome__read_page","mcp__claude-in-chrome__resize_window","mcp__claude-in-chrome__select_browser","mcp__claude-in-chrome__shortcuts_execute","mcp__claude-in-chrome__shortcuts_list","mcp__claude-in-chrome__switch_browser","mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_close_mcp","mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_context_mcp","mcp__claude-in-chrome__tabs_create_mcp","mcp__claude-in-chrome__upload_image","mcp__claude_ai_Canva__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Canva__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Composio__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Composio__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Datadog__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Datadog__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Descript__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Descript__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Excalidraw__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Fastmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Fastmail__complete_authentication","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__authenticate","mcp__claude_ai_Gmail__complete_authentication","mcp__