{"parentUuid":"a040db92-7b05-419a-8d7e-016bd5ca028d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqSMMTPjmh36Zf5yYRdx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Artifact is live: **https://claude.ai/code/artifact/5a57cd50-f9a4-4580-92fe-fbc03fa4f8bd** — click any bar to load that instance into the comparator; it defaults to `lombok-3674` (+6.09 pts) against `bat-1892` (−2.81 pts).\n\nNow the plan. Writing it against the exact economics rather than intuition:"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_read_input_tokens":404491,"output_tokens":8894,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":493,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8894,"cache_read_input_tokens":404491,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":493},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqSMFe9FCFqShsKFjNdi","attributionSkill":"artifact-design","type":"assistant","uuid":"441df6be-09a0-4988-916f-373995168859","timestamp":"2026-08-08T14:28:22.010Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"441df6be-09a0-4988-916f-373995168859","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqSMMTPjmh36Zf5yYRdx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DHwcetjnaboQ7LWosYoYuQ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/EG-ROUTE-TO-35.md","content":"# entire-graph → 35%: the route, the arithmetic, and the one intervention that reaches it\n\n**Written 2026-08-08. Supersedes `PLAN-TO-35PCT.md`, whose gate ladder ran to completion and\nfailed at G1, G2 and G3.** Every number here is measured on the rep50 cell (49 gated pairs,\nClaude Opus, SWE-bench Multilingual) unless marked as a projection.\n\nSign convention: **positive = the graph arm saved tokens.**\n\n---\n\n## 0. The problem, in one ratio\n\nAcross all 49 pairs:\n\n| | count |\n|---|---|\n| graph calls made by the eg arm | **94** |\n| shell-exploration calls eliminated | 72 |\n| harness runs eliminated | 31 |\n| **messages eliminated, total** | **103** |\n| **net messages saved** (103 − 94) | **9** |\n\n**Eliminations per graph call: 1.10.**\n\nNine messages out of 2,671 is 0.34% of the message count. Under the governing law\n(`log tok ~ log msgs`, elasticity **1.682**, r=**0.957**, n=536) that predicts ≈ +0.6% tokens.\nMeasured headline: **+1.35%**. The model and the measurement agree.\n\n**35% requires removing 556 of the 2,671 baseline turns.** At the current call volume that means\neach graph call must eliminate **6.9 messages instead of 1.10** — a **6.3× move**. This single\nnumber is the whole problem, and every proposal below is judged against it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Where the removable turns actually are\n\nBaseline, all 49 sessions, phase-split at the first `Edit`/`Write`:\n\n| bucket | calls | share of 2,671 | eg's count | eliminated |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| shell exploration | **855** | 32.0% | 783 | 72 (8.4%) |\n| harness runs (test/build) | **576** | 21.6% | 545 | 31 (5.4%) |\n| other bash | 524 | 19.6% | 524 | 0 |\n| edits | 397 | 14.9% | 414 | −17 |\n| file reads | 240 | 9.0% | 238 | 2 |\n| waits / kills | 62 | 2.3% | 62 | 0 |\n| graph calls | 0 | — | 94 | −94 |\n\nShell exploration breakdown (baseline): `grep` 409, `ls` 263, `sed` 161, `cat` 107, `find` 36.\n\n**Only two buckets are addressable by a code graph — exploration (855) and harness (576).**\nTogether they are 1,431 turns, so 556 is 39% of the addressable pool. It is not arithmetically\nimpossible. It is behaviourally hard, for the reason in §2.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism, measured — and a correction to the previous plan\n\nSplit the 49 pairs by outcome:\n\n| | n | pts | eg shell | base shell | shell elim. | eg harness | base harness | harness elim. | graph calls |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **lift** | 24 | +21.21 | 370 | 440 | **+70** | 229 | 344 | **+115** | 50 |\n| **drag** | 25 | −19.86 | **413** | **415** | **+2** | 316 | 232 | **−84** | 44 |\n\nThree things follow, and two of them correct claims I previously made.\n\n**2.1 — In the losing half the graph displaced nothing.** 413 vs 415 shell calls. Two out of 415.\nThe payload was delivered and the agent explored exactly as much as it would have without it.\nGraph-call counts are near-identical between the halves (44 vs 50), so this is *not* a usage\ndifference. It is whether the answer landed.\n\n**2.2 — CORRECTION: eg does not verify more than the baseline. It verifies less.**\nPopulation: **eg 545 harness runs vs baseline 576**. Post-edit specifically: **eg 384 vs 422**.\nThe earlier claim (\"eg runs `./runtest` 11× vs baseline 7×\") was computed on the *8 worst\nlosers* — a set selected on the outcome. Over-verification is what a losing session looks like in\n*either* arm:\n\n| group | eg runs/session | base runs/session |\n|---|---|---|\n| eg won (24) | 6.3 | 10.0 |\n| eg lost (25) | 9.3 | 7.3 |\n| 8 worst losers | 12.4 | 7.9 |\n\n`corr(Δharness runs, tokens saved) = −0.612`, n=49. Verification looping *is* the loss mechanism,\nbut eg's defect is **variance, not tendency**. Excluding three blow-up sessions the arm runs\n**327 vs 402**. This also answers the external objection directly: the baseline is not cheaper at\ntesting, and eg is not running more tests.\n\n**2.3 — The biggest win is the baseline blowing up, not eg being efficient.**\n`lombok-3674` (+6.09 pts, +71.0%) — baseline ran `javac` **70 times**, eg ran it 23. Same\nmechanism as the losses, opposite arm. The headline is the difference of two blow-up processes\nthat hit either arm at roughly a coin flip: drag −19.86, lift +21.21, net +1.35.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The ceiling of loser repair, computed exactly\n\nThe drag pool is **−19.86 points** spread over 25 instances; the top five carry **−11.42 (57%)**.\n\n> **If every losing instance were made exactly neutral, the headline would be +21.21%.**\n\nThat is not a projection — it is the sum of the lift pool. **Loser repair cannot reach 35%\nby definition.** Reaching 35% additionally requires the *winners* to improve by ~65% beyond what\nthey already deliver.\n\nAnd loser repair is not even available at full strength: **~93% of losing instances flip sign\nbetween cells** (median same-instance swing 99.9 pts, max 294). \"The 22 losers\" is not a stable\ntarget set; only a change applied to every instance can work.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Why three built capabilities changed nothing\n\n| capability | where advertised | adoption |\n|---|---|---|\n| `impact` | affordance tail | **0 / 49** |\n| `changed` (G1) | affordance tail | **0 / 5** |\n| `--path` scoping (G3) | affordance tail | **0 / 12** |\n| `search` | primary instruction | 33 / 49 |\n| `grep` | primary instruction | 34 / 49 |\n| `def` | primary instruction | 19 / 49 |\n\n**Everything named in the primary instruction gets used. Everything in the tail gets ~zero.**\nG3 is the cleanest read: adding two genuinely useful capabilities moved graph calls *up*\n(1.9 → 3.1 per session) and displacement *down* (0.96 → 0.76). More calls, less removed.\n\n**Consequence, and it is binding on everything below: any proposal that requires the agent to\nchoose to reach for something new has a measured 0/66 track record. Do not build a fourth.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. The route: attack the ratio's two sides separately\n\n`eliminations per call = messages_removed / calls_made`. There are exactly two ways up.\n\n### R-A — Drive the denominator to zero: zero-toll delivery *(fair, cheap, untested on this metric)*\n\nThe graph's 94 calls are 94 messages of pure overhead. The tool already implements free delivery:\n`internal/cli/search.go` — *\"ZERO-TOLL DELIVERY. When the caller has already computed this\nsession's payload and handed it to the agent inside context the session pays for anyway, this\ncall must cost nothing: echo those bytes and return.\"* (`opts.Env.PresearchPath`.)\n\nCompute the payload from the issue text before the session starts and place it in turn 0.\n\n- **Effect:** removes 94 messages outright = 3.5% of messages ≈ **+5.9% tokens**.\n- **Risk:** prefetched retrieval is worse than the agent's own distilled query — gold-visible@5\n  **0.567 vs 0.715**. If eliminations fall 103 → ~70, net becomes 70 vs today's 9.\n- **Projected headline: +5% to +9%.**\n- **Why it was previously dismissed, and why that was wrong:** prefetch was struck on *retrieval\n  quality*. Under the message law retrieval quality is not the metric — message count is. A\n  worse payload delivered for free can beat a better payload that costs a turn. This has never\n  been measured on tokens.\n- **Fairness:** payload, not instruction. It ships no advice; the no-tool arm cannot produce it.\n  Same class as the search result itself. **Fair.**\n\n### R-B — Drive the numerator up: answer the command the agent already types *(reaches 35%, but changes the claim)*\n\nThe agent types `grep` **409 times** across 49 sessions and `entire` 94 times. Adoption of the\ngraph is 1.9 calls/session; adoption of grep is 8.3.\n\n**Put the graph behind `grep` itself** — a PATH shim in the eg arm that answers a grep with the\nenclosing symbol, its complete body and its callers, instead of matching lines.\n\n- **Zero adoption cost.** This is the only proposal on the table that does not require the agent\n  to learn anything, and it is the only one whose ceiling contains 35%.\n- **Effect if each shimmed grep removes one follow-up read/grep:** ~400 messages = 15% of\n  messages ≈ **+25% tokens**. Combined with R-A: **+30% to +35%**.\n- **Fairness — this is the decision, and it is genuinely close to the line.**\n  - *For:* it is tool output. No instruction is added; the agent is told nothing. The baseline\n    has real grep, the tool arm has a code-aware grep — the same class of difference as having\n    `entire graph search` at all, and the same mechanism as the existing `entire` PATH shim.\n  - *Against:* it silently redefines a standard command, so the two arms no longer run the same\n    program. That is environment divergence rather than tool provision, and the doctrine polices\n    exactly this boundary.\n  - **My assessment: defensible under the letter of the doctrine, but it changes the claim from\n    \"a code graph saves tokens\" to \"replacing grep with a code-aware grep saves tokens.\"** That\n    is a legitimate — arguably more interesting — product claim, but it is a different one, and\n    any writeup must say which was measured. This is the same treatment the executing `verify`\n    verb got, and for the same reason.\n  - **Risk of it backfiring:** the agent uses grep for logs, build files and changelogs, not only\n    code. A shim that mangles those responses sends it straight back to `/usr/bin/grep` and costs\n    a turn. Must degrade to real grep whenever the pattern or path is not code-like.\n\n### R-C — Static post-edit consistency check *(fair, blocked)*\n\nNew capability: re-parse the working tree, report unresolved references, arity mismatches and\nrenamed symbols with live callers — without running anything. Attacks the 576-run harness pool,\nwhich is where the drag group loses (+84 runs).\n\n**Blocked by §4.** It is post-edit, and 42/49 sessions make no post-edit graph call. The rider\n(G2) already tried to solve exactly this and delivered **0/5**, measuring **−12.19%**. Do not\nbuild this until a delivery channel exists that does not need agent initiative — which means a\nharness hook, which means `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE`, which alters every future cell.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Struck — do not re-propose\n\nEach was measured and killed. Re-proposing any of these has already cost real money.\n\n1. Eleven payload-quality metrics (completeness, gold coverage, byte width, rank) — all 0.\n2. Retrieval/ranking quality — **anti-correlated**: gold at rank 1 in losers 6/8 vs winners 2/5.\n3. Session-length routing · language/repo gating · confidence gating (abstention worse).\n4. Per-loser repair — +0.0 pt; losers are not stable (93% flip sign).\n5. Locate-call reduction as a token lever — corr +0.188 over 10 cells.\n6. Raising the turn cap — both arms grow proportionally.\n7. `EG_VERIFY_MODE=noworld` — +35.1% **worse**.\n8. Symmetric frugality clamp — modelled net-negative: in the winners it is the *baseline* that\n   flails (lombok baseline `javac`×70 vs eg ×23), so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline.\n9. More graph calls — G3: 1.9 → 3.1 calls, displacement 0.96 → 0.76.\n10. Any new verb in the affordance tail — 0/66 across three attempts.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Gates\n\nMechanism counts only below n≈30. MDE is ~25–30 pts against a ~100-pt same-instance noise floor,\nso **no token claim may be made from a small cell in either direction** — a discipline G3\nviolated in spirit by moving −37.7 → +9.8 → −1.2 across three polls of the same cell.\n\n| gate | what | n | pass criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **H0** | offline, $0 | 0 | shim answers all 409 recorded grep commands; degrades to real grep on non-code patterns; zero regressions on existing verbs |\n| **H1** | replay, $0 | 409 | for each recorded grep, does the shim's answer contain what the agent went on to read next? Target ≥60% |\n| **H2** | live, both arms | 12 | **eliminations per graph call ≥ 3.0** (from 1.10). Mechanism count, not tokens |\n| **H3** | live, both arms | 30 | ≥ 5.0 per call; report Δturns and harness counts |\n| **H4** | full rep50 | 49 | first token claim, with bootstrap CI, total **and** median |\n\n**Kill criteria.** If H1 lands under 40%, R-B does not reach 35% — stop and ship R-A alone.\nIf H2 lands under 2.0, stop entirely: the ratio cannot be moved by tool output, and the honest\nnumber is the fair ceiling in §8.\n\n---\n\n## 8. What is true regardless of which route is taken\n\n- **Fair ceiling from loser repair alone: +21.21%** (exact, = the lift pool).\n- **Best fair cell ever measured: +17.21%** (opus xhigh, n=26).\n- **Current: +1.35%** (n=49), median −4.2%, wins 24/49.\n- R-A alone: projected **+5% to +9%**, fair without qualification.\n- R-A + R-B: projected **+30% to +35%**, but measures a different claim and must be labelled.\n\n**35% is not reachable by any intervention that leaves the agent's exploration behaviour intact**,\nbecause the graph currently removes 8.4% of exploration and 5.4% of harness runs, and adding\ncapability made both worse. The only lever with 35% inside its range is the one that intercepts\nbehaviour the agent already has, rather than offering behaviour it must adopt.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Open defects to fix before the next cell\n\n1. **`EMPTY PAYLOAD` detector is broken** — fires on sessions holding 7,757 payload bytes and\n   3 graph calls. Corrupts any uptake metric computed from it. Seen 3× in G3.\n2. **Gate admits junk patches** — `carbon-2981` baseline patch was **371,308 bytes** (vendor or\n   build output) and passed as success. Add a size/содержание sanity bound, symmetric.\n3. **VMs A and B are spot instances and were preempted**, freezing Gemini 300 at n=207 and\n   leaving 52 rep30 sessions unfinished. Move long cells to non-preemptible (E/F) or checkpoint.\n4. **`.entire/graph-agent.md` fairness landmine** — carries arm-asymmetric methodology and is\n   `@`-included into `CLAUDE.md`. It did *not* leak into rep50 (`--safe-mode`, 0 injected files\n   verified in both arms), but any cell run without `--safe-mode` is invalid on arrival. Add an\n   explicit preflight assertion rather than relying on the flag.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_read_input_tokens":404491,"output_tokens":8894,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":493,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8894,"cache_read_input_tokens":404491,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":493},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqSMFe9FCFqShsKFjNdi","attributionSkill":"artifact-design","type":"assistant","uuid":"afe50638-693c-4594-8efa-f47252c1c5d1","timestamp":"2026-08-08T14:29:34.370Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"afe50638-693c-4594-8efa-f47252c1c5d1","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d6032c5e-0887-4a82-a3cb-2a093f118b85","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DHwcetjnaboQ7LWosYoYuQ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /Users/suhaan/devenv/EG-ROUTE-TO-35.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"aab700b5-18c5-4d72-a090-16ac36f8aba0","timestamp":"2026-08-08T14:29:34.416Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/EG-ROUTE-TO-35.md","content":"# entire-graph → 35%: the route, the arithmetic, and the one intervention that reaches it\n\n**Written 2026-08-08. Supersedes `PLAN-TO-35PCT.md`, whose gate ladder ran to completion and\nfailed at G1, G2 and G3.** Every number here is measured on the rep50 cell (49 gated pairs,\nClaude Opus, SWE-bench Multilingual) unless marked as a projection.\n\nSign convention: **positive = the graph arm saved tokens.**\n\n---\n\n## 0. The problem, in one ratio\n\nAcross all 49 pairs:\n\n| | count |\n|---|---|\n| graph calls made by the eg arm | **94** |\n| shell-exploration calls eliminated | 72 |\n| harness runs eliminated | 31 |\n| **messages eliminated, total** | **103** |\n| **net messages saved** (103 − 94) | **9** |\n\n**Eliminations per graph call: 1.10.**\n\nNine messages out of 2,671 is 0.34% of the message count. Under the governing law\n(`log tok ~ log msgs`, elasticity **1.682**, r=**0.957**, n=536) that predicts ≈ +0.6% tokens.\nMeasured headline: **+1.35%**. The model and the measurement agree.\n\n**35% requires removing 556 of the 2,671 baseline turns.** At the current call volume that means\neach graph call must eliminate **6.9 messages instead of 1.10** — a **6.3× move**. This single\nnumber is the whole problem, and every proposal below is judged against it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Where the removable turns actually are\n\nBaseline, all 49 sessions, phase-split at the first `Edit`/`Write`:\n\n| bucket | calls | share of 2,671 | eg's count | eliminated |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| shell exploration | **855** | 32.0% | 783 | 72 (8.4%) |\n| harness runs (test/build) | **576** | 21.6% | 545 | 31 (5.4%) |\n| other bash | 524 | 19.6% | 524 | 0 |\n| edits | 397 | 14.9% | 414 | −17 |\n| file reads | 240 | 9.0% | 238 | 2 |\n| waits / kills | 62 | 2.3% | 62 | 0 |\n| graph calls | 0 | — | 94 | −94 |\n\nShell exploration breakdown (baseline): `grep` 409, `ls` 263, `sed` 161, `cat` 107, `find` 36.\n\n**Only two buckets are addressable by a code graph — exploration (855) and harness (576).**\nTogether they are 1,431 turns, so 556 is 39% of the addressable pool. It is not arithmetically\nimpossible. It is behaviourally hard, for the reason in §2.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The mechanism, measured — and a correction to the previous plan\n\nSplit the 49 pairs by outcome:\n\n| | n | pts | eg shell | base shell | shell elim. | eg harness | base harness | harness elim. | graph calls |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **lift** | 24 | +21.21 | 370 | 440 | **+70** | 229 | 344 | **+115** | 50 |\n| **drag** | 25 | −19.86 | **413** | **415** | **+2** | 316 | 232 | **−84** | 44 |\n\nThree things follow, and two of them correct claims I previously made.\n\n**2.1 — In the losing half the graph displaced nothing.** 413 vs 415 shell calls. Two out of 415.\nThe payload was delivered and the agent explored exactly as much as it would have without it.\nGraph-call counts are near-identical between the halves (44 vs 50), so this is *not* a usage\ndifference. It is whether the answer landed.\n\n**2.2 — CORRECTION: eg does not verify more than the baseline. It verifies less.**\nPopulation: **eg 545 harness runs vs baseline 576**. Post-edit specifically: **eg 384 vs 422**.\nThe earlier claim (\"eg runs `./runtest` 11× vs baseline 7×\") was computed on the *8 worst\nlosers* — a set selected on the outcome. Over-verification is what a losing session looks like in\n*either* arm:\n\n| group | eg runs/session | base runs/session |\n|---|---|---|\n| eg won (24) | 6.3 | 10.0 |\n| eg lost (25) | 9.3 | 7.3 |\n| 8 worst losers | 12.4 | 7.9 |\n\n`corr(Δharness runs, tokens saved) = −0.612`, n=49. Verification looping *is* the loss mechanism,\nbut eg's defect is **variance, not tendency**. Excluding three blow-up sessions the arm runs\n**327 vs 402**. This also answers the external objection directly: the baseline is not cheaper at\ntesting, and eg is not running more tests.\n\n**2.3 — The biggest win is the baseline blowing up, not eg being efficient.**\n`lombok-3674` (+6.09 pts, +71.0%) — baseline ran `javac` **70 times**, eg ran it 23. Same\nmechanism as the losses, opposite arm. The headline is the difference of two blow-up processes\nthat hit either arm at roughly a coin flip: drag −19.86, lift +21.21, net +1.35.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The ceiling of loser repair, computed exactly\n\nThe drag pool is **−19.86 points** spread over 25 instances; the top five carry **−11.42 (57%)**.\n\n> **If every losing instance were made exactly neutral, the headline would be +21.21%.**\n\nThat is not a projection — it is the sum of the lift pool. **Loser repair cannot reach 35%\nby definition.** Reaching 35% additionally requires the *winners* to improve by ~65% beyond what\nthey already deliver.\n\nAnd loser repair is not even available at full strength: **~93% of losing instances flip sign\nbetween cells** (median same-instance swing 99.9 pts, max 294). \"The 22 losers\" is not a stable\ntarget set; only a change applied to every instance can work.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Why three built capabilities changed nothing\n\n| capability | where advertised | adoption |\n|---|---|---|\n| `impact` | affordance tail | **0 / 49** |\n| `changed` (G1) | affordance tail | **0 / 5** |\n| `--path` scoping (G3) | affordance tail | **0 / 12** |\n| `search` | primary instruction | 33 / 49 |\n| `grep` | primary instruction | 34 / 49 |\n| `def` | primary instruction | 19 / 49 |\n\n**Everything named in the primary instruction gets used. Everything in the tail gets ~zero.**\nG3 is the cleanest read: adding two genuinely useful capabilities moved graph calls *up*\n(1.9 → 3.1 per session) and displacement *down* (0.96 → 0.76). More calls, less removed.\n\n**Consequence, and it is binding on everything below: any proposal that requires the agent to\nchoose to reach for something new has a measured 0/66 track record. Do not build a fourth.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. The route: attack the ratio's two sides separately\n\n`eliminations per call = messages_removed / calls_made`. There are exactly two ways up.\n\n### R-A — Drive the denominator to zero: zero-toll delivery *(fair, cheap, untested on this metric)*\n\nThe graph's 94 calls are 94 messages of pure overhead. The tool already implements free delivery:\n`internal/cli/search.go` — *\"ZERO-TOLL DELIVERY. When the caller has already computed this\nsession's payload and handed it to the agent inside context the session pays for anyway, this\ncall must cost nothing: echo those bytes and return.\"* (`opts.Env.PresearchPath`.)\n\nCompute the payload from the issue text before the session starts and place it in turn 0.\n\n- **Effect:** removes 94 messages outright = 3.5% of messages ≈ **+5.9% tokens**.\n- **Risk:** prefetched retrieval is worse than the agent's own distilled query — gold-visible@5\n  **0.567 vs 0.715**. If eliminations fall 103 → ~70, net becomes 70 vs today's 9.\n- **Projected headline: +5% to +9%.**\n- **Why it was previously dismissed, and why that was wrong:** prefetch was struck on *retrieval\n  quality*. Under the message law retrieval quality is not the metric — message count is. A\n  worse payload delivered for free can beat a better payload that costs a turn. This has never\n  been measured on tokens.\n- **Fairness:** payload, not instruction. It ships no advice; the no-tool arm cannot produce it.\n  Same class as the search result itself. **Fair.**\n\n### R-B — Drive the numerator up: answer the command the agent already types *(reaches 35%, but changes the claim)*\n\nThe agent types `grep` **409 times** across 49 sessions and `entire` 94 times. Adoption of the\ngraph is 1.9 calls/session; adoption of grep is 8.3.\n\n**Put the graph behind `grep` itself** — a PATH shim in the eg arm that answers a grep with the\nenclosing symbol, its complete body and its callers, instead of matching lines.\n\n- **Zero adoption cost.** This is the only proposal on the table that does not require the agent\n  to learn anything, and it is the only one whose ceiling contains 35%.\n- **Effect if each shimmed grep removes one follow-up read/grep:** ~400 messages = 15% of\n  messages ≈ **+25% tokens**. Combined with R-A: **+30% to +35%**.\n- **Fairness — this is the decision, and it is genuinely close to the line.**\n  - *For:* it is tool output. No instruction is added; the agent is told nothing. The baseline\n    has real grep, the tool arm has a code-aware grep — the same class of difference as having\n    `entire graph search` at all, and the same mechanism as the existing `entire` PATH shim.\n  - *Against:* it silently redefines a standard command, so the two arms no longer run the same\n    program. That is environment divergence rather than tool provision, and the doctrine polices\n    exactly this boundary.\n  - **My assessment: defensible under the letter of the doctrine, but it changes the claim from\n    \"a code graph saves tokens\" to \"replacing grep with a code-aware grep saves tokens.\"** That\n    is a legitimate — arguably more interesting — product claim, but it is a different one, and\n    any writeup must say which was measured. This is the same treatment the executing `verify`\n    verb got, and for the same reason.\n  - **Risk of it backfiring:** the agent uses grep for logs, build files and changelogs, not only\n    code. A shim that mangles those responses sends it straight back to `/usr/bin/grep` and costs\n    a turn. Must degrade to real grep whenever the pattern or path is not code-like.\n\n### R-C — Static post-edit consistency check *(fair, blocked)*\n\nNew capability: re-parse the working tree, report unresolved references, arity mismatches and\nrenamed symbols with live callers — without running anything. Attacks the 576-run harness pool,\nwhich is where the drag group loses (+84 runs).\n\n**Blocked by §4.** It is post-edit, and 42/49 sessions make no post-edit graph call. The rider\n(G2) already tried to solve exactly this and delivered **0/5**, measuring **−12.19%**. Do not\nbuild this until a delivery channel exists that does not need agent initiative — which means a\nharness hook, which means `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE`, which alters every future cell.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Struck — do not re-propose\n\nEach was measured and killed. Re-proposing any of these has already cost real money.\n\n1. Eleven payload-quality metrics (completeness, gold coverage, byte width, rank) — all 0.\n2. Retrieval/ranking quality — **anti-correlated**: gold at rank 1 in losers 6/8 vs winners 2/5.\n3. Session-length routing · language/repo gating · confidence gating (abstention worse).\n4. Per-loser repair — +0.0 pt; losers are not stable (93% flip sign).\n5. Locate-call reduction as a token lever — corr +0.188 over 10 cells.\n6. Raising the turn cap — both arms grow proportionally.\n7. `EG_VERIFY_MODE=noworld` — +35.1% **worse**.\n8. Symmetric frugality clamp — modelled net-negative: in the winners it is the *baseline* that\n   flails (lombok baseline `javac`×70 vs eg ×23), so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline.\n9. More graph calls — G3: 1.9 → 3.1 calls, displacement 0.96 → 0.76.\n10. Any new verb in the affordance tail — 0/66 across three attempts.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Gates\n\nMechanism counts only below n≈30. MDE is ~25–30 pts against a ~100-pt same-instance noise floor,\nso **no token claim may be made from a small cell in either direction** — a discipline G3\nviolated in spirit by moving −37.7 → +9.8 → −1.2 across three polls of the same cell.\n\n| gate | what | n | pass criterion |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **H0** | offline, $0 | 0 | shim answers all 409 recorded grep commands; degrades to real grep on non-code patterns; zero regressions on existing verbs |\n| **H1** | replay, $0 | 409 | for each recorded grep, does the shim's answer contain what the agent went on to read next? Target ≥60% |\n| **H2** | live, both arms | 12 | **eliminations per graph call ≥ 3.0** (from 1.10). Mechanism count, not tokens |\n| **H3** | live, both arms | 30 | ≥ 5.0 per call; report Δturns and harness counts |\n| **H4** | full rep50 | 49 | first token claim, with bootstrap CI, total **and** median |\n\n**Kill criteria.** If H1 lands under 40%, R-B does not reach 35% — stop and ship R-A alone.\nIf H2 lands under 2.0, stop entirely: the ratio cannot be moved by tool output, and the honest\nnumber is the fair ceiling in §8.\n\n---\n\n## 8. What is true regardless of which route is taken\n\n- **Fair ceiling from loser repair alone: +21.21%** (exact, = the lift pool).\n- **Best fair cell ever measured: +17.21%** (opus xhigh, n=26).\n- **Current: +1.35%** (n=49), median −4.2%, wins 24/49.\n- R-A alone: projected **+5% to +9%**, fair without qualification.\n- R-A + R-B: projected **+30% to +35%**, but measures a different claim and must be labelled.\n\n**35% is not reachable by any intervention that leaves the agent's exploration behaviour intact**,\nbecause the graph currently removes 8.4% of exploration and 5.4% of harness runs, and adding\ncapability made both worse. The only lever with 35% inside its range is the one that intercepts\nbehaviour the agent already has, rather than offering behaviour it must adopt.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Open defects to fix before the next cell\n\n1. **`EMPTY PAYLOAD` detector is broken** — fires on sessions holding 7,757 payload bytes and\n   3 graph calls. Corrupts any uptake metric computed from it. Seen 3× in G3.\n2. **Gate admits junk patches** — `carbon-2981` baseline patch was **371,308 bytes** (vendor or\n   build output) and passed as success. Add a size/содержание sanity bound, symmetric.\n3. **VMs A and B are spot instances and were preempted**, freezing Gemini 300 at n=207 and\n   leaving 52 rep30 sessions unfinished. Move long cells to non-preemptible (E/F) or checkpoint.\n4. **`.entire/graph-agent.md` fairness landmine** — carries arm-asymmetric methodology and is\n   `@`-included into `CLAUDE.md`. It did *not* leak into rep50 (`--safe-mode`, 0 injected files\n   verified in both arms), but any cell run without `--safe-mode` is invalid on arrival. Add an\n   explicit preflight assertion rather than relying on the flag.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"afe50638-693c-4594-8efa-f47252c1c5d1","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
