e | +2.3% |\n\n> **35% is not reachable on a fair measurement. The honest number is +15% to +21%.**\n\n---\n\n## 5. What to do, in order\n\n### 5.1 SHIP — R-A, zero-toll delivery *(built, gated, committed `b669a57b`)*\n\n`EG_PREFETCH=1` has always injected the payload into turn 0 **and** let the agent's residual\nsearch re-derive it at full price (~5 s + an index build) — delivered twice, paid for once at\nfull price. That is why every prefetch attempt measured null. `EG_PRESEARCH` makes the residual\ncall an echo.\n\nOffline gate passed, $0: echo byte-identical to the prompt block; **0.02 s vs 5.21 s**; missing\npayload errors rather than silently re-querying; real search unaffected when unset; other verbs\nunaffected; export proven eg-only by block matching (line 704, inside `arm = eg` 446–939 and the\n`EG_PREFETCH` gate at 498).\n\nWorth **+4.8% to +6.9%**. Fair without qualification: the eg prompt gains tool output and *loses*\nan instruction. **Next step: run it as a paired cell.**\n\n### 5.2 BUILD — coverage of the agent's *next* query\n\nTarget the 69.1% coverage share of source exploration. The failing queries are specific symbol\nlookups. This is the one pool that is genuinely tool-fixable.\n\nDo **not** measure it with gold-file coverage — that metric is anti-correlated with winning\n(gold at rank 1: losers 6/8, winners 2/5) and eleven payload metrics have died on it. Measure it\nas: *of the identifiers the agent greps for after receiving the payload, what fraction were in\nthe payload?* Today that is **30.9%** (all-terms 15.9% + partial credit). Gate on raising it.\n\nPrice the payload growth against the capture — a wider payload costs persist-tokens too, and at\nmedian 1,124 B the graph's output is not free.\n\n### 5.3 DO NOT BUILD\n\n- **R-B, graph-backed grep** — dead twice: 38.9% interception, and graph output is *larger* than\n  a median grep.\n- **Anything gated on an agent-initiated call** — 0/66 (`impact` 0/49, `changed` 0/5, `--path`\n  0/12). A fourth verb will measure zero.\n- **Whole-file-read suppression** — 42.3% of the remaining cost is the agent re-opening a file it\n  already has in full. Only an instruction fixes that, and that instruction is unfair.\n- The twelve struck levers in §6.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Struck — measured, do not re-propose\n\n1. Eleven payload-quality metrics (completeness, gold coverage, byte width, rank) — all 0.\n2. **Complete-symbol payload density** — corr with token saving **−0.170**; the 3+ group measures\n   **−4.28%** against +1.35% overall. *(Twelfth.)*\n3. Retrieval/ranking quality — anti-correlated (gold at rank 1: losers 6/8 vs winners 2/5).\n4. Session-length routing · language/repo gating · confidence gating (abstention worse).\n5. Per-loser repair — +0.0 pt; ~93% of losers flip sign between cells.\n6. Locate-call reduction as a token lever — corr +0.188 over 10 cells.\n7. Raising the turn cap — both arms grow proportionally.\n8. `EG_VERIFY_MODE=noworld` — +35.1% **worse**.\n9. Symmetric frugality clamp — net-negative: in the winners it is the *baseline* that flails\n   (lombok baseline `javac`×70 vs eg ×23), so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline.\n10. More graph calls — G3: 1.9 → 3.1 calls, displacement 0.96 → 0.76.\n11. Post-edit delivery gated on an agent-initiated call — G2 measured **−12.19%**, rider fired 0/5.\n12. Any new verb in the affordance tail — 0/66.\n\n### 6.1 Open defects — fix before the next cell\n\n1. **`EMPTY PAYLOAD` detector is broken.** Fires on sessions holding 7,757 payload bytes and 3\n   graph calls (3× in G3). It also blanks 36 of 105 whole-file reads (36% of that cost) from\n   classification. Any payload forensics is unreliable until it is fixed.\n2. **Gate admits junk patches** — `carbon-2981` baseline patch was 371,308 B (vendor/build\n   output) and passed as success. Add a symmetric size/content bound.\n3. **VMs A and B are spot and were preempted** — Gemini 300 frozen at n=207, 52 rep30 sessions\n   stranded. Move long cells to non-preemptible (E/F) or checkpoint.\n4. **`.entire/graph-agent.md` fairness landmine** — carries arm-asymmetric methodology and is\n   `@`-included into `CLAUDE.md`. It did *not* leak into rep50 (`--safe-mode`, 0 injected files\n   verified both arms), but any cell run without `--safe-mode` is invalid on arrival. Add a\n   preflight assertion rather than relying on the flag.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Corrections issued this session\n\nRecorded so they are not re-derived, and because two were mine.\n\n1. **eg does NOT verify more than the baseline — it verifies less** (545 vs 576 harness runs;\n   post-edit 384 vs 422). The opposite claim came from the 8 worst *losers*, a set selected on\n   the outcome. Over-verification is what a losing session looks like in either arm; the biggest\n   win, `lombok-3674` (+71.0%), is the *baseline* running `javac` 70× against eg's 23.\n2. **First-word Bash classification undercounted exploration by 41%** (855 → 1,206 baseline).\n   `wc -l f && cat f` and `gem list | grep …` were filed as \"other bash\". Corrected, the graph\n   costs **37 more messages than it removes**, and the saving is entirely context density.\n3. **Perfect loser repair caps at +21.21%**, exactly — it is the sum of the lift pool, not an\n   estimate. Loser repair cannot reach 35% by definition.\n4. The **+35.06%** target was an arithmetic artifact (applying the winner group's eg/base ratio,\n   earned on 2.64M-median baselines, to 0.99M baselines where the waste does not exist).\n   Retracted.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"57691b59-bce3-407a-8686-7081cf3e4b99","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
