{"parentUuid":"5ab7e575-fe2d-473c-87da-7509574cad05","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cdqgac7CMJWFvhcoFQtDX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correlations, ranked — and the strongest signal is **edit churn**, not localization:\n\n| correlate with token saving | r |\n|---|---|\n| Δ harness runs | **−0.612** |\n| Δ exploration eliminated | +0.485 |\n| **Δ repeat edits to same file** | **−0.457** |\n| Δ edit calls | −0.449 |\n| Δ first-edit turn | −0.342 |\n| Δ distinct files edited | −0.264 |\n\n(Δ first-edit *fraction* reads +0.459 but is confounded — winners have fewer total tools, so the denominator shrinks. Discarding it.)\n\nMedians are all ≈0, so this is tail-driven, consistent with everything else. Let me stress-test the design implication before writing it up."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4354,"cache_read_input_tokens":683764,"output_tokens":4633,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4354,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4633,"cache_read_input_tokens":683764,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4354},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqgaRJozg2reg7SHUKZz","type":"assistant","uuid":"a3cf1a3d-c9f2-435e-8ea5-73e4238abdf6","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:34:41.238Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"a3cf1a3d-c9f2-435e-8ea5-73e4238abdf6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cdqgac7CMJWFvhcoFQtDX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ApPhDXDSBaeFbiuqfohUsB","name":"Agent","input":{"subagent_type":"Plan","description":"Stress-test post-edit hook design","prompt":"You are stress-testing a benchmark intervention design for FAIRNESS and REGRESSION RISK. Be adversarial. Caveman-ultra compressed output — dense bullets, no prose padding. Use RTK if you shell out.\n\nCONTEXT (all measured, opus, SWE-bench Multilingual, 49 gated pairs, \"positive = graph arm saved tokens\"):\n\n- Headline +1.35%, t=-0.17 (indistinguishable from zero). Median -4.2%. Winners 24, losers 25.\n- Drag pool -19.86 pts, lift pool +21.21 pts. Top winner lombok-3674 alone = +6.09 pts (29% of ALL lift). Baseline there ran javac 70x vs eg 23x.\n- Loser vs winner behavioural split (sums across group):\n    PRE-edit exploration: losers -46, winners -58  (graph WORKS pre-edit in BOTH)\n    POST-edit exploration: losers +159, winners -81\n    POST-edit harness runs: losers +50, winners -88\n    edits: losers +42, winners -25\n    turns: losers +296, winners -258\n- Graph calls: 94 total, 91 PRE-edit, 3 POST-edit. 42/49 sessions never call it after their first edit.\n- Correlates with token saving: harness runs -0.612, exploration eliminated +0.485, repeat-edits-to-same-file -0.457, edit calls -0.449, first-edit turn -0.342.\n- 97.7% of excess token cost lands after the first edit.\n- Persistence-weighted context cost: exploration 45%, whole-file reads 30%, harness 11%. eg is context-NEUTRAL overall (38.5M vs 38.6M).\n\nALREADY TRIED AND FAILED (do not re-propose):\n- New verbs in the prompt's affordance tail: `impact` 0/49 uses, `changed` 0/5, `--path` 0/12. Total 0/66 adoption.\n- G2 \"post-edit rider\" (append changed-file info to callers/impact output when tree is dirty): measured -12.19% n=10, rider fired 0/5 because sessions make no post-edit graph call.\n- 12 payload-quality metrics, ranking/retrieval quality (gold-at-rank-1 sessions LOST more often), session-length routing, symmetric frugality clamps (modelled net-negative: in WINNERS it is the BASELINE that flails, so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline).\n\nPROPOSED INTERVENTION TO STRESS-TEST:\nA Claude Code POST-EDIT HOOK (PostToolUse on Edit/Write) that fires automatically — no agent initiative required — and injects a static consistency check computed by the code graph: after the agent edits symbol X, report whether call sites of X still match its signature (arity/name/type), which callers exist, and what else references it. It runs NO tests and executes nothing.\n\nRationale: it is the only mechanism that reaches the post-edit phase where 97.7% of cost lives, and it targets the two strongest correlates (harness runs -0.612, repeat edits -0.457) by catching a broken edit statically instead of via a compile-test-fix loop.\n\nKNOWN BLOCKERS:\n- `--safe-mode` (run_3arm.sh:2364) disables ALL customizations including hooks; applied to every arm via ISO_ARGS. `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE=1` (:2391) drops it SYMMETRICALLY from all arms; precedent exists (EG_MCP=1 uses it to give the tool arm an MCP server).\n- Hooks are read from project-local `.claude/settings.json`. That is an untracked file in the checkout, and `collect_patch.sh` scores untracked files via `git add -N && git diff` — so the hook config would land in the GRADED PATCH. Fixable via `.git/info/exclude` but that modifies the scoring path.\n- safe-mode exists because the operator's own config leaked 9.4KB skill + 3.1KB subagent listings into turn-0.\n\nFAIRNESS DOCTRINE (binding):\n- Tool OUTPUT differences legitimate. Arm-asymmetric INSTRUCTIONS are not.\n- Test: \"would this sentence/change help an arm with NO tool?\" If yes -> both arms or neither.\n- A published -35% was withdrawn because the tool arm had 2 extra frugality instructions; removing them moved that cell from -14.9% to +9.1%.\n\nANSWER THESE, each with a verdict and the reasoning:\n1. Is unsolicited post-edit injection FAIR under the stated doctrine? Argue both sides, then commit to a verdict. Consider: it is tool output about the agent's own edit; the baseline has no analogue; but it also arrives without being asked for, unlike every other tool output in this benchmark.\n2. REGRESSION RISK to the winners. Winners win by CONVERGING (post-edit exploration -81, harness -88). Could injecting content after every edit make them do MORE work? What is the mechanism by which this backfires, and how big could it be? Note lombok-3674 alone is 29% of all lift.\n3. Does it actually attack the measured correlates, or is that reasoning circular? Repeat-edits -0.457 and harness -0.612 are correlates of LOSING, not proven causes.\n4. What is the honest expected effect size, given the addressable post-edit surface and that eg is already context-NEUTRAL? Give a number with reasoning.\n5. Is there a BETTER intervention I have not considered that (a) reaches post-edit, (b) needs no agent initiative, (c) is fair, (d) does not require changing patch collection?\n6. Concrete kill criteria and a winner-protection tripwire for a gated rollout.\n\nDo NOT be agreeable. If this design is bad, say so and say why."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4354,"cache_read_input_tokens":683764,"output_tokens":4633,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4354,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":4633,"cache_read_input_tokens":683764,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4354},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqgaRJozg2reg7SHUKZz","type":"assistant","uuid":"6cb0f5f1-49f7-406a-b68c-a0ed234dfb26","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:35:08.363Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"6cb0f5f1-49f7-406a-b68c-a0ed234dfb26","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"b0f46be0-2c71-4d1c-96bc-346a5d0deab4","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ApPhDXDSBaeFbiuqfohUsB","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: a9993175fdd51f084 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'a9993175fdd51f084', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/a9993175fdd51f084.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"adc85cb2-91ec-44f6-b4a4-d3424d5c27c2","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:35:08.479Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"a9993175fdd51f084","description":"Stress-test post-edit hook design","resolvedModel":"claude-opus-5","prompt":"You are stress-testing a benchmark intervention design for FAIRNESS and REGRESSION RISK. Be adversarial. Caveman-ultra compressed output — dense bullets, no prose padding. Use RTK if you shell out.\n\nCONTEXT (all measured, opus, SWE-bench Multilingual, 49 gated pairs, \"positive = graph arm saved tokens\"):\n\n- Headline +1.35%, t=-0.17 (indistinguishable from zero). Median -4.2%. Winners 24, losers 25.\n- Drag pool -19.86 pts, lift pool +21.21 pts. Top winner lombok-3674 alone = +6.09 pts (29% of ALL lift). Baseline there ran javac 70x vs eg 23x.\n- Loser vs winner behavioural split (sums across group):\n    PRE-edit exploration: losers -46, winners -58  (graph WORKS pre-edit in BOTH)\n    POST-edit exploration: losers +159, winners -81\n    POST-edit harness runs: losers +50, winners -88\n    edits: losers +42, winners -25\n    turns: losers +296, winners -258\n- Graph calls: 94 total, 91 PRE-edit, 3 POST-edit. 42/49 sessions never call it after their first edit.\n- Correlates with token saving: harness runs -0.612, exploration eliminated +0.485, repeat-edits-to-same-file -0.457, edit calls -0.449, first-edit turn -0.342.\n- 97.7% of excess token cost lands after the first edit.\n- Persistence-weighted context cost: exploration 45%, whole-file reads 30%, harness 11%. eg is context-NEUTRAL overall (38.5M vs 38.6M).\n\nALREADY TRIED AND FAILED (do not re-propose):\n- New verbs in the prompt's affordance tail: `impact` 0/49 uses, `changed` 0/5, `--path` 0/12. Total 0/66 adoption.\n- G2 \"post-edit rider\" (append changed-file info to callers/impact output when tree is dirty): measured -12.19% n=10, rider fired 0/5 because sessions make no post-edit graph call.\n- 12 payload-quality metrics, ranking/retrieval quality (gold-at-rank-1 sessions LOST more often), session-length routing, symmetric frugality clamps (modelled net-negative: in WINNERS it is the BASELINE that flails, so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline).\n\nPROPOSED INTERVENTION TO STRESS-TEST:\nA Claude Code POST-EDIT HOOK (PostToolUse on Edit/Write) that fires automatically — no agent initiative required — and injects a static consistency check computed by the code graph: after the agent edits symbol X, report whether call sites of X still match its signature (arity/name/type), which callers exist, and what else references it. It runs NO tests and executes nothing.\n\nRationale: it is the only mechanism that reaches the post-edit phase where 97.7% of cost lives, and it targets the two strongest correlates (harness runs -0.612, repeat edits -0.457) by catching a broken edit statically instead of via a compile-test-fix loop.\n\nKNOWN BLOCKERS:\n- `--safe-mode` (run_3arm.sh:2364) disables ALL customizations including hooks; applied to every arm via ISO_ARGS. `CC_NO_SAFE_MODE=1` (:2391) drops it SYMMETRICALLY from all arms; precedent exists (EG_MCP=1 uses it to give the tool arm an MCP server).\n- Hooks are read from project-local `.claude/settings.json`. That is an untracked file in the checkout, and `collect_patch.sh` scores untracked files via `git add -N && git diff` — so the hook config would land in the GRADED PATCH. Fixable via `.git/info/exclude` but that modifies the scoring path.\n- safe-mode exists because the operator's own config leaked 9.4KB skill + 3.1KB subagent listings into turn-0.\n\nFAIRNESS DOCTRINE (binding):\n- Tool OUTPUT differences legitimate. Arm-asymmetric INSTRUCTIONS are not.\n- Test: \"would this sentence/change help an arm with NO tool?\" If yes -> both arms or neither.\n- A published -35% was withdrawn because the tool arm had 2 extra frugality instructions; removing them moved that cell from -14.9% to +9.1%.\n\nANSWER THESE, each with a verdict and the reasoning:\n1. Is unsolicited post-edit injection FAIR under the stated doctrine? Argue both sides, then commit to a verdict. Consider: it is tool output about the agent's own edit; the baseline has no analogue; but it also arrives without being asked for, unlike every other tool output in this benchmark.\n2. REGRESSION RISK to the winners. Winners win by CONVERGING (post-edit exploration -81, harness -88). Could injecting content after every edit make them do MORE work? What is the mechanism by which this backfires, and how big could it be? Note lombok-3674 alone is 29% of all lift.\n3. Does it actually attack the measured correlates, or is that reasoning circular? Repeat-edits -0.457 and harness -0.612 are correlates of LOSING, not proven causes.\n4. What is the honest expected effect size, given the addressable post-edit surface and that eg is already context-NEUTRAL? Give a number with reasoning.\n5. Is there a BETTER intervention I have not considered that (a) reaches post-edit, (b) needs no agent initiative, (c) is fair, (d) does not require changing patch collection?\n6. Concrete kill criteria and a winner-protection tripwire for a gated rollout.\n\nDo NOT be agreeable. If this design is bad, say so and say why.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/a9993175fdd51f084.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6cb0f5f1-49f7-406a-b68c-a0ed234dfb26","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
