{"parentUuid":"5408d9eb-9f1c-460e-afb5-c69c26b11351","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqhvPqBaravcQSxox1rF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_read_input_tokens":736244,"output_tokens":8701,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8701,"cache_read_input_tokens":736244,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqhvGcMeYqP5Q2ew1PFS","type":"assistant","uuid":"effa5fd4-7da6-479b-964c-bbc0580bb807","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:51:53.573Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"effa5fd4-7da6-479b-964c-bbc0580bb807","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqhvPqBaravcQSxox1rF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NzT7ANk4G4X4qYKGUWHudS","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'eg-fix-three-defects',\n  description: 'Design and offline-validate a fix for each of the three measured loss mechanisms on Opus',\n  phases: [\n    { title: 'Design', detail: 'one designer per defect, grounded in source' },\n    { title: 'Validate', detail: 'offline evidence + adversarial refutation per proposal' },\n    { title: 'Spec', detail: 'single implementation-ready synthesis' },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst BASE = `\nREAD-ONLY. Design and offline validation only — do NOT modify any file, do NOT run a benchmark\ncell, do NOT spend model budget on sessions. Produce specs and evidence.\nCaveman-ultra compressed output. Dense bullets, numbers, file:line. Use RTK where it saves tokens.\n\nPROJECT: entire-graph, a local code-graph CLI (Go, tree-sitter). Benchmarked against a no-tool\nbaseline on SWE-bench Multilingual through Claude Code sessions. Metric: tokens.\nSign convention: POSITIVE = graph arm SAVED tokens.\n\nSTATE ON OPUS (rep50, 49 gated pairs, freshly verified):\n  total +1.35%  median -4.16%  10% trimmed -7.65%  wins 24/49  t = -0.17\n  LEAVE-ONE-OUT: drop lombok-3674 -> -5.18%. The positive headline is ONE instance.\n  drag pool -19.86 pts (25 instances), lift pool +21.21 pts (24). lombok-3674 alone +6.09 pts.\n  USER'S ACTUAL GOAL (relaxed from 35%): make the LOSERS behave like median winners. Eliminating\n  the drag pool alone yields +21.21%. A code graph should not make instances worse.\n\nTHE COST MODEL (this governs every design decision):\n  cache_read is 95.5% of billed tokens. A tool result injected at turn i is re-charged on every\n  turn after it, so its cost is (bytes/4) x turns-remaining -- a \"persist-token\".\n  Baseline totals 38.6M persist-tokens; eg 38.5M -- the tool is CONTEXT-NEUTRAL. There is no slack:\n  any byte added is spent from a zero balance.\n  Cost by class (baseline): exploration 45.1% (1,206 calls, 14k each), whole-file Read 30.3%\n  (only 119 calls but 98k each -- 7x anything else), harness 11.2% (576 calls, 7k each).\n  Median sizes: graph payload 1,124 B, grep 732 B, whole-file Read 5,125 B (p90 20,181 B).\n  One graph call prices at 1.61 messages; a regression holding call count fixed charges +7.0%\n  CI[1.9,13.2] for merely having called.\n\nLOSER vs WINNER (group sums, eg minus baseline):\n  pre-edit exploration:  losers -46,  winners -58   <-- the graph WORKS pre-edit in BOTH groups\n  post-edit exploration: losers +159, winners -81\n  post-edit harness:     losers +50,  winners -88\n  harness ENV failures:  losers +22,  winners -45   (corr with saving -0.442)\n  throwaway scripts run: losers +16,  winners -48   (corr -0.350)\n  edits:                 losers +42,  winners -25   (repeat-edits corr -0.457)\n  graph calls: 94 total, 91 PRE-edit, 3 post. 42/49 sessions never call it after their first edit.\n\nFAIRNESS DOCTRINE (binding, no exceptions):\n  Tool OUTPUT differences are legitimate. Arm-asymmetric INSTRUCTIONS are not.\n  Test: \"would this sentence or change help an arm with NO tool?\" If yes -> both arms or neither.\n  A published -35% was withdrawn because the tool arm carried two extra frugality sentences;\n  removing them moved that same cell from -14.9% to +9.1%.\n\nCLOSED — do not propose any of these:\n  new verb advertised in the prompt's affordance tail (0/66 adoption: impact 0/49, changed 0/5,\n  --path 0/12); post-edit rider attached to graph calls (fired 0/5, cell measured -12.19%);\n  better ranking (gold-at-rank-1 sessions LOST more often); bigger payloads; graph-backed grep\n  (graph output median 1,124 B is LARGER than a median grep 732 B; interception ceiling 38.9%);\n  12 payload-quality metrics incl. complete-symbol density (r -0.170); symmetric frugality clamp\n  (net-negative: in WINNERS the BASELINE flails, so a shared clamp hands savings to the baseline);\n  post-edit static-consistency hook (stress-tested: its fair symmetric form's biggest beneficiary\n  is the baseline in lombok-3674 = 29% of all lift, so it eats its own lift source; ~48% of the\n  corpus is dynamically typed where arity checks are unreliable).\n\nNOISE FLOOR: same-instance spread across cells median 99.9pt, max 294pt. 93% of losers flip sign\nbetween cells. MDE ~25-30pt at n=30. n to demonstrate +6% is 499 pairs. NEVER propose a gate that\nreads tokens below n=50; gate on mechanism counts.\n\nPATHS (read-only):\n  tool:    /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph  (internal/sem/search_verify.go, internal/sem/search.go,\n           internal/cli/search.go, internal/cli/def.go, internal/cli/root.go)\n  harness: /Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench (tools/run_3arm.sh, tools/eg-q)\n  ledger:  /Users/suhaan/devenv/graph-bench-results/TokensNotes.md\n  plans:   /Users/suhaan/devenv/EG-FINAL-PLAN.md\n  data on VM (batch your ssh calls, each takes 30-60s):\n    P=graph-benchmarks-504800\n    gcloud compute ssh graphbench-c --project=$P --zone=us-east1-b --tunnel-through-iap --quiet --command='...'\n    results/REP50_r1_{eg,baseline}/<arm>/<iid>/{cc_out.json,patch.diff,gq_calls.log,gq_calls.log.payload}\n    transcripts ~/.claude/projects/*/<session_id>.jsonl   (session_id in cc_out.json)\n`\n\nconst DEFECTS = [\n  {\n    key: 'toll',\n    prompt: `${BASE}\n\nDEFECT 1 — THE CALL IS PAID FOR AND REMOVES NOTHING IN LOSERS.\n  losers:  eg shell exploration 413 vs baseline 415  (TWO calls displaced out of 415)\n  winners: eg 370 vs baseline 440\nThe call costs ~1.61 messages plus a payload that persists in context for the rest of the session.\nIn the losing half that is pure overhead.\n\nALREADY BUILT AND DEPLOYED, evaluate it as the leading candidate:\n  R-A \"zero-toll delivery\" (commit b669a57b). Pre-compute the search payload from the issue text\n  before the session, inject it into turn 0, and set EG_PRESEARCH so any residual search the agent\n  makes is a verbatim echo (measured 0.02s vs 5.21s, byte-identical, no index build).\n  Prior evidence, 178 gated pairs: one call = 1.61 messages; +7.0% CI[1.9,13.2] tax for merely\n  having called; sessions whose call displaced no grep cost ratio 1.165 CI[1.073,1.264] and ran\n  +1.21 turns, versus 0.938 CI[0.889,0.990] and -2.07 turns for calls that did displace.\n  Counterfactual on THIS cell: +4.3% to +6.0%, bootstrap CI spans zero at n=49.\n  Design note in internal/cli/search.go:254 says pre-delivery must be UNIVERSAL because nothing\n  predicts which call will be a no-op (best AUC 0.553 at a 28% base rate).\n\nYOUR JOB:\n1. Verify R-A's mechanism by reading internal/cli/search.go (echoPresearchPayload, the\n   PresearchPath branch) and tools/run_3arm.sh (the EG_PREFETCH block, ~line 482-710) and\n   tools/eg-q (the zero-toll echo in the search branch). Confirm it does what is claimed and find\n   anything that would make it misfire in a real cell.\n2. PRICE THE TURN-0 INJECTION HONESTLY. The payload no longer costs a turn, but it now sits in\n   context from turn 0 instead of turn ~5, so it persists LONGER. Compute the net using the\n   persist model. Does R-A still win once that is charged?\n3. The design note says pre-delivery must be universal. Test that: is there ANY pre-run signal\n   (available before the session, no answer leakage) that predicts a no-op call? If yes, selective\n   delivery beats universal.\n4. State the honest expected effect with an interval, and the cheapest offline evidence that would\n   confirm or kill it before a cell is spent.` },\n\n  {\n    key: 'addtask',\n    prompt: `${BASE}\n\nDEFECT 2 — ON \"ADD\" TASKS THE GRAPH RETURNS A CONFIDENT WRONG ANSWER.\nSearch always returns something. When the task is to ADD behaviour that does not exist yet, the\ntop hit is plausible and wrong, and the agent follows it.\n  pooled MOD (fix existing broken code) +14.35% (n=36) vs ADD -3.37% (n=21); same sign 4/4 cells,\n  ~17.7pt gap, across both models and both prompts.\n  Corroborating: gold at rank 2-3 measures -85.3%, WORSE than gold not found at all (-25.6%).\n  Worst instance in the whole campaign is a pure ADD (fluentd \"add_tag_prefix parameter\", -263.6%).\n\nWHAT IS BLOCKED, and why this is not simply \"abstention\":\n  - Routing on the gold patch is ANSWER LEAKAGE. Forbidden.\n  - A keyword classifier over the problem statement scores 57.5% against a 56% base rate, ADD\n    recall 0.10. Useless.\n  - Confidence / score-gap gating was tried and STRUCK: abstention measured worse. Read\n    TokensNotes.md section 2.8 for exactly what was tried, and do not re-propose that same thing.\n  - BUT TokensNotes.md section 3.1 records an untried idea: \"a structural signal -- does the issue\n    name an existing symbol that resolves in the graph? This is appealing because the graph itself\n    can answer it.\" And section 3.3 asks whether the ADD penalty is avoidable rather than routable.\n\nYOUR JOB:\n1. Read TokensNotes.md sections 1.1, 2.8, 3.1, 3.3 and state precisely what was tried and why it\n   failed, so your proposal is not a rerun.\n2. Build and TEST the structural signal offline, at zero model cost: for each of the 49 rep50\n   instances, extract identifiers from the problem statement, resolve them against that instance's\n   graph symbol table (graphs/<iid>/graph.symbols.ndjson on the VM), and compute a resolve-rate.\n   Then test whether resolve-rate separates MOD from ADD, and whether it predicts the token\n   outcome. Report AUC or an equivalent, and the base rate. BE HONEST if it does not separate.\n3. If the signal works, design what the TOOL does with it — remembering that abstention is struck\n   and that anything the agent must read costs persist-tokens. Consider: does the answer change\n   shape, shrink, or say something different? What is the cheapest expression of \"the thing you\n   describe does not exist here\" that is tool output rather than advice?\n4. Fairness argument in the required form, and the honest expected effect with an interval.\n5. Kill criterion: what offline result would mean this is dead?` },\n\n  {\n    key: 'verify',\n    prompt: `${BASE}\n\nDEFECT 3 — THE VERIFY-REPAIR SPIRAL, POST-EDIT, WHERE THE TOOL IS ABSENT.\n  losers vs baseline: harness ENV failures +22, throwaway scripts run +16, post-edit harness +50,\n  post-edit exploration +159. Winners show the mirror: -45, -48, -88, -81.\n  corr(delta env-failures, saving) = -0.442; corr(delta harness runs, saving) = -0.612.\n  Chain: the verification command fails on the ENVIRONMENT rather than on the patch -> the agent\n  improvises a throwaway harness -> then re-explores to repair that.\n  Ledger section 1.3 (LIVE, not struck): an extra throwaway harness costs +39,527 tok/call,\n  CI [+16.5K, +63.7K], stable to drop-10, negative in 3/4 cells. \"The lever is giving the agent a\n  verification command that actually RUNS, so it does not improvise one. A VERIFY command that\n  fails on the environment rather than the patch sends the agent into 20-34 post-edit repair calls.\"\n\nWHAT EXISTS TODAY:\n  internal/sem/search_verify.go derives a VERIFY command and prints it in the search payload.\n  Its ONLY runnability check is exec.LookPath on the runner (~line 1526) -- it confirms pytest is\n  installed, it never confirms the command RUNS.\n  VERIFY appears in only 8 of 33 logged payloads, so ~76% of sessions get no command at all.\n  internal/cli/verify.go has a full executing verify verb with 6 ecosystem parsers -- but exposing\n  an executing verb changes the claim from \"does a code graph save tokens\" to \"does a bundle of\n  dev tools save tokens\", and an external reviewer already called that out. Treat shipping the\n  executing verb as OUT OF SCOPE; this defect is about the DERIVED COMMAND being correct.\n\nYOUR JOB:\n1. Read internal/sem/search_verify.go end to end. Document how the command is derived, every rung\n   of the fallback ladder, and exactly where it can emit something that will not run.\n2. Design a COLLECT-ONLY PROBE that validates the derived command without running the suite\n   (pytest --collect-only -q, go test -run XXX -count=0, cargo test --no-run, mvn -q -DskipTests\n   compile, phpunit --list-tests, rspec --dry-run). Specify where it hooks in, its timeout, and\n   the fallback when the probe fails. Cite file:line.\n3. Measure the size of the prize offline: on the VM, for the 25 losing instances, count post-edit\n   harness runs whose result text contains an ENVIRONMENT failure (command not found, module not\n   found, no such file, cannot find) rather than a test failure. Then estimate turns removed if\n   those had been prevented, and convert to a token effect via the persist model. Give an interval.\n4. Separately assess raising VERIFY coverage from 8/33 toward 100%: what fraction of instances can\n   have a command derived at all, and does emitting one where none exists today risk making things\n   WORSE (an unrunnable command is worse than no command).\n5. Fairness argument in the required form, plus a kill criterion.` },\n]\n\nconst SPEC = {\n  type: 'object', additionalProperties: false,\n  required: ['defect', 'verdict', 'proposal', 'expected_pct_low', 'expected_pct_high', 'fair', 'kill_criterion'],\n  properties: {\n    defect: { type: 'string' },\n    verdict: { type: 'string', enum: ['build', 'build-with-changes', 'do-not-build'] },\n    proposal: { type: 'string', description: 'Concrete change, with file:line' },\n    offline_evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'What was actually measured, with numbers' },\n    expected_pct_low: { type: 'number' },\n    expected_pct_high: { type: 'number' },\n    fair: { type: 'boolean' },\n    fairness_argument: { type: 'string' },\n    kill_criterion: { type: 'string' },\n    risk_to_winners: { type: 'string' },\n  },\n}\n\nconst VERDICT = {\n  type: 'object', additionalProperties: false,\n  required: ['refuted', 'reason'],\n  properties: {\n    refuted: { type: 'boolean' },\n    reason: { type: 'string' },\n    revised_low: { type: 'number' },\n    revised_high: { type: 'number' },\n  },\n}\n\nconst designed = await pipeline(\n  DEFECTS,\n  (d) => agent(d.prompt, { label: `design:${d.key}`, phase: 'Design', schema: SPEC, effort: 'high' }),\n  (spec, d) => {\n    if (!spec) return null\n    return parallel([\n      () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nPROPOSAL for defect \"${d.key}\":\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n        `REFUTE ON MECHANISM AND COST. eg is CONTEXT-NEUTRAL (38.5M vs 38.6M persist-tokens) -- there is no slack. ` +\n        `Does this add bytes? Price them with the persist model and net them against the claimed benefit. ` +\n        `Does it actually remove TURNS, or only change what is inside a turn? Twelve payload-quality levers died ` +\n        `exactly that way. Default refuted=true unless you can trace which specific calls stop happening.`,\n        { label: `refute-cost:${d.key}`, phase: 'Validate', schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n      () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nPROPOSAL for defect \"${d.key}\":\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n        `REFUTE ON FAIRNESS AND WINNER REGRESSION. Apply the no-tool-arm test literally. Then: winners win by ` +\n        `CONVERGING (post-edit exploration -81, harness -88, turns -258) and lombok-3674 alone is 29% of all lift ` +\n        `(+6.09 of +21.21 pts), where the BASELINE ran javac 70x against eg's 23x. Could this proposal make winners ` +\n        `do MORE work, or help the baseline more than eg? Default refuted=true.`,\n        { label: `refute-fair:${d.key}`, phase: 'Validate', schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n      () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nPROPOSAL for defect \"${d.key}\":\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n        `REFUTE ON MEASURABILITY AND PRIOR ART. Is the claimed effect larger than the ~100pt same-instance noise ` +\n        `floor, and what n would demonstrate it (paired sd is 0.494; +6% needs 499 pairs)? Separately: open ` +\n        `TokensNotes.md and confirm this is not already in the STRUCK list under another name. This project has ` +\n        `retracted a dozen findings by re-proposing a dead lever with new words. Default refuted=true.`,\n        { label: `refute-prior:${d.key}`, phase: 'Validate', schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n    ]).then((votes) => {\n      const live = votes.filter(Boolean)\n      const kills = live.filter((v) => v.refuted).length\n      const lows = live.map((v) => v.revised_low).filter((n) => typeof n === 'number')\n      const highs = live.map((v) => v.revised_high).filter((n) => typeof n === 'number')\n      return {\n        ...spec,\n        survives: live.length > 0 && kills < 2,\n        kills,\n        votes: live.map((v) => ({ refuted: v.refuted, reason: v.reason })),\n        consensus_low: lows.length ? Math.min(...lows) : spec.expected_pct_low,\n        consensus_high: highs.length ? Math.max(...highs) : spec.expected_pct_high,\n      }\n    })\n  },\n)\n\nconst specs = designed.filter(Boolean)\nconst live = specs.filter((s) => s.survives)\nlog(`${live.length}/${specs.length} proposals survived 3-way adversarial refutation`)\n\nphase('Spec')\n\nconst final = await agent(\n  `${BASE}\\n\\n` +\n  `Three defects were each given a designer and then three adversarial refuters (cost/mechanism, ` +\n  `fairness/winner-regression, measurability/prior-art), each defaulting to refuted. A proposal ` +\n  `survives only if fewer than 2 of 3 refuted it.\\n\\n` +\n  `ALL PROPOSALS WITH VERDICTS:\\n${JSON.stringify(specs, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n  `Write the implementation spec. It must contain, for each surviving proposal:\\n` +\n  `  - exact files and functions to change, with file:line\\n` +\n  `  - the change itself, precisely enough to implement without re-deriving it\\n` +\n  `  - the offline ($0) validation that must pass BEFORE any cell is spent\\n` +\n  `  - honest expected effect WITH an interval, and the n required to demonstrate it\\n` +\n  `  - the fairness argument in the required form\\n` +\n  `  - a winner-protection tripwire (lombok-3674, fmt-2457, qutebrowser-c580, redis-11510 are ` +\n  `    included in every sample; abort on >10pt regression per the user's instruction)\\n` +\n  `Then state plainly: summing the surviving proposals, what is the expected result on Opus, and ` +\n  `does it eliminate the -19.86 pt drag pool (the user's actual goal) or not? If the honest answer ` +\n  `is that it does not, say so and say what the residual is. Do not restate anything from the ` +\n  `CLOSED list as if it were new.`,\n  { label: 'implementation-spec', phase: 'Spec', effort: 'high' },\n)\n\nreturn { live_count: live.length, specs, final }\n","description":"Design and validate fixes for the three defects"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_read_input_tokens":736244,"output_tokens":8701,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8701,"cache_read_input_tokens":736244,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1575},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqhvGcMeYqP5Q2ew1PFS","type":"assistant","uuid":"90db8552-0572-4cea-9cce-e022c08aa0f3","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:53:25.553Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
