{"parentUuid":"eb3505dc-e8b8-4a4a-90b1-e07e61978949","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cdqi4Vv2QPsvm74soGid6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"output_tokens":2398,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2398,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdqi4KxzNGmkr2U84P4zV","type":"assistant","uuid":"d7b2a8e5-194f-4227-9883-e3617be4db73","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:53:35.522Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"d7b2a8e5-194f-4227-9883-e3617be4db73","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cdqi4Vv2QPsvm74soGid6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes — two Opus agents are already on exactly that (both asked \"what capability added to or changed in entire-graph would have collapsed those turns\"), plus the fix workflow. But those are loser-specific. Launching one that's forward-looking about the architecture itself, and gathering the facts for your diagram:"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"output_tokens":2398,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2398,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdqi4KxzNGmkr2U84P4zV","type":"assistant","uuid":"af714fc5-1444-4bee-a0c0-a38abb8a99bf","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:53:36.931Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"af714fc5-1444-4bee-a0c0-a38abb8a99bf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cdqi4Vv2QPsvm74soGid6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017zgs2WTkSvwaWYkhwWpUf9","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Find capabilities to add to the graph","model":"opus","prompt":"READ-ONLY architecture review. Do not modify any file. Caveman-ultra compressed output — dense bullets, file:line, numbers. Use RTK where it saves tokens.\n\nTASK: entire-graph is a local, deterministic code-graph CLI (Go + tree-sitter, no network, no model). Read its source and tell me WHAT CAPABILITY IT SHOULD HAVE THAT IT DOES NOT — specifically capabilities that would reduce the number of TURNS a coding agent spends on a SWE-bench-style bug fix.\n\nSOURCE: /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph\n  internal/sem/    parsing, graph construction, search  (provider.go, search.go, search_verify.go, search_related.go, dependents.go)\n  internal/cli/    hand-rolled dispatch (root.go, search.go, def.go, impact.go, neighbors.go, changed.go, verify.go, callsite.go)\n  internal/gitutil/ git subprocess helpers\n  docs/adr/        contracts\n  AGENTS.md        the shipped operating doctrine\n\nSTART HERE, these are load-bearing:\n  `entire-graph capabilities --json` lists semantic vs inventory-only languages, relation types, features.\n  30 relation types exist (CALLS, IMPORTS, EXTENDS, HANDLES_ROUTE, USES_TYPE, PARAM_TYPE,\n  RETURNS_TYPE, DATA_FLOWS, FILE_CHANGES_WITH, CONTAINS, ...).\n\nKNOWN DEAD ENDS — a proposal that reduces to any of these is worthless, say so and move on:\n  - a new VERB advertised in the prompt's affordance tail. Measured adoption 0/66 across three\n    attempts (impact 0/49, changed 0/5, --path 0/12). The agent does not reach for new verbs.\n  - anything requiring a graph call AFTER the first edit: 42/49 sessions make none.\n  - better ranking: sessions where the gold file ranked FIRST lost more often than sessions where\n    it was missed entirely. Retrieval quality and token cost are different axes.\n  - bigger or richer payloads: the tool is already CONTEXT-NEUTRAL (38.5M vs 38.6M persist-weighted\n    tokens vs the no-tool baseline). Every added byte is spent from a zero balance. Twelve\n    payload-quality metrics have been measured and all are dead, including complete-symbol density\n    (correlates -0.170 with token saving).\n\nTHE COST MODEL you must price against:\n  cache_read is 95.5% of billed tokens. A tool result injected at turn i is re-charged on every\n  later turn, so cost ~= (bytes/4) x turns-remaining (\"persist-tokens\").\n  Baseline session cost by class: exploration 45.1% (1,206 calls x 14k), whole-file Read 30.3%\n  (only 119 calls but 98k EACH - 7x anything else), harness runs 11.2% (576 x 7k).\n  Median sizes: graph payload 1,124 B, grep 732 B, whole-file Read 5,125 B (p90 20,181 B).\n  A graph call prices at 1.61 messages.\n  => The single most valuable thing a capability can do is PREVENT A WHOLE-FILE READ or REMOVE A\n     TURN. Adding bytes to prevent nothing is negative.\n\nCORPUS: SWE-bench Multilingual. ~48% dynamically typed (Ruby 39, PHP 32, JS 23, TS 8) vs typed\n(Java 31, Rust 31, Go 27, C 22). Any capability relying on static types works on ~half at best.\n\nDELIVER:\n1. A CAPABILITY INVENTORY: what the graph computes today but never surfaces to the agent, or\n   surfaces only behind an unset flag / a verb nobody calls. The project has repeatedly found\n   capabilities that shipped and were never wired up — find the rest. file:line for each.\n2. For each of the 30 relation types: is it populated (roughly how many edges on a real repo), and\n   is it reachable by the agent? Flag any that are computed and thrown away. Known example to\n   verify: TESTS reportedly has ~10 edges out of ~36,000 and is name-convention only.\n3. THE MAIN ASK — 3 to 6 NEW capabilities, ranked by (turns removed) / (bytes added). For each:\n     - the question the agent is actually trying to answer when it burns turns\n     - the exact command and output shape\n     - why the graph can answer it and grep cannot\n     - which of the dead ends above it avoids, and how it reaches the agent WITHOUT being a new\n       verb in the affordance tail\n     - honest byte cost and turn saving\n4. Anything in the DEFAULT behaviour that is actively harmful given the cost model — e.g. defaults\n   that inflate payload size, or `search --profile fast` reportedly excluding TESTS/SIMILAR_TO/all\n   type relations/DATA_FLOWS/FILE_CHANGES_WITH so those blocks come back structurally empty.\n5. Be blunt: if the honest conclusion is that no new capability clears the bar because the tool is\n   context-neutral and the agent will not call new things, say that and explain what that implies\n   about the product."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"output_tokens":2398,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2398,"cache_read_input_tokens":737819,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9568,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9568},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cdqi4KxzNGmkr2U84P4zV","type":"assistant","uuid":"8532517d-34e4-4047-8730-3cfdbe02c8f2","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:54:01.611Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"8532517d-34e4-4047-8730-3cfdbe02c8f2","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"46bfb63d-25dc-4661-aa22-95b5a836ced8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017zgs2WTkSvwaWYkhwWpUf9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Async agent launched successfully. (This tool result is internal metadata — never quote or paste any part of it, including the agentId below, into a user-facing reply.)\nagentId: ad5c11381e8da2811 (internal ID - do not mention to user. Use SendMessage with to: 'ad5c11381e8da2811', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent.)\nThe agent is working in the background. You will be notified automatically when it completes. You know nothing about its results until that notification arrives — do not report, assume, or predict them; continue other work or respond to the user in the meantime.\nDo not duplicate this agent's work — avoid working with the same files or topics it is using.\noutput_file: /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/ad5c11381e8da2811.output\nDo NOT Read or tail this file via the shell tool — it is the full subagent JSONL transcript and reading it will overflow your context. If the user asks for progress, say the agent is still running; you'll get a completion notification."}]}]},"uuid":"ca830d66-a1be-40c4-8ef1-f6a2dad57692","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:54:01.689Z","toolUseResult":{"isAsync":true,"status":"async_launched","agentId":"ad5c11381e8da2811","description":"Find capabilities to add to the graph","resolvedModel":"claude-opus-5","prompt":"READ-ONLY architecture review. Do not modify any file. Caveman-ultra compressed output — dense bullets, file:line, numbers. Use RTK where it saves tokens.\n\nTASK: entire-graph is a local, deterministic code-graph CLI (Go + tree-sitter, no network, no model). Read its source and tell me WHAT CAPABILITY IT SHOULD HAVE THAT IT DOES NOT — specifically capabilities that would reduce the number of TURNS a coding agent spends on a SWE-bench-style bug fix.\n\nSOURCE: /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph\n  internal/sem/    parsing, graph construction, search  (provider.go, search.go, search_verify.go, search_related.go, dependents.go)\n  internal/cli/    hand-rolled dispatch (root.go, search.go, def.go, impact.go, neighbors.go, changed.go, verify.go, callsite.go)\n  internal/gitutil/ git subprocess helpers\n  docs/adr/        contracts\n  AGENTS.md        the shipped operating doctrine\n\nSTART HERE, these are load-bearing:\n  `entire-graph capabilities --json` lists semantic vs inventory-only languages, relation types, features.\n  30 relation types exist (CALLS, IMPORTS, EXTENDS, HANDLES_ROUTE, USES_TYPE, PARAM_TYPE,\n  RETURNS_TYPE, DATA_FLOWS, FILE_CHANGES_WITH, CONTAINS, ...).\n\nKNOWN DEAD ENDS — a proposal that reduces to any of these is worthless, say so and move on:\n  - a new VERB advertised in the prompt's affordance tail. Measured adoption 0/66 across three\n    attempts (impact 0/49, changed 0/5, --path 0/12). The agent does not reach for new verbs.\n  - anything requiring a graph call AFTER the first edit: 42/49 sessions make none.\n  - better ranking: sessions where the gold file ranked FIRST lost more often than sessions where\n    it was missed entirely. Retrieval quality and token cost are different axes.\n  - bigger or richer payloads: the tool is already CONTEXT-NEUTRAL (38.5M vs 38.6M persist-weighted\n    tokens vs the no-tool baseline). Every added byte is spent from a zero balance. Twelve\n    payload-quality metrics have been measured and all are dead, including complete-symbol density\n    (correlates -0.170 with token saving).\n\nTHE COST MODEL you must price against:\n  cache_read is 95.5% of billed tokens. A tool result injected at turn i is re-charged on every\n  later turn, so cost ~= (bytes/4) x turns-remaining (\"persist-tokens\").\n  Baseline session cost by class: exploration 45.1% (1,206 calls x 14k), whole-file Read 30.3%\n  (only 119 calls but 98k EACH - 7x anything else), harness runs 11.2% (576 x 7k).\n  Median sizes: graph payload 1,124 B, grep 732 B, whole-file Read 5,125 B (p90 20,181 B).\n  A graph call prices at 1.61 messages.\n  => The single most valuable thing a capability can do is PREVENT A WHOLE-FILE READ or REMOVE A\n     TURN. Adding bytes to prevent nothing is negative.\n\nCORPUS: SWE-bench Multilingual. ~48% dynamically typed (Ruby 39, PHP 32, JS 23, TS 8) vs typed\n(Java 31, Rust 31, Go 27, C 22). Any capability relying on static types works on ~half at best.\n\nDELIVER:\n1. A CAPABILITY INVENTORY: what the graph computes today but never surfaces to the agent, or\n   surfaces only behind an unset flag / a verb nobody calls. The project has repeatedly found\n   capabilities that shipped and were never wired up — find the rest. file:line for each.\n2. For each of the 30 relation types: is it populated (roughly how many edges on a real repo), and\n   is it reachable by the agent? Flag any that are computed and thrown away. Known example to\n   verify: TESTS reportedly has ~10 edges out of ~36,000 and is name-convention only.\n3. THE MAIN ASK — 3 to 6 NEW capabilities, ranked by (turns removed) / (bytes added). For each:\n     - the question the agent is actually trying to answer when it burns turns\n     - the exact command and output shape\n     - why the graph can answer it and grep cannot\n     - which of the dead ends above it avoids, and how it reaches the agent WITHOUT being a new\n       verb in the affordance tail\n     - honest byte cost and turn saving\n4. Anything in the DEFAULT behaviour that is actively harmful given the cost model — e.g. defaults\n   that inflate payload size, or `search --profile fast` reportedly excluding TESTS/SIMILAR_TO/all\n   type relations/DATA_FLOWS/FILE_CHANGES_WITH so those blocks come back structurally empty.\n5. Be blunt: if the honest conclusion is that no new capability clears the bar because the tool is\n   context-neutral and the agent will not call new things, say that and explain what that implies\n   about the product.","outputFile":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/ad5c11381e8da2811.output","canReadOutputFile":true},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8532517d-34e4-4047-8730-3cfdbe02c8f2","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
