{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-08-08T17:58:21.748Z","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a833340527d7617c5</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01LeVWSqPm6XBdu6Ps36SzSX</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/a833340527d7617c5.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Investigate bat-1892 and valkey-790 losses\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I have everything. Here is the forensic account.\n\n## Bottom line up front\n\nThe graph payload was **correct in both instances** — top hit was the exact function to edit, both times. Neither loss was caused by the graph. But the two instances differ in whether a graph capability *could* have helped:\n\n- **bat-1892**: graph is innocent **and near-powerless**. 42 of eg's 99 tool calls are environment/answer-leak/hung-test work no code graph can address.\n- **valkey-790**: graph is innocent but **not powerless**. ~31 of the 44 harness calls are *pre-edit* and answer a repo-derivable question.\n\nCell-wide (49 paired), turn delta is the entire mechanism: winners median **−8 turns**, losers median **+9**. My two are the extremes, and both show the same signature — eg wall-clock ≈2.7× baseline (3258s vs 1192s; 3517s vs 1290s) because **eg actually built and ran the real test suite and baseline did not**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. sharkdp__bat-1892 — ADD\n\n**Task**: add a `--ignored-suffix` CLI option so users can extend the hardcoded `IGNORED_SUFFIXES` list. **ADD** (new flag, new type, new wiring across 8 files).\n\n**Graph payload — correct.** Top hit was the exact function to replace:\n```\n1. src/assets/ignored_suffixes.rs:28-42 score=19.38 symbol=try_with_stripped_suffix\n2. src/assets.rs:280-288 HighlightingAssets.get_syntax_for_file_name\n3. src/assets.rs:290-301 HighlightingAssets.get_syntax_for_file_extension\nSAME-CONCEPT LITERAL \"IGNORED_SUFFIXES\" — 2 in 1 file: src/assets/ignored_suffixes.rs:6 EDIT\n```\neg followed it directly (calls 3–4 read exactly those two files) and used **5 fewer** localization calls than baseline (10 vs 15). The graph *won* the localization phase and then lost the session elsewhere.\n\n**Where the +35 calls went** (eg 99 vs base 64):\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| sibling-checkout answer-leak mining | 12 | 0 | **+12** |\n| hung-test recovery | 11 | 0 | **+11** |\n| real build runs | 6 | 0 | +6 |\n| env/toolchain discovery | 8 | 5 | +3 |\n| verification | 10 | 7 | +3 |\n| companion doc/completion sites | 10 | 7 | +3 |\n| harness building | 11 | 9 | +2 |\n| graph call | 1 | 0 | +1 |\n| edits | 18 | 19 | −1 |\n| **localization** | **10** | **15** | **−5** |\n\nThree things drove it, none graph-related:\n\n- **Answer leak (12 calls).** Call 9 was a blocked `curl` to `raw.githubusercontent.com` for the upstream file. Call 10 then listed the shared checkouts dir and found **`sharkdp__bat-3108`** — a *later* checkout of the same repo that already contains the implemented feature (`src/syntax_mapping/ignored_suffixes.rs`, `IgnoredSuffixes&lt;'a&gt;`). Calls 11–18, 20, 51, 81 mine it for the reference implementation. Baseline never listed that directory.\n- **Toolchain discovery asymmetry (decisive).** Call 34 (`env | grep -i cargo`) found `CARGO_HOME=.../pkg_iso/cargo` with a populated offline registry. That unlocked ~40 calls of real `cargo build` / `cargo test`. **Baseline had a *larger* registry (198M/147 crates vs eg's 77M/132) and never found it** — it concluded at T069 \"no crate registry cache offline\" (false), wrote a standalone `rustc` mock (`/tmp/repro/main.rs`) that re-implemented the logic, and **never compiled the bat crate at all**.\n- **Hung pty test (11 calls).** `cargo test` blocked forever on `no_args_doesnt_break` (uses `openpty`). Calls 82–95 are sleeps, `pgrep`, `ps -g`, `kill`, a 10-minute timeout, and source-diving to identify the hanging test, ending in `--skip no_args_doesnt_break`.\n\n**Where a graph capability would have removed turns**: essentially one place. Calls 21, 25–30, 50, 52, 80 (**10 calls**) all ask *\"where does the analogous existing flag `--map-syntax` appear, so I can mirror `--ignored-suffix` there?\"* — man page, 4 completion files, clap_app, app.rs, CHANGELOG, README. Nothing else in the session is graph-shaped.\n\n**Caused by the payload?** No. Evidence against causation: the payload named 2 files, eg read exactly those 2, and finished localization in **fewer** calls than baseline. The leak was triggered by a blocked `curl`, not by the payload. The build/hang costs began 20+ calls after the last payload-derived read.\n\n---\n\n## 2. valkey-io__valkey-790 — MOD\n\n**Task**: `CLUSTER SHARDS` returns an empty `slots` array for a shard whose primary has failed. **MOD** — note this contradicts the pooled MOD +14.35% trend; a MOD lost 127%.\n\n**Graph payload — outstanding.** Top-2 hits were the command entry point and the buggy function, including the exact defective lines:\n```\n1. src/cluster_legacy.c:5698-5707 score=84.25 clusterCommandShards  signals=...,exact-code-token,all-query-terms\n2. src/cluster_legacy.c:5666-5693 score=83.05 addShardReplyForClusterShards\n     n = clusterNodeGetPrimary(n);              &lt;-- the bug\n     if (n-&gt;slot_info_pairs != NULL) { ... } else { addReplyArrayLen(c, 0); }   &lt;-- the empty array\n```\nThat is the patched hunk verbatim. Both arms' final patches modify precisely these lines.\n\n**Where the +39 calls went** (eg 92 vs base 53):\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| test-harness wrangling + hand-rolled repro | 48 | 23 | **+25** |\n| verification / regression suites | 17 | 7 | **+10** |\n| blocked commands | 2 | 0 | +2 |\n| style (clang-format) | 3 | 1 | +2 |\n| edits | 2 | 1 | +1 |\n| graph call | 1 | 0 | +1 |\n| build | 3 | 3 | 0 |\n| **localization** | **15** | **17** | **−2** |\n\nAgain the graph *saved* localization. The loss is one specific detour:\n\nBoth arms hit the same wall — `./runtest` fails because the generated `unixsocket` path under the long checkout dir exceeds the 107-byte `sun_path` limit. **Baseline solved it in one clever call** (#34: build a symlink farm at `/tmp/vk` with a real `tests/tmp`), then ran the project's own tcl suite for the rest of the session. **eg tried a symlink (#31), reverted it (#33), abandoned `runtest` entirely, and hand-rolled a 6-node cluster in bash** (`/tmp/w/79eb3c7e/repro.sh`, calls 35–50: port collisions, backgrounded 300s timeouts, `pkill` loops, and finally discovering it needed `--cluster-replica-validity-factor 0` for failover to fire). Then at calls 60–65 it **came back to `runtest` anyway** and solved the same path problem a second way (patching `tests/support/tmpfile.tcl` `::tmproot`). It solved one environment problem twice, with a 16-call detour in between.\n\n**Where a graph capability would have removed turns** — and critically, **all of this is pre-edit** (first edit = call 53), so it is reachable:\n\n| # | What it was trying to learn | What it did | One-turn answer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A | Which existing tests exercise `CLUSTER SHARDS`, and how are they invoked | calls 4, 5, 6, 9, 10, 11 (**6 calls**) | symbol→test-file map + the literal `./runtest --single unit/cluster/&lt;x&gt;` / `./runtest-cluster --single 28-cluster-shards` invocations |\n| B | How do I write a cluster test here — `start_cluster`, `R`, `wait_for_cluster_state`, `default_replica_allocation` | calls 18, 19, 20, **21, 22, 23** (three consecutive failed greps for `proc R`), 24 (**7 calls**) | tcl `proc` definition sites: `start_cluster tests/support/cluster_util.tcl:238`, `R tests/instances.tcl:547`, … |\n| C | What the failover-marking test pattern looks like | call 15, plus base's 13–15 | test-file exemplars for the symbol |\n\n**≈13–15 calls**, all answerable from repo-derived facts. Calls 21/22/23 are the sharpest indictment: three greps failed to find `proc R` because entire-graph indexes no `.tcl` symbols — baseline burned a 4th call on the identical miss.\n\nThe remaining ~25 harness calls (socket path length, port collisions, `client-default-*` config errors, process cleanup) are pure environment and **no code graph can touch them**.\n\n**Caused by the payload?** No. The payload was consumed in 2 calls and eg reached the correct edit having used *fewer* localization calls than baseline. The detour started at call 26, twenty-four calls after the payload, and was triggered by a `runtest` port collision.\n\n---\n\n## Proposals, ranked by (turns removed) / (bytes added)\n\nHard delivery constraint I worked under: **both sessions made exactly one graph call, pre-edit, and never returned.** So the only channel that reaches anyone is the *first `entire graph search` response*. Anything pulled — new verbs, post-edit riders — is dead on arrival, consistent with the 0/66 adoption you already measured.\n\n**1. Test-invocation card, pushed into the first search response — ~14 turns / ~500 B (0.028 turns/byte).**\nFor the top hit's symbol, emit repo-derived facts only:\n```\nTESTS REFERENCING clusterCommandShards / addShardReplyForClusterShards:\n  tests/unit/cluster/announce-client-ip.tcl:23   (start_cluster)\n  tests/cluster/tests/28-cluster-shards.tcl:1    (legacy harness)\nRUN: ./runtest --single unit/cluster/&lt;name&gt;  |  ./runtest-cluster --single 28-cluster-shards\nHELPERS: start_cluster tests/support/cluster_util.tcl:238 · R tests/instances.tcl:547 ·\n         wait_for_cluster_state tests/support/cluster_util.tcl:151\n```\nEvery line is a file:line fact. Fairness test passes — a no-tool arm cannot obtain it without spending exactly the turns being removed (baseline spent 7 on the same questions). Not a new verb; not post-edit.\n\n**2. Index non-code symbols (tcl `proc`, shell functions, make targets) — ~4 turns / ~0 B.**\nAdds no payload bytes; it is what makes #1 derivable, and it directly kills the `proc R` triple-miss. Should ship folded into #1, not standalone — standalone it only pays on a second call that 42/49 sessions never make.\n\n**3. Companion-site list for ADD tasks — ~8 turns / ~400 B (0.020 turns/byte), low confidence.**\nWhen the top hit sits in a feature cluster, list every site where the nearest sibling option is declared, including non-source files (man page, 4 completion files, changelog). Would have collapsed bat's 10 doc-site calls. **I rate this low confidence** because I cannot see a clean mechanism for the tool to know that `--map-syntax` is the analogue of `--ignored-suffix` from the query \"ignore known backup template filename suffix\". The existing literal sweep does not get there — it correctly reports `IGNORED_SUFFIXES` appears in one file, which is true and useless.\n\n**Nothing else in the graph's power helps these two.** 42/99 bat calls (leak 12, hung test 11, env 8, builds 6, dead ends 2, harness 3) and ~25/92 valkey calls are environment and toolchain, not code intelligence.\n\n---\n\n## Two benchmark-integrity findings that should change decisions\n\n- **Answer leak is live.** Concurrent instance checkouts sit in a shared parent directory, and `sharkdp__bat-3108` is a *later revision of the same repo containing the implemented feature*. eg found it and spent 12 calls mining it. Whichever arm happens to run `ls &lt;checkouts-parent&gt;` gets the upstream solution. The checkouts have since been cleaned, so I could not confirm whether baseline's parent held it at run time — but the exposure is structural, not arm-specific.\n- **The token metric is rewarding failure to provision.** On bat, baseline \"won\" 5.13M vs 9.23M in large part because it never compiled the crate — it verified a re-implementation of the logic in a standalone `rustc` file while a fully usable 198M offline registry sat unfound at `$CARGO_HOME`. Provisioning logged `'cargo fetch' exit=127` for both arms, so no arm was told the toolchain existed. Any pair where one arm builds and the other does not is measuring provisioning luck, not retrieval. Worth auditing how many of the 25 losers share this shape — the four largest all show eg wall-clock ≫ baseline, which is the same signature.\n\n**Files** (on `graphbench-c`): payloads `~/work/graphmark/agentic-swebench/results/REP50_r1_eg/eg/{sharkdp__bat-1892,valkey-io__valkey-790}/gq_calls.log.payload`; transcripts `~/.claude/projects/*/{aa948100-b9a6-4692-a84e-d90048dcf195,06b94d0f-5f62-4666-b2d0-f831ef8b8f7f,aedc5159-3563-4b94-a8ec-faca4ac3d4c0,0240f4fa-3a7d-448f-8661-c1d9b87e0bde}.jsonl`. Local extracts and per-call bucketing: `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49/scratchpad/tc_{BAT_EG,BAT_BASE,VK_EG,VK_BASE}.txt`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>184092</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>27</tool_uses><duration_ms>585235</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
