{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"remove","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:02:19.775Z","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac8e7cc6a691f2030</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WsyNbjjx3TLJv1wjUpJugH</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/ac8e7cc6a691f2030.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Investigate ansible and prometheus losses\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Killed the stray job — I have everything I need.\n\n## Both instances are ADD tasks. That is the structural story.\n\n| | ansible PlayIterator | prometheus 9248 |\n|---|---|---|\n| task | introduce `IteratingStates`/`FailedStates` enums + `__getattr__` back-comnpat shim | make `atan2` a first-class binary operator |\n| MOD or ADD | **ADD** | **ADD** (eg's own repro: `parse error: unexpected identifier \"atan2\"` — it isn't even lexed) |\n| eg / base calls | 115 / 90 (+25) | 109 / 67 (+42) |\n| **pre-edit** | **37 / 17 (+20)** | **54 / 27 (+27)** |\n| post-edit | 78 / 73 (+5) | 55 / 40 (+15) |\n| graph calls | 1 (`search`), pre-edit | 1 (`grep`), pre-edit |\n\n**80% and 64% of the surplus is PRE-EDIT** — the opposite of the corpus-level \"97.7% of loss is post-edit\". These two losers bleed exactly where the graph operates, so it does not get the \"absent at the scene\" defence.\n\nWhy ADD matters mechanically: a code graph indexes *what exists*. On a MOD task rank-1 is the edit site. On an ADD task the best possible hit is a *sibling* of the answer (`POW`/`MOD` for atan2; `ITERATING_*` for `IteratingStates`). The real work is a **completeness** question — every place the new member must be threaded — not a **location** question. The graph answers the question these tasks don't ask.\n\n## Instance 1: prometheus-9248 — the graph was actively worse than grep on move 1\n\n`gq_calls.log`: **`grep atan2|ATAN2`** — one call, no `search`.\n\nSame query both arms, opening move:\n\n- **base** `grep -rn \"atan2\\|ATAN2\" --include=*.go --include=*.y --include=*.test .` → **31 B: `(Bash completed with no output)`**\n- **eg** `entire graph grep \"atan2|ATAN2\"` → **165 B: `web/ui/static/vendor/rickshaw/vendor/d3.v3.js:1 | d3=function(){function n(n){return null!=n&amp;&amp;!isNaN(n)}…(1 matches)`**\n\n`entire graph grep` has no vendor/minified/include filtering, so it manufactured a false positive where plain grep returned a clean zero. Clean-zero is an actionable signal; \"1 hit in minified vendored JS\" is not.\n\n**Consequence, turn by turn.** Baseline's call #2 pivoted to the *error string from the issue text* — `grep -rn \"not allowed for Scalar operations\"` → `promql/engine.go:2089` — pinning `scalarBinop` (a gold edit site) at **call 2**. eg never grepped the error string; it spent calls 3,5,6,7,8 re-confirming atan2's absence (call 7 re-derived the graph's own d3.js hit) and only reached `scalarBinop`/`vectorElemBinop` at **calls 21–22**. **~19 turns of delay, seeded by the tool's opening answer.**\n\nBucketed surplus (+42):\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| codemirror/web-UI excursion | 25 | 5 | **+20** | eg made 8 edits there; base made **0** |\n| env / goyacc toolchain hunt | 18 | 5 | +13 | incl. auditing 3 stale `goyacc` binaries from **other sessions'** `/tmp` |\n| redundant \"does atan2 exist\" | 6 | 3 | +3 | |\n| reads | 4 | 4 | +0 | but eg Read `generated_parser.y` **unbounded (28,762 B)** vs base `lim=270` (9,045 B) |\n| repro harness | 15 | 8 | +7 | eg built it in `/tmp` behind a separate `go.mod` → `too many arguments in call to engine.NewInstantQuery` → recovery turn |\n| dead ends | 2 | 1 | +1 | `cp: cannot stat promql/parser/generated_parser.y` (wrong cwd) |\n\neg's patch touched 5 extra files, all `web/ui/module/codemirror-promql/**`. It discovered `web/ui/node_modules` does not exist and `npm` is netjailed at call ~97 — **after** all 8 edits. **The eg harness already knew this**: `gq_calls.log.probe` contains `stripped: cd web/ui/module/codemirror-promql &amp;&amp; npx mocha src/parser/parser.test.ts`. The tool probed that subtree, found it unverifiable, and threw the fact away.\n\n## Instance 2: ansible PlayIterator — gold at rank 1, still lost 25 turns\n\nPayload was **good**: hit #1 `lib/ansible/executor/play_iterator.py:128-133` (the exact constant block being replaced, `signals=…exact-symbol,graph:callers`), hit #4 `HostState.__init__`, hit #5 `linear.py:82-198 _get_next_task_lockstep`. Two of three gold files in the top five. It did not point anywhere wrong.\n\nAnd it saved nothing: eg then Read `play_iterator.py` **unbounded (28,576 B)** — byte-identical to what baseline read at its call #2 with no tool at all. Both patches touch the **same 4 files**. The +25 turns bought zero extra scope.\n\nWhere they went:\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| repro/import wrangling (py3.12 `find_module`→`find_spec` break in ansible's collection loader) | 12 pre-edit + shim rewritten again at call ~84 | 8, post-edit | +6 |\n| pytest acquisition | 3 `pip install` into `.venv` (pytest, pycodestyle/pyflakes, mock) | 0 — used `~/gfyvenv/bin/python`, already had pytest 9.1.1 | +3 |\n| deprecation-convention hunt (read the pylint sanity plugin `test/lib/…/pylint/plugins/deprecated.py` in 2 chunks + revisit) | 6 | 3 (one `grep -n \"def deprecated\" -A 40 lib/ansible/utils/display.py`) | +3 |\n| extra localization reads (`test_linear.py` full, extra grep rounds) | 25 | 12 | +13 |\n| repo edits | 30 | 35 | −5 |\n\neg saw `/home/suhaan_entire_io/gfyvenv/bin/pytest → pytest 9.1.1` at call ~29 and did not use it. That is model variance, not tool behaviour.\n\n## Q5 — is the graph guilty?\n\n**prometheus: guilty, and I can name the mechanism.** Not \"it sent the agent to the wrong file\" — baseline saw the same d3.js hit at its call #3 and ignored it. The mechanism is **the tool consumed the first-search slot and returned a signal too weak to force a pivot**. Baseline's empty result forced the error-string pivot on move 2; eg's noisy one-hit result invited five more confirmation greps. Tool output that is strictly noisier than the no-tool equivalent on the same query is a defect, and it is the tool's *only* contribution to this session.\n\n**ansible: innocent.** Correct payload, rank 1, right files. The +25 turns are environment and harness work no code graph can see. Say it plainly.\n\n## Q6 — can static analysis work on dynamic repos here?\n\nSharply: **the payload worked; the follow-up verbs cannot.**\n\n- Retrieval on Python worked well (ansible payload used real `graph:callers` edges and nailed rank 1).\n- But the dominant operation in *both* tasks is find-all-references over an enumerated constant family. In ansible those references are `self.ITERATING_X`, `PlayIterator.ITERATING_X` and `iterator.ITERATING_X` where `iterator` is an **untyped parameter**. No static index resolves that binding; `impact`/`callers` would fall back to name matching — which is grep, at 1,124 B median vs grep's 732 B. Strictly worse.\n- The task's own solution is `__getattr__`-based dynamic dispatch — invisible to any static index by construction.\n- In prometheus, **6 of eg's 10 edited files** are a yacc grammar, a lezer grammar, and `tokens.js` — formats the graph does not parse at all. The single most important file in the task is invisible to it.\n\nSo for ~48% of this corpus, the honest position is: retrieval is fine, relations are unreliable, and the graph's edge over grep has to come from facts grep cannot compute — not from reference resolution.\n\n## Proposals, ranked by (turns removed)/(bytes added)\n\nAll are payload **fields on existing `search`/`grep` output** — not new verbs (0/66 adoption), not riders, not reranking, not bigger payloads.\n\n| # | capability | turns removed | bytes | ratio | conf |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **P0** | **Vendor/minified/generated exclusion in `graph grep`.** Make a no-real-hits query read as no real hits. | prom 3 | **−134** | ∞ | high |\n| **P1** | **Probe-result surfacing.** eg's VERIFY probe already computes, at index time, which subtrees have a runnable check, then discards it. Emit it as observation, not advice: `web/ui/module/codemirror-promql: probe 'npx mocha …' exit 127 (no node_modules)`. | prom ~8, ans ~3 | ~180 | 1/16 B | **low** |\n| **P2** | **Generated-artifact provenance edge.** Parse the standard `// Code generated by X … DO NOT EDIT.` header, resolve the generator to its module+version from `go.sum`/lockfile: `generated_parser.y.go GENERATED_FROM generated_parser.y BY golang.org/x/tools/cmd/goyacc@v0.1.5`. | prom ~5 | ~120 | 1/24 B | high |\n| **P3** | **Error-literal → emitting-symbol index.** Key on quoted strings in the problem statement, normalise `%q`/`%s`/`{}`. `operator %q not allowed for Scalar operations` → `engine.go:2089 scalarBinop`, `engine.go:2429 vectorElemBinop`. **Already built, shipped OFF** — this cell ran `EG_LITERALS=\"0\"`. | prom ~6 | ~270 (fires only on quoted literals) | 1/45 B | med |\n| **P4** | **Enumerated-family closure.** Seeded on a member of a token/const/map family, return every co-member's file:line. Seeded on `POW`/`MOD` this returns the *entire* prometheus edit set across `.y`, `lex.go`, `engine.go`, `.grammar`, `.terms.ts`, `operators.md`. Answers the completeness question ADD tasks actually ask. | prom ~5, ans ~1 | ~750 | 1/125 B | med |\n\nFairness check on each: none is a sentence of advice, all are computed repo facts a no-tool arm can only obtain by spending the turns. P1 is the one to watch — it must be phrased as a probe result (`exit 127`), never as \"don't edit there\", or it fails the test.\n\n## The bottom line, and two things you should not miss\n\n**Recoverable: prometheus ~27 of 42 surplus turns. Ansible ~6 of 25.** Ansible is essentially unrecoverable by anything in the graph's power — it is an environment loss wearing a tool's name. If the corpus's 25 losers look like ansible more than like prometheus, no graph capability closes them and you are optimising the wrong layer.\n\n**P3 is already built and switched off.** The single capability that would have collapsed prometheus's biggest delay is `EG_LITERALS`, disabled in this cell. Before building anything new, re-run these two instances with it on. Caveat worth stating: memory has literals adjacent to a reverted IDF-weighting regression (45→38%) — but that was a *ranking* change, and this is an additional retrieval channel keyed on quoted strings. Different mechanism, needs its own verification, and per doctrine a single 30-cell will not settle it.\n\n**One harness bug, arm-asymmetric by luck.** `find / -name goyacc*` in prom-eg surfaced `/tmp/goyacc`, `/tmp/goyacc-build/goyacc`, `/tmp/goyaccbuild/goyacc` — built by *other cells* in the REP50 run, not by either arm of this pair (this pair's baseline built `/tmp/goyaccwork/goyacc`, a different path, and the two overlapped 23:01–23:15). eg spent 2 calls running `file` and `md5sum` on foreign binaries before rebuilding from source anyway. Shared `/tmp` across concurrent cells leaks build artifacts between arms and instances. That is precisely the class of thing that fabricates per-instance deltas, and per the doctrine it wants auditing before any of these numbers get quoted.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>187869</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>28</tool_uses><duration_ms>760761</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
{"parentUuid":"1dfc38c0-b04e-4cb0-92e9-0be29c9c2566","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac8e7cc6a691f2030</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WsyNbjjx3TLJv1wjUpJugH</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-suhaan-devenv/0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208/tasks/ac8e7cc6a691f2030.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Investigate ansible and prometheus losses\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>Killed the stray job — I have everything I need.\n\n## Both instances are ADD tasks. That is the structural story.\n\n| | ansible PlayIterator | prometheus 9248 |\n|---|---|---|\n| task | introduce `IteratingStates`/`FailedStates` enums + `__getattr__` back-comnpat shim | make `atan2` a first-class binary operator |\n| MOD or ADD | **ADD** | **ADD** (eg's own repro: `parse error: unexpected identifier \"atan2\"` — it isn't even lexed) |\n| eg / base calls | 115 / 90 (+25) | 109 / 67 (+42) |\n| **pre-edit** | **37 / 17 (+20)** | **54 / 27 (+27)** |\n| post-edit | 78 / 73 (+5) | 55 / 40 (+15) |\n| graph calls | 1 (`search`), pre-edit | 1 (`grep`), pre-edit |\n\n**80% and 64% of the surplus is PRE-EDIT** — the opposite of the corpus-level \"97.7% of loss is post-edit\". These two losers bleed exactly where the graph operates, so it does not get the \"absent at the scene\" defence.\n\nWhy ADD matters mechanically: a code graph indexes *what exists*. On a MOD task rank-1 is the edit site. On an ADD task the best possible hit is a *sibling* of the answer (`POW`/`MOD` for atan2; `ITERATING_*` for `IteratingStates`). The real work is a **completeness** question — every place the new member must be threaded — not a **location** question. The graph answers the question these tasks don't ask.\n\n## Instance 1: prometheus-9248 — the graph was actively worse than grep on move 1\n\n`gq_calls.log`: **`grep atan2|ATAN2`** — one call, no `search`.\n\nSame query both arms, opening move:\n\n- **base** `grep -rn \"atan2\\|ATAN2\" --include=*.go --include=*.y --include=*.test .` → **31 B: `(Bash completed with no output)`**\n- **eg** `entire graph grep \"atan2|ATAN2\"` → **165 B: `web/ui/static/vendor/rickshaw/vendor/d3.v3.js:1 | d3=function(){function n(n){return null!=n&amp;&amp;!isNaN(n)}…(1 matches)`**\n\n`entire graph grep` has no vendor/minified/include filtering, so it manufactured a false positive where plain grep returned a clean zero. Clean-zero is an actionable signal; \"1 hit in minified vendored JS\" is not.\n\n**Consequence, turn by turn.** Baseline's call #2 pivoted to the *error string from the issue text* — `grep -rn \"not allowed for Scalar operations\"` → `promql/engine.go:2089` — pinning `scalarBinop` (a gold edit site) at **call 2**. eg never grepped the error string; it spent calls 3,5,6,7,8 re-confirming atan2's absence (call 7 re-derived the graph's own d3.js hit) and only reached `scalarBinop`/`vectorElemBinop` at **calls 21–22**. **~19 turns of delay, seeded by the tool's opening answer.**\n\nBucketed surplus (+42):\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| codemirror/web-UI excursion | 25 | 5 | **+20** | eg made 8 edits there; base made **0** |\n| env / goyacc toolchain hunt | 18 | 5 | +13 | incl. auditing 3 stale `goyacc` binaries from **other sessions'** `/tmp` |\n| redundant \"does atan2 exist\" | 6 | 3 | +3 | |\n| reads | 4 | 4 | +0 | but eg Read `generated_parser.y` **unbounded (28,762 B)** vs base `lim=270` (9,045 B) |\n| repro harness | 15 | 8 | +7 | eg built it in `/tmp` behind a separate `go.mod` → `too many arguments in call to engine.NewInstantQuery` → recovery turn |\n| dead ends | 2 | 1 | +1 | `cp: cannot stat promql/parser/generated_parser.y` (wrong cwd) |\n\neg's patch touched 5 extra files, all `web/ui/module/codemirror-promql/**`. It discovered `web/ui/node_modules` does not exist and `npm` is netjailed at call ~97 — **after** all 8 edits. **The eg harness already knew this**: `gq_calls.log.probe` contains `stripped: cd web/ui/module/codemirror-promql &amp;&amp; npx mocha src/parser/parser.test.ts`. The tool probed that subtree, found it unverifiable, and threw the fact away.\n\n## Instance 2: ansible PlayIterator — gold at rank 1, still lost 25 turns\n\nPayload was **good**: hit #1 `lib/ansible/executor/play_iterator.py:128-133` (the exact constant block being replaced, `signals=…exact-symbol,graph:callers`), hit #4 `HostState.__init__`, hit #5 `linear.py:82-198 _get_next_task_lockstep`. Two of three gold files in the top five. It did not point anywhere wrong.\n\nAnd it saved nothing: eg then Read `play_iterator.py` **unbounded (28,576 B)** — byte-identical to what baseline read at its call #2 with no tool at all. Both patches touch the **same 4 files**. The +25 turns bought zero extra scope.\n\nWhere they went:\n\n| bucket | eg | base | Δ |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| repro/import wrangling (py3.12 `find_module`→`find_spec` break in ansible's collection loader) | 12 pre-edit + shim rewritten again at call ~84 | 8, post-edit | +6 |\n| pytest acquisition | 3 `pip install` into `.venv` (pytest, pycodestyle/pyflakes, mock) | 0 — used `~/gfyvenv/bin/python`, already had pytest 9.1.1 | +3 |\n| deprecation-convention hunt (read the pylint sanity plugin `test/lib/…/pylint/plugins/deprecated.py` in 2 chunks + revisit) | 6 | 3 (one `grep -n \"def deprecated\" -A 40 lib/ansible/utils/display.py`) | +3 |\n| extra localization reads (`test_linear.py` full, extra grep rounds) | 25 | 12 | +13 |\n| repo edits | 30 | 35 | −5 |\n\neg saw `/home/suhaan_entire_io/gfyvenv/bin/pytest → pytest 9.1.1` at call ~29 and did not use it. That is model variance, not tool behaviour.\n\n## Q5 — is the graph guilty?\n\n**prometheus: guilty, and I can name the mechanism.** Not \"it sent the agent to the wrong file\" — baseline saw the same d3.js hit at its call #3 and ignored it. The mechanism is **the tool consumed the first-search slot and returned a signal too weak to force a pivot**. Baseline's empty result forced the error-string pivot on move 2; eg's noisy one-hit result invited five more confirmation greps. Tool output that is strictly noisier than the no-tool equivalent on the same query is a defect, and it is the tool's *only* contribution to this session.\n\n**ansible: innocent.** Correct payload, rank 1, right files. The +25 turns are environment and harness work no code graph can see. Say it plainly.\n\n## Q6 — can static analysis work on dynamic repos here?\n\nSharply: **the payload worked; the follow-up verbs cannot.**\n\n- Retrieval on Python worked well (ansible payload used real `graph:callers` edges and nailed rank 1).\n- But the dominant operation in *both* tasks is find-all-references over an enumerated constant family. In ansible those references are `self.ITERATING_X`, `PlayIterator.ITERATING_X` and `iterator.ITERATING_X` where `iterator` is an **untyped parameter**. No static index resolves that binding; `impact`/`callers` would fall back to name matching — which is grep, at 1,124 B median vs grep's 732 B. Strictly worse.\n- The task's own solution is `__getattr__`-based dynamic dispatch — invisible to any static index by construction.\n- In prometheus, **6 of eg's 10 edited files** are a yacc grammar, a lezer grammar, and `tokens.js` — formats the graph does not parse at all. The single most important file in the task is invisible to it.\n\nSo for ~48% of this corpus, the honest position is: retrieval is fine, relations are unreliable, and the graph's edge over grep has to come from facts grep cannot compute — not from reference resolution.\n\n## Proposals, ranked by (turns removed)/(bytes added)\n\nAll are payload **fields on existing `search`/`grep` output** — not new verbs (0/66 adoption), not riders, not reranking, not bigger payloads.\n\n| # | capability | turns removed | bytes | ratio | conf |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **P0** | **Vendor/minified/generated exclusion in `graph grep`.** Make a no-real-hits query read as no real hits. | prom 3 | **−134** | ∞ | high |\n| **P1** | **Probe-result surfacing.** eg's VERIFY probe already computes, at index time, which subtrees have a runnable check, then discards it. Emit it as observation, not advice: `web/ui/module/codemirror-promql: probe 'npx mocha …' exit 127 (no node_modules)`. | prom ~8, ans ~3 | ~180 | 1/16 B | **low** |\n| **P2** | **Generated-artifact provenance edge.** Parse the standard `// Code generated by X … DO NOT EDIT.` header, resolve the generator to its module+version from `go.sum`/lockfile: `generated_parser.y.go GENERATED_FROM generated_parser.y BY golang.org/x/tools/cmd/goyacc@v0.1.5`. | prom ~5 | ~120 | 1/24 B | high |\n| **P3** | **Error-literal → emitting-symbol index.** Key on quoted strings in the problem statement, normalise `%q`/`%s`/`{}`. `operator %q not allowed for Scalar operations` → `engine.go:2089 scalarBinop`, `engine.go:2429 vectorElemBinop`. **Already built, shipped OFF** — this cell ran `EG_LITERALS=\"0\"`. | prom ~6 | ~270 (fires only on quoted literals) | 1/45 B | med |\n| **P4** | **Enumerated-family closure.** Seeded on a member of a token/const/map family, return every co-member's file:line. Seeded on `POW`/`MOD` this returns the *entire* prometheus edit set across `.y`, `lex.go`, `engine.go`, `.grammar`, `.terms.ts`, `operators.md`. Answers the completeness question ADD tasks actually ask. | prom ~5, ans ~1 | ~750 | 1/125 B | med |\n\nFairness check on each: none is a sentence of advice, all are computed repo facts a no-tool arm can only obtain by spending the turns. P1 is the one to watch — it must be phrased as a probe result (`exit 127`), never as \"don't edit there\", or it fails the test.\n\n## The bottom line, and two things you should not miss\n\n**Recoverable: prometheus ~27 of 42 surplus turns. Ansible ~6 of 25.** Ansible is essentially unrecoverable by anything in the graph's power — it is an environment loss wearing a tool's name. If the corpus's 25 losers look like ansible more than like prometheus, no graph capability closes them and you are optimising the wrong layer.\n\n**P3 is already built and switched off.** The single capability that would have collapsed prometheus's biggest delay is `EG_LITERALS`, disabled in this cell. Before building anything new, re-run these two instances with it on. Caveat worth stating: memory has literals adjacent to a reverted IDF-weighting regression (45→38%) — but that was a *ranking* change, and this is an additional retrieval channel keyed on quoted strings. Different mechanism, needs its own verification, and per doctrine a single 30-cell will not settle it.\n\n**One harness bug, arm-asymmetric by luck.** `find / -name goyacc*` in prom-eg surfaced `/tmp/goyacc`, `/tmp/goyacc-build/goyacc`, `/tmp/goyaccbuild/goyacc` — built by *other cells* in the REP50 run, not by either arm of this pair (this pair's baseline built `/tmp/goyaccwork/goyacc`, a different path, and the two overlapped 23:01–23:15). eg spent 2 calls running `file` and `md5sum` on foreign binaries before rebuilding from source anyway. Shared `/tmp` across concurrent cells leaks build artifacts between arms and instances. That is precisely the class of thing that fabricates per-instance deltas, and per the doctrine it wants auditing before any of these numbers get quoted.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>187869</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>28</tool_uses><duration_ms>760761</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","commandMode":"task-notification","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:01:42.338Z"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"75c9abbb-50f0-40f8-b99d-7f8b94c5055f","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:01:42.338Z","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-