{"parentUuid":"583ce8d1-95df-4644-aaf2-380f46db805a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqjpSn768GciJw8uNgAn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"output_tokens":8337,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8337,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqjpKFfdYrsX3PLp7HTu","type":"assistant","uuid":"1ef3649f-16d1-4101-ba02-ac00629b2a09","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:16:47.329Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"1ef3649f-16d1-4101-ba02-ac00629b2a09","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqjpSn768GciJw8uNgAn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're right, and that's a design flaw in how I built it. I gave each defect **one** designer and **three** refuters set to \"default refuted.\" When a first idea died there was no second attempt — so it was structurally a kill-machine, not a fix-finder.\n\nIt did produce real value (the 5.2× R-A correction, ADD = 54% of drag, the probe already being on, two genuine bugs), but that's diagnosis, not fixes.\n\nRebuilding it as an iterative loop — propose, get refuted, **revise using the refutation**, repeat — and pointing it at the one thing the data says actually predicts winning:"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"output_tokens":8337,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8337,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqjpKFfdYrsX3PLp7HTu","type":"assistant","uuid":"e657ebbf-1c1d-40d1-92d1-6721ac323283","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:16:50.916Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph","sessionId":"73009f6a-a642-4aaa-8da3-51eb6bcc5b49","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"stateless-napping-unicorn"}
{"parentUuid":"e657ebbf-1c1d-40d1-92d1-6721ac323283","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdqjpSn768GciJw8uNgAn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014a8QRH3uMB465cQQLsUQEA","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'eg-find-surviving-fix',\n  description: 'Iteratively design fixes that survive adversarial refutation, revising each proposal using the reason it was killed',\n  phases: [\n    { title: 'Round1', detail: 'propose against the displacement objective' },\n    { title: 'Round2', detail: 'revise using the refutation and re-test' },\n    { title: 'Round3', detail: 'final revision and verdict' },\n    { title: 'Ship', detail: 'synthesis of survivors' },\n  ],\n}\n\nconst BASE = `\nREAD-ONLY: design and offline validation only. Do NOT modify files, do NOT run a benchmark cell.\nCaveman-ultra compressed output. Numbers and file:line. Use RTK where it saves tokens.\n\nentire-graph: local deterministic code-graph CLI (Go, tree-sitter). Benchmarked vs a no-tool\nbaseline on SWE-bench Multilingual through Claude Code. Metric: tokens.\nSign: POSITIVE = graph arm SAVED tokens.\n\n=== THE OBJECTIVE (this is what you are optimising, not \"tokens\" in the abstract) ===\nMeasured on rep50 (49 gated pairs, Opus): DISPLACEMENT is the discriminator.\n  label a session \"displaced\" if eg's shell-exploration call count < its paired baseline's.\n    displaced & won  = 19/21  (90.5%)\n    not-displaced & won = 6/28 (21.4%)\n    AUC(no-displacement -> loss) = 0.838\n  Sessions that CALL the graph: pooled +6.30%, median +12.61%.\n  Sessions that never call it (16/49): pooled -1.02%, median -24.94%.\n  => CALLING AND DISPLACING IS THE WINNING BEHAVIOUR. The goal is to raise the fraction of\n     sessions that displace, WITHOUT adding payload bytes and WITHOUT an instruction.\n\n=== HEADLINE STATE ===\ntotal +1.35% | median -4.16% | 10% trimmed -7.65% | t=-0.17 | LOO drop lombok-3674 -> -5.18%\ndrag -19.86 pts over 25 instances; lift +21.21 over 24; lombok-3674 alone +6.09 (29% of lift).\n\n=== DRAG DECOMPOSITION (independent gold-patch labeller) ===\ngold-MOD n=36: +13.59% (median +12.67%)\ngold-ADD n=13: -23.19% (median -18.03%)\n**gold-ADD = 8 of 25 drag instances and -10.68 of the -19.86 drag points = 54% OF ALL DRAG.**\nCAUTION: GEM300 INVERTS this (MOD -14.13% n=44, ADD +0.86%), so MOD/ADD is not universal.\n\n=== COST MODEL ===\ncache_read = 94.6-95.5% of billed tokens. A result at turn i is re-charged every later turn:\ncost ~ (bytes/4) x turns-remaining. BUT turn cost is NOT uniform: the first graph search lands at\nassistant turn median 2 of a median 85-turn session, where cache_read is 12,231 tokens vs the\nsession mean of 63,137/turn. Early messages are CHEAP. Late messages are expensive.\nOne default search payload = 10,822 B ~ 54k persist ~ 0.55 of the whole-file Read it replaces.\nBaseline cost by class: exploration 45.1% (1,206 calls x 14k), whole-file Read 30.3% (119 x 98k),\nharness 11.2% (576 x 7k). eg is CONTEXT-NEUTRAL overall (38.5M vs 38.6M).\n\n=== KILLED THIS WEEK — do not re-propose, and do not propose anything that reduces to these ===\n- R-A zero-toll pre-delivery: repriced +0.96% CI[+0.53,+1.38] (was +4.3-6.0%, a 5.2x overcharge\n  from pricing a turn-2 message at session-mean cost). Also aimed wrong: the 16 sessions that\n  never call search ARE the losing cohort, and universal injection charges them ~45k for nothing.\n  n to demonstrate = 19,504 pairs.\n- VERIFY collect-only probe: **EG_VERIFY_PROBE=1 was ALREADY SET in run_REP50.sh:141** — it was\n  live during rep50. Also 21/25 losers received NO VERIFY line at all, so it is a no-op on 84%\n  of the drag. Also flagged as an eg-only build-cache warm-up (fairness).\n- MOD/ADD structural router (\"does the issue name a symbol that resolves in the graph\"): tested\n  offline, does not separate. And `exact-symbol` is ALREADY printed to the agent per hit\n  (search.go:4589 -> cli/search.go:796,799); rank-1 exactness is IDENTICAL on ADD and MOD\n  (0.36 vs 0.36), so any wording keyed on it fires equally on the winners.\n- Post-edit consistency hook: its fair symmetric form's biggest beneficiary is the BASELINE in\n  lombok-3674 (29% of lift) -> eats its own lift source. Cost -2% to -6%.\n- New verb in the prompt's affordance tail: 0/66 adoption (impact 0/49, changed 0/5, --path 0/12).\n- Anything needing an agent-initiated POST-EDIT call: 42/49 sessions make none.\n- Better ranking (gold-at-rank-1 sessions LOST more often); bigger payloads; graph-backed grep\n  (graph output median 1,124 B > median grep 732 B); 12 dead payload-quality metrics; symmetric\n  frugality clamp (helps the baseline more in exactly the instances eg wins).\n\n=== CONFIRMED, UNEXPLOITED, AND CHEAP (these are real and nobody has shipped them) ===\n1. `graph grep` has NO vendor/minified/generated filtering — it shells to `grep -rInE` over the\n   worktree and filters ONLY tests. On prometheus-9248 it returned one hit inside minified\n   vendored d3.v3.js where plain grep returned a CLEAN ZERO. A clean zero is actionable; one\n   noise hit is not. Baseline pivoted on move 2 and found the fix site; eg reached it at call 21.\n   ~19 turns lost. THIS IS THE ONLY MEASURED CASE OF THE GRAPH BEING WORSE THAN NO TOOL.\n   Fixing it REMOVES bytes.\n2. FILE OUTLINE (internal/sem/search_outline.go, 217 LOC, flag cli/search.go:1637) is DEFAULT OFF\n   and absent from printHelp. Its own measurement: 77 targeted greps landed on a file the payload\n   had already named; 35 were immediately followed by a Read of that same file. Cost +1,421 B\n   (~7.1k persist) against ~57k saved. ~8x return.\n3. Complete bodies emitted to rank 5 (search_enclosure.go:41) but act-rates are rank1 62% read /\n   54% edited, rank2 27%/20%, rank3+ 0-11%. Measured: --body-head-ranks 5->2 takes the payload\n   10,822 -> 2,590 B (-76%).\n4. echoPresearchPayload (cli/search.go:286-292) has NO length check: a zero-byte payload yields\n   0 bytes on stdout with exit 0 — the \"announced but never shipped\" failure class.\n5. The presearch echo is QUERY-BLIND and header-less (cli/search.go:134-136): any later search on\n   any new symbol returns the turn-0 payload verbatim with no indication it answered a different\n   question. An absorbing state for the session.\n6. search defaults to --profile fast (9/30 relations) while both agent guides mandate full; under\n   the default TESTS/SIMILAR_TO/DATA_FLOWS/FILE_CHANGES_WITH and all type relations are\n   structurally EMPTY. neighbors/impact default to full; search is the odd one out.\n7. TESTS relation: 10 edges of 36,354 on a Go repo, 0 of 89,587 on a Python repo WITH a pytest\n   suite (name-convention only, types.go:280-297).\n8. AGENTS.md:72 states `edges --to/--from/--relation` does not exist. It does (root.go:576-594),\n   and it is the only route to 13 relation types.\n9. capabilities --json declares 7 heuristic relations; by the code ~27 of 30 are text-pattern\n   derived, INCLUDING CALLS (35% of all edges) which is a regex name-set, not AST call sites.\n10. The in-tree binary is STALE vs source (rejects verify, changed, --path). Rebuild before ship.\n\n=== FAIRNESS (binding) ===\nTool OUTPUT differences legitimate; arm-asymmetric INSTRUCTIONS are not.\nTest: \"would this sentence or change help an arm with NO tool?\" yes -> both arms or neither.\nA published -35% was withdrawn for two extra frugality sentences; removing them moved that cell\nfrom -14.9% to +9.1%. Watch for disguised instructions (\"you do not need to run it yourself\" at\nrun_3arm.sh:754 is instruction-shaped and is ON by default; EG_PREFETCH_NOSUPPRESS=1 removes it).\n\n=== NOISE FLOOR ===\npaired sd 0.494. n to detect +6% = 499 pairs. Same-instance spread median 99.9pt. 93% of losers\nflip sign between cells. NEVER gate on tokens below n=50 — gate on mechanism counts.\n\nPATHS (read-only): tool /Users/suhaan/devenv/entire-graph ; harness\n/Users/suhaan/graphmark-bench/agentic-swebench ; ledger\n/Users/suhaan/devenv/graph-bench-results/TokensNotes.md\nVM data (batch ssh, 30-60s each):\n  P=graph-benchmarks-504800\n  gcloud compute ssh graphbench-c --project=$P --zone=us-east1-b --tunnel-through-iap --quiet --command='...'\n  results/REP50_r1_{eg,baseline}/<arm>/<iid>/{cc_out.json,patch.diff,gq_calls.log,gq_calls.log.payload}\n  transcripts ~/.claude/projects/*/<session_id>.jsonl\n`\n\nconst TRACKS = [\n  { key: 'bytes', brief: `TRACK A — REMOVE BYTES WITHOUT LOSING ANSWERS.\nItems 1,2,3,6 in the CONFIRMED list are byte-level changes with measured act-rates. Design the\ncombined change: grep vendor/minified exclusion + FILE OUTLINE default + body ranks 5->2 +\nthe profile default. Price the NET byte delta and the NET persist delta, and say which of the\n1,206 baseline exploration calls or 119 whole-file Reads specifically stop happening.` },\n  { key: 'displace', brief: `TRACK B — RAISE THE DISPLACEMENT RATE.\n19/21 displaced sessions won; 6/28 non-displaced won; AUC 0.838. 16/49 sessions never call the\ngraph at all and they are the losing cohort. Design a change that makes MORE sessions displace.\nConstraints: no new verb (0/66), no instruction, no post-edit agent call. You may change what\nEXISTING verbs return, what the DEFAULT behaviour is, or how the harness exposes the tool —\nprovided it is output/capability, not advice.` },\n  { key: 'add', brief: `TRACK C — THE ADD-TASK DRAG (54% of all drag, 8 instances, -10.68 pts).\nThe router and the abstention affordance are both refuted (see KILLED). exact-symbol is already\nprinted and does not separate (0.36 vs 0.36). GEM300 inverts the MOD/ADD sign. Given all that:\nis there ANY tool-output change that reduces the ADD penalty, or is the honest answer that the\npenalty is a property of the task and the correct action is to STOP claiming the tool helps on\nADD tasks? Argue it out and commit to one.` },\n]\n\nconst SPEC = {\n  type: 'object', additionalProperties: false,\n  required: ['track', 'proposal', 'mechanism', 'net_bytes', 'expected_low', 'expected_high', 'fair', 'survives_known_kills'],\n  properties: {\n    track: { type: 'string' },\n    proposal: { type: 'string', description: 'Concrete change with file:line' },\n    mechanism: { type: 'string', description: 'Which specific calls stop happening, and why' },\n    net_bytes: { type: 'number', description: 'Net payload byte delta, negative is good' },\n    expected_low: { type: 'number' },\n    expected_high: { type: 'number' },\n    fair: { type: 'boolean' },\n    fairness_argument: { type: 'string' },\n    survives_known_kills: { type: 'string', description: 'Explicitly: why this is not a restatement of any KILLED item' },\n    offline_test: { type: 'string', description: 'The $0 test that would confirm or kill it' },\n  },\n}\n\nconst VERDICT = {\n  type: 'object', additionalProperties: false,\n  required: ['refuted', 'reason'],\n  properties: {\n    refuted: { type: 'boolean' },\n    reason: { type: 'string' },\n    what_would_fix_it: { type: 'string', description: 'If refuted: the specific change that would make it survive, or \"nothing\" if unsalvageable' },\n  },\n}\n\nfunction refute(spec, round) {\n  return parallel([\n    () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nROUND ${round} PROPOSAL:\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n      `REFUTE ON MECHANISM AND COST. eg is context-neutral — no slack. Price every added byte at ` +\n      `(bytes/4) x turns-remaining, and remember early turns are CHEAP (turn 2 = 12,231 cache_read ` +\n      `vs 63,137 session mean). Can you trace the specific calls that stop happening? Twelve ` +\n      `payload-quality levers died by changing content without changing behaviour. ` +\n      `If refuted, you MUST say what specific change would make it survive, or \"nothing\".`,\n      { label: `r${round}-cost:${spec.track}`, phase: `Round${round}`, schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n    () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nROUND ${round} PROPOSAL:\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n      `REFUTE ON FAIRNESS AND WINNER REGRESSION. Apply the no-tool-arm test literally, and look for ` +\n      `DISGUISED instructions. Then: winners win by converging, and lombok-3674 alone is 29% of all ` +\n      `lift where the BASELINE ran javac 70x vs eg 23x. Could this help the baseline more than eg, ` +\n      `or make winners do more? If refuted, say what change would make it survive, or \"nothing\".`,\n      { label: `r${round}-fair:${spec.track}`, phase: `Round${round}`, schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n    () => agent(`${BASE}\\n\\nROUND ${round} PROPOSAL:\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n      `REFUTE ON PRIOR ART AND MEASURABILITY. Open TokensNotes.md and the harness runners and check ` +\n      `whether this is ALREADY SHIPPED or ALREADY ON — EG_VERIFY_PROBE was already set in ` +\n      `run_REP50.sh:141 and that killed a whole proposal last round. Then: is the effect bigger than ` +\n      `the ~100pt same-instance noise floor, and what n demonstrates it? ` +\n      `If refuted, say what change would make it survive, or \"nothing\".`,\n      { label: `r${round}-prior:${spec.track}`, phase: `Round${round}`, schema: VERDICT, effort: 'high' }),\n  ]).then((votes) => {\n    const live = votes.filter(Boolean)\n    const kills = live.filter((v) => v.refuted)\n    return {\n      survives: live.length > 0 && kills.length < 2,\n      kills: kills.length,\n      reasons: kills.map((v) => v.reason),\n      fixes: kills.map((v) => v.what_would_fix_it).filter((x) => x && x !== 'nothing'),\n      unsalvageable: kills.length >= 2 && kills.every((v) => !v.what_would_fix_it || v.what_would_fix_it === 'nothing'),\n    }\n  })\n}\n\n// Three rounds: propose -> refute -> REVISE USING THE REFUTATION -> refute -> revise -> final.\nconst results = await pipeline(\n  TRACKS,\n  async (t) => {\n    let spec = await agent(`${BASE}\\n\\n${t.brief}\\n\\nPropose ONE concrete change. It must survive the ` +\n      `KILLED list — say explicitly why it is not a restatement of any of them.`,\n      { label: `propose:${t.key}`, phase: 'Round1', schema: SPEC, effort: 'high' })\n    if (!spec) return null\n    let history = []\n    for (let round = 1; round <= 3; round++) {\n      const v = await refute(spec, round)\n      history.push({ round, kills: v.kills, reasons: v.reasons })\n      if (v.survives) return { track: t.key, spec, survived_at_round: round, history }\n      if (v.unsalvageable || round === 3) return { track: t.key, spec, survived_at_round: null, history, dead: true }\n      // REVISE using why it died — this is what the previous workflow lacked\n      const revised = await agent(`${BASE}\\n\\n${t.brief}\\n\\nYour previous proposal was REFUTED.\\n\\n` +\n        `PROPOSAL:\\n${JSON.stringify(spec, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n        `WHY IT WAS KILLED:\\n${v.reasons.map((r, i) => `(${i + 1}) ${r}`).join('\\n\\n')}\\n\\n` +\n        `SUGGESTED REPAIRS FROM THE REFUTERS:\\n${v.fixes.join('\\n') || '(none offered)'}\\n\\n` +\n        `Produce a REVISED proposal that specifically answers every refutation above. If the ` +\n        `refutations are correct and the idea is unsalvageable, say so in `proposal` beginning ` +\n        `with \"ABANDON:\" and propose the best remaining alternative on this track instead.`,\n        { label: `revise${round}:${t.key}`, phase: `Round${round + 1}`, schema: SPEC, effort: 'high' })\n      if (!revised) return { track: t.key, spec, survived_at_round: null, history, dead: true }\n      spec = revised\n    }\n    return { track: t.key, spec, survived_at_round: null, history, dead: true }\n  },\n)\n\nconst all = results.filter(Boolean)\nconst survivors = all.filter((r) => r.survived_at_round)\nlog(`${survivors.length}/${all.length} tracks produced a surviving proposal`)\n\nphase('Ship')\n\nconst ship = await agent(\n  `${BASE}\\n\\n` +\n  `Three tracks each ran up to 3 rounds of propose -> adversarial refute -> revise-using-the-refutation.\\n\\n` +\n  `RESULTS:\\n${JSON.stringify(all, null, 1)}\\n\\n` +\n  `Write the shipping decision. For each SURVIVING proposal: exact files and functions with ` +\n  `file:line, the change stated precisely enough to implement, the $0 offline test that must pass ` +\n  `first, honest expected effect WITH an interval, the n required to demonstrate it, and the ` +\n  `fairness argument.\\n\\n` +\n  `For each DEAD track: state plainly that it is dead and why, so nobody retries it.\\n\\n` +\n  `Then answer the user's actual question: does the surviving set reduce the -19.86 pt drag pool, ` +\n  `and by roughly how much? If the honest answer is \"not measurably\", say that. Do not manufacture ` +\n  `a number. Note explicitly that the gold-ADD cohort is 54% of the drag and whether anything here ` +\n  `touches it.`,\n  { label: 'ship-decision', phase: 'Ship', effort: 'high' },\n)\n\nreturn { survivors: survivors.length, tracks: all.map((r) => ({ track: r.track, survived_at_round: r.survived_at_round, dead: !!r.dead })), ship }\n","description":"Iterative fix-finding with revision"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"output_tokens":8337,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8337,"cache_read_input_tokens":843000,"cache_creation_input_tokens":710,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":710},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdqjpKFfdYrsX3PLp7HTu","type":"assistant","uuid":"9f60d97e-6704-449e-bc50-08fa6e094f73","timestamp":"2026-08-08T18:18:13.975Z","effort":"xhigh","session_id":"0d4c4262-2100-4779-8745-e855e0a78208","userType":"ext